      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of science is such as will benefit all classes of people. Some of the greatest discoveries of the age, while they have proved directly beneficial to thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Even some of the best medi- oinal preparations, however much they may do to remove one class of disease, or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it entirely untouched, or if they benefit one class of patients, they may injure another. Now the great need of the age is some scientifically arranged preparation which will cope effectually with the pre- valent diseases of this country, which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, -which will be equally adopted to the needs of the merchant and the workman, the professional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk, the factory hand, the miner, and the roadside labourer. It should be, too, such a pre paration as contains no injurious ingredients, and which may be taken with impunity by the weakly child, or the delicate lady, as well as by the stronger constituted man. Such a discovery would deserve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal Preparations, and would indeed be an Invaluable Boon to Suffering Humanity. Now this much needed boon has been found, has been tried, has been proved, and wherever proved, it has been found satisfactory. We refer to Gwilym' Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, which when once -tried, has been always recommended, and has proved successful when all other medicine has failed to give relief, and we may say further, that it has proved per- manently beneficial, when other preparations at best gave only temporary relief. It is strongly recommended as The Best Remedy of the Age for Indigestion in its different forms, such as Sick Headache, Pains in the Side, Giddiness, Loss of Appetite, also for Nervousness and tNervous Disorders, Sleeplessness, Neuralgia, Low Spirits, and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness, either after an illness, long con- finement to ill-ventilated rooms or any other cause. it strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengthens those parts of the system which have been weakened by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it is a Natural Tonic, and suited to all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledged to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease. If you suspect that your health is beginning to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking i now a course of this excellent Tonic, which s sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 41. 6d. each, but should any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the Proprietors will forward same for the above prices, carriage free. Avoid Imitations. When purchasing,see the name, "Gwilym Evans," on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufac- tufing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES. _ffiarrlngton Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES. _ffiarrlngton Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (presiding), Aldermen Sutton, Francomb and Hutchinson, Councillors Tinnion, Wilkinson and Thornton, and Messrs. T. Birchall, Charles White, J. W. Hayward, S. Wright, Burgess, C. J. Holmes, W. Roberts and G.</p>
        <p>Pilling. - ANOTHER ADJOURNMENT.—The case of Bridget Nevin Winwick road, who was charged with neglecting her children was again mentioned by Mr. Browne on behalf of the N'.S.P.C.C.—Defendant had not been persuaded to go to a home, but she now expressed her willingness to return to Ireland, a brother having agreed to pay the passage money.—A further adjournment for a week was granted. VIOLENT ASSAULT ON A POLICEMAN.—Patrick and ' John M'Loughlin 1.9, James-street, were charged with having been drunk and disorderly,on Sunday, and further with assaulting Police-constable Jones—The officer stated that at abont 1.15 on Sunday morning he was on duty in Bnttermarket-street, when he heard a great noise in James-street. On proceeding there be found the prisoners creating a disturbance. He got hold of Patrick, who commenced to kick him in the stomach. The other prisoner then came out of the house, and kicked him. A crowd of roughs gathered round, and every time he turned over in the struggle some person kicked him. Inspector Taylor who went to P.C. Jones's assistance corroborated. The constable was on the ground when he arrived and was being kicked about like a football." He had to be taken to the Police Station in a cab on Sunday and also had to take a cab to the court that morning.—lnspector Phillips said it was a serious case and had not Inspector Taylor arrived at the momentserious injury would have been done to the constable.—The magistrates fined both the prisoners 20s. and costs or a month's imprisonment. - - - - -</p>
        <p>TuEsDA.Y.—Before Dr. Fox (in the chair), Alderman Hutchinson. Messrs. W. Richardson and J. R. Young. A WOMAN'S DISGRACEFUL BEHAVIOUR.—Margaret Cook, last from Liverpool, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Seotland-road on Monday night. —Sergeant Evans said prisoner was singing and shouting. On being remonstrated with she then went into Horsemarket-street, which is- under repair, and picking up a sett threw it at one of the windows of the Central Hotel. The missile did not strike the glass, but knocked a piece out of the window, sill. She again picked the sett up and was about to throw it a second time when the sergeant prevented her. The prisoner said she was sorry she did not send the sett through the window.—The magistrates sent her to prison for a month. WEDNESDAY.—Before Aldermen Fairclough (in the chair) and Hutchinson, Messrs. Birchen, Reynolds, _</p>
        <p>and F. W. Monks. - ATTRUPTED SUICIDE.—Peter Scully, a well-known character, was charged on remand with attempting to commit suicide.—Superintendent Monaghan said that about six o'clock on Tuesday, the 13th inst., prisoner went home drunk and remained in the house until ten .o'clock. He commenced to quarrel with his mother about some moiaey, and ended by drawing a knife across his throat three times. His mother was frightened and brought P.C. Cooper (36) to the house. The officer found prisoner lying on his back on the floor with the knife by his side. There was a slight scratch on his throat and prisoner was very excited. He was taken to the Police Station, and on being placed in a cell, tore his shirt and tried, to hang himself to the ventilator.— Prisoner now expressed regret, and promised to sign the pledge.—He was discharged. -</p>
        <p>- - FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Hepherd (in the chair), Coun- cillors Thornton and Wilkinson,Messrs. Jolley,Pilling,</p>
        <p>Reynolds and Burgess. ASSAIILT.—Martin Mo'ylan, a farm labourer, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Bnttermarket-street on Friday morning and with assaulting Martha Stokes.—The evidence showed that prisoner was very drunk and used filthy language., Without any provocation be struqk Mrs. Stokes a blow over the left eye.—P.C. Fish was sent for and locked him up.—Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs for drunken- ness and sentenced to fourteen days' imprisonment, without the option of afine, for the assault. ARREARS OF Mars-rmiaNce.—Joseph Bates, last from St. Helens, appeared on a warrant Rharging him with arrears of maintenance to his wife, Amounting to £4 10s.6d.—Prisoner pleaded inability to pay the 13s. per week which the order required, and could not make any, offer as to the arrears. The case was adjourned for a month to enable him to pay l3s. per week, the magis- trates making no order as to the arrears. FAMILY DESERTION.—WIE. Newton, who was arrested at Wigan was charged with running away from the borough, his wife and child chargeable to the Union.—Mr. B. ,Shearer said the wife and child were admitted to the Workhouse on March 27th last year,and had been chargeable to the Union about sixty weeks. Prisoner had previously been before the Court for a similar offence, but the case was withdrawn to give him a chance of improving. He (Mr. Shearer) hoped the magistrates would make an example of the man.— Prisoner was sentenced to six weeks' hard labour. WIFE ASSAULT.—John Doherty, 5, Smith-street, was summoned for assaulting his wife, Mary Doherty, on September 17th.—Complainant said her husband came home drunk, and having struck her, ran her out of the house.—Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months and ordered to pay the costs (75.). UNHAPPY HOME LlFE.—Thomas Flynn, 26, Jockey street, was charged with persistent cruelty to his wife Jane Flynn.—Complainant stated that on Saturday night defendant came home drunk and made a rush at her. She ran into the street and he seized her by the hair. Her shoulder was hurt in the struggle. For the past three years defendant had continually assaulted her.—Prisoner, who has a bad record, alleged that his wife was drunk and fell down, causing the injuries com- plained of.—A separation order with 12s. per week alimony was granted.</p>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions. WED'. r:SD A v.—Before Messrs John White (presiding) and T. Halton. 311 ELLANEOVS.—Frederick Shadwell, Pinner's-brow, Warrington, was fined is. and costs for riding a bicycle without a lamp at Winwick on September 14th.—Arthur Hardy, Dallam, Burtonwood, was fined 10s. for keeping a dog without a licence.—Thomas Kelly had to pay ss. for using obscene language at Burtonwood.—On the evidence of P.S. Bannister, Joseph Houghton, of Burton- wood, was fined 10s, for tossing with copper coins, and James Donnelly and John Doan were fined is. and costs each for a similar offence.</p>
        <p>With reference to the account which recently appeared in the " Figaro " of the meetsng between Lord Kitchener and Colonel Marchand at Fashoda in 1898. Lord Kitchener's militerysecretary states that "the account is so evidently all invention that it is a sufficient con- tradiction in itself."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>(Copyright.) THE lIERB-DOCTOR'S WARNING.</head>
        <p>(Copyright.) THE lIERB-DOCTOR'S WARNING.</p>
        <p>By KATHAAINE S. MACQUOID, Author of "His Herres Desire," "Checkmate "In the Volcanic Eifel," &amp;c., &amp;c.</p>
        <p>It was a sultry day in August. Ralph Orne looked up from his drawing, a study of • two girls in --black-and-white, with a deep sigh.; then he leant back in his chair and laughed at the dis- mayed face of Jeanne Pitou, as she tried to stifle a yawn, which shewed her splendid white teeth, but effectually put the mouth, on which the young painter bad been engaged, out of drawing. "No use, my girl; we wilkive it up for to-day— it is too hot for anything; I wonder you and Mathurine have kept awake so long." Jeanne, a tall, strapping, dark-eyed lass of fifteen, looked ashamed of her drowsiness; but small, fair Mathurine gave him a contented smile, and strained her pale little eyes to their fullest Opening. "I am quite awake, if monsieur pleases to con- tinue the sitting with me." "Chut !" Jeanne slapped her fellow-model's shoulder, and whispered: "Can you not see that monsieur is tired too? He wants to go and refresh himself in the wood."</p>
        <p>Ralph rose, and said "Yes," when Jeanne offered to carry his board and easel back to the hotel; then, with a farewell nod to both girls, he took the way to the river, crossed its rocky stream and reached the edge of the deep, cool chestnut wood. Before he plunged in among the trees he turned to look at the charming village, its grey houses nestling beside the water, which, foaming over the cumbering boulders in its bed, busied itself in feeding the sluices of numerous mills, some of • them perched on the rocks beside the river, shaded by the tall poplar trees which fringed it; here and there a little bridge spanned the rocks and connected one mill with another.</p>
        <p>It was more like a picture than a real village, and the costume of the people kept up this unreal character. The men's baggy breeches fastened at the knee, their long-waisted waistcoats, and low- crowned, wide-brimmed hats told at once that the scene was Breton; while the winged caps and large finely-goffered collars of the girls specialised the locality as a part of Finistere. rllph Orne had been for several months painting in the village : he wished to go North now that the weather had become so warm, but was so fascinated by his surroundings that he could not drag himself away. The Englishmen he had been intimate with had left the little place; just a few others, French and German, lingered. Ralph was the best-looking and the most popular among the artists; and very soon after his arrival he had been able to have his choice among the village models—. men and women, lads and girls, accustomed to sit to the p inters who every year visited this out-of- the-way corner of Brittany. He wandered along under the trees, letting his thoughts and fancies wander too as they pleased; he was not earnest enough about his pictures to think deeply concerning them. He called himself an artist, and he liked to paint, but he took Art as easily as he took most other things, and as he did not need to live by it, he thought it was useless to turn a pleasure into ,the grind which "some artists made of their lives." His reverie was sud- denly disturbed; he stumbled against a granite boulder, so moss-grown that, in the semi-gloom of the wood, he came upon it unawares. A tall, slender figure rose up from the farther side of the stone.</p>
        <p>"Pardon me, M. Ralph," a, soft voice said; "monsieur did not say he wanted me this after- noon, so I came out here."_ _ .</p>
        <p>She looked up at him with a glow of delight in her pale face and deep-blue eyes. She was not so pretty as Mathurine was, but she looked more refined; in spite of the clumsy black stuff gown she wore, her figure was graceful; only a very narrow border of golden' hair shewed under the fiat muslin front of her winged cap. Ralph Orne looked grave while the girl spoke. "But, A.nnik, I know that your mother grudges the time you spare to me when you sit. Ought you r o to work for her this.afternoon ? " _ _ .</p>
        <p>The glow faded from Annik's eyes; they became sad and downcast.</p>
        <p>"M. Ralph knows I do not like indoor work; if my mother would only let me sew out of doors! But she will not; she says I should soil and roughen the silk I embroider with, if I sewed in the open air." , He wished for his sketch-book, her attitude was so unconsciously charming as she leant against the mossy stone. His silence surprised her; she looked up, and blushed with pleasure at finding his eyes fixed on her.</p>
        <p>"Then you like sitting, although y3u have not been trained for it as Jeanne and Mathurine have ? "</p>
        <p>"I have only sat to you," elle said softly; "I do not know if it would give me pleasure to sit to a stranger." There was worship in %et eyes as she looked at him; Ralph had become accustomed to the admira- tion of these simple village girls, mere children be considered them, and he took it as his right. "You know M. Gramont, Annik ? Well, he has asked me whether lOU will Sit to him when I go away ? "</p>
        <p>Annik grew white; she pressed her hand firmly on the stone.</p>
        <p>"Does monsieur mean that he is going away? Then with a smile: "But that is not for long, is it? Monsieur goes only for awhile—he will return ? " Ralph had meant to say that his final departure was decided; but she looked tearful; he thought be would not speak of it till a day or so before- hand. _ _</p>
        <p>"Yes, yes," he answered cheerfully; "I am only now going to Quiroper ; perhaps .on to Mont St. Michel. I shall not be away a week, so if your mother will let you sit to M. Gramont, you can do so to-morrow, as I shall not be here to want you for my picture. Good-bye, my little one; au revoir." He nodded and kissed his hand to her. "I am going down to the sea." "Poor little girl ! " He walked away, singing a little French song. "This place is too rough for her, that is why she likes to sit to me; she escapes some ,of the coarse daily drudgery of life. I believe a Breton woman has a harder lot than the peasants of most countries; she has to work all day like a beast of burden, and her .husband, or her father, as the case may be, never gives her so nuch as a word of thanks. Perhaps the Norman women work as hard, but they are kindly treated; they are their husbands' companions. Poor little Annik is too gentle and sensitive to be the married slave some of her fellows are. By the way, I must caution Gramont to be gentle with her." The handsome, fair-faced young Englishman strode along in his grey knickerbocker suit, with the happy belief that, as an Englishman, he was impefior to anyone in the village.</p>
        <p>The girl sighed and looked after him till he dis- appeared among the broad chesnut leafage; then she sat down on the stone, and hid her face in her hands.</p>
        <p>I A sharp tap on the shoulder waked her from a I long day-dream. Looking up, she saw the eyes of a Itwell-known face glowering . at her from under a ragged blue-green shawl, weather-dyed till its hue would have delighted a painter. She—for it was an old woman—had a bunch of green herbs in one lean, dark hand; the other held the hazel stick with which she had struck the girl's shoulder. Annik rose and nodded; she longed to cross her, self, but feared to give offence, and thereby• to draw on herself the ill-favour of Barbe Daoulas. The old woman called herself a herb-doctor, but she had the reputation of working witch-spells on the cattle, and sometimes on persons she disliked. "Good-day to you, Barbe. The girl tried to smile, but the smile was stifled as she met a cruel sneer in the old woman's bright dark eyes. "Is all well with you ? " "The wise speak not of themselves; they do but warn the foolish," she said solemnly. "I have a message for thee, Annik Ploua ; I bid thee heed it." "A message!" Annik's heart throbbed with fear, as she stared hard at the dark, shrivelled face. "Who can send me a message through you?" "Creatures of earth are earthy, unless wings are vouchsafed them; then they soar, and compel secrets to reveal themselves. The message I bear you is one of life or death, according to the way in which you hearken to its counsel." Her dark eyes seemed to gleam as her tall., slender figure bent over the girl, who was again sitting on the mossy stone. Annik shivered. Years ago she had heard her father say, under his breath, that Barbe Daoulas bad dealings with the Evil One, and the girl had never lost the impression that there was something</p>
        <p>uncanny about the old herb-woman. one reit so uneasy at being alone with her in the 61oomy wood that she could hardly keep from crossing herself. Then a defiant mood seized her: she said coolly: "I cannot tell what I may do till I hear the message." Her seeming indifference angered Barbe ; the skinny fingers clenched her stick more tightly, and she struck the ground with it to give vent to her violent temper. "Before I speak the warning, I see it will not avail; as well pour water on the sea-sand as speak wis.iom in the ear of the foolish. What dost thou here, Annik Ploua ? " Her voice rose in shrill denunciation. "Thy mother slaves at home, and groans for help, while thou, like an immodest maiden, waitest in the wood to waylay the stranger." "That is not true ! " Annik started up with defiance in her face.</p>
        <p>The old crone laughed scornfully, and pointed the stick at her.</p>
        <p>"Who, then, was the tall man that passed but now out of sight among the broad chestnut fans? Fool that thou art to fancy the Englishman wastes a thought on thee It shamed me but now to note the looks thou hest cast at him. Had he cared the top joint of his little finger for thee, as man cares for woman, thou wouldest have left thy innocence in this wood—so little dost thou heed it. Listen, Annik Ploua!" She raised the hazel wand in warning. "This is my message, earth- worm, that thinkest thyself a soaring bird. Be humble, if thou wouldest escape a fall; work at thy appointed labour, and keep within, lest thy brain, swollen with vain conceits, bring thee to destruction. Ere the leaves fall from the chestnut trees, the Englishman will return to his dismal country, and will forget thy existence. Pride hurries ever to the precipice of destruction." The herb-woman turned away, and disappearef among the trees.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 24, 1904.</p>
        <p>the sure-footed ease that always excited her aciiiiira- tion. He had come to look for her, sh felt sure of it, and as soon as he neared the wood he cs,iled out:</p>
        <p>"A.nnik 1 n She came a little forward, and Ralph joined her, raising his hat as he did so. _ _</p>
        <p>"I thought I should find you "—he smiled at her. "I have brought you a farewell token to remember me by, to show that I have appre- ciated your kindness to me." He drew a little parcel from his pocket, and taking off its paper wrappings, displayed a lovely little mother-of-pearl itui; opening it by a spring, he showed her that it was lined with pale blue velvet, and held a gilt thimble, a dainty needle- case, and scissors. Annik looked at it sadly; she did not take it from his hand. She looked up at him, and the passion he saw in her eyes startled him with a foreboding of the truth. "Is it true that you are going back to England ? " "Yes," he answered; "to-morrow or next day. But, Annik, do you not like my little gift? I thought it would please you." "It is like you to give me anything so beautiful; but, M. Ralph, I could not have you think I need anything to remind me of you. How can you bear to leave us, when we so dearly love you? " "It is very kind of you to say so; I shall not easily forget the kindness that has been shewn me; but, dear child, this is not my home. If you were in my place, much as you loved your friends, you would not give up your home to stay with them."</p>
        <p>She had not really believed in his departure; at these words the flood of her despair carried away with it all her power of self-control; it seemed to her blind passion that a question lurked in his words; if she failed to answer it, he would never know how she had loved him.</p>
        <p>"I would give up everything "—her eyes were full of passionate love—"everything but him I loved; I would follow him wherever he went; I would be his slave, his servant, his model—what it pleased him to make me—so long as he took me with him; he should •never repent his love for me."</p>
        <p>Ralph's eyes drooped under her passionate gaze; he felt truly perplexed. Annik was a dear little girl, but he was not conscious that he had ever made love to her ; possibly he had shewn his admiration. He tried hard to assure himself she did not mean all she said; it was only a Breton, poetic mode of expressing the sorrow she felt abotit his departure. " "Come, come "—he smiled cheerfully at her— "let us go home, and show your mother my gift; I know she will admire it, and I shall be rewarded for the pains I took in choosing it for you, dear little girl; come, now." He was determined to avoid a scene, as he called it, and he went on till he stood under the poplars beside the river. Then he looked over his shoulder to where Annik still stood among the chestnut trees.</p>
        <p>The girl was no longer alone. He recognised the herb-doctor in the tall figure that stood facing her; the old woman shook her fist at her, but Annik broke away, and hurried after Mr. Orne. He heard a peal of most unearthly laughter.</p>
        <p>Three years later found Ralph a poor man. He had turned seriously to painting as a means of livelihood; his Breton drawings had met with so much success that when spring came again he resolved to revisit the charming village by the river. . _</p>
        <p>At his last lea.vetaking, Annik's composure had reassured him. He had forgotten his little scene with her; but he determined that this time he would draw only from the practised models of the little place: he would make no special friend. He had grown tired of the journey from Quimperle; bidding his driver,take his luggage on to the hotel, he decided to walk the last few miles. He sat down to rest at the cross-roads. As he rose to resume his walk he saw a tall, bent figure on the road before him. He soon. over- took her, and flung her some money. "I may as well begin with a bless* as with a curse he told himself, for he knew how ready the Breton beggar is to anathematise those who pass by with- out an alms. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The figure stopped and picked up the, money. As she flung it back at him, he recognised the scowling face of Barbe Daoulas. "Bird of prey 1 " she said in her harsh guttural, "I have watched for thee, but thou hast loitered by the way; now thou art too late to see the ruin thou hast made. Before the snows melted Annik went to her rest; the daisies star the sod above her; but the worm feasts merrily below." She laughed at the horror in his face, and pointed to the churchyard. "Go, Englishman, and see your work I Go I see where Annik lies with her broken heart ! "</p>
        <p>[THE END. ]</p>
        <p>• • •„•,•</p>
