      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>A week had gone by. Annik had not sat -to M. Gramont in Ralph's absence; she had excused herself by saying her mother needed her. She had worked unusually hard at home. At the week's end her mother said in her guttural Breton: "Thy help has given me a holiday, my sweet one. May thy work be blessed to thee, beloved! Thou mayest sit long hours to the Englishman when he returns; I give thee full leave." Annik put her arms round 'her pale-faced mother, and lovingly kissed her. "Thou shalt have all the money he pays," she said; "I would not take a sou from him except to give to thee." Her mother looked grave. "Nay, that would be proud and wrong, my child; it would be to forget thy station and his. Thou art a peasant's daughter, and he may be a lord in his own country." • Annik turned away to hide her burning cheeks; it seemed as if everyone was in league to open her eyes to the truth of her feelings for Ralph Orne. Barba Daoulas' warning had revealed to her what had ailed her all these weeks. Even without the old woman's plain speaking, the misery of this week's separation from the Englishman must have taught her that these delicious hopes, these sicken- ing doubts and fears, were symptoms of love, that strange disease which she had read about and heard told in songs and ballads, but of which she had not seen an example in her native village, where girls married the husbands their parents found for them, or more frequently stayed single and went away to service in far-off towns. Cer- tainly Annik knew that there had not been any girl who had given her love unsought to a man who had not returned it.</p>
        <p>It was the day of Ralph Orne's expected return; she would not know of his arrival, the hotel being in the market-place, some little way from the river, near which her father's house nestled among rocks and poplar trees. Her day's work was done, and she sat spinning just within the open door, so feverish with impatience that already her thread had thrice snapped, and she had been forced to stop and lay it together. A sudden thought, like a flash, darted across her musings. Was it certain that the Englishman did not return her love? The herb-doctor had said so, but everyone knew that Barbe Daoulas loved to make others miserable; her mother had cautioned her not to forget the difference between herself and the Englishman, but then Ralph Orne and Annik talked in French, and her mother only spoke and understood Breton. Annik was sure she had not ventured to put herself on an eq-uality with M. Ralph, although he was so kind and sociable. She had heard Jeanne say: "Englishmen are cold and proud " ; it might be that he did care for her, and yet kept his feelings hidden. She started; there was a sound of voices by the river, and she recognised one of them. "Yes," it said, "I am going home almost directly; I have a letter which calls me back to England. lam sorry to leave, as you will guess, when I have shewn you how charming the village and its surroundings are; many of the inhabitants, too, are very interesting to me." _ _</p>
        <p>Annik sal still; she- longed to shew herself. Something whispered: "If you go and stand on the threshold, he will come and talk to you; he will perhaps say he has missed you." If Ralph had been alone she would have gone to him, but Orne's words had shewn her that he had a stranger with him, and this made her feel shy.</p>
        <p>he went to bed earlier than usual, but she could not sleep. Years ago her mother had given her a book of Breton lays and ballads, which had helped to increase the natural romance of the girl's nature. To-night, while she lay tossing in a fever of unrest, she said to herself that the girls in those ballads need not have died broken-hearted if they had told their lovers of the love that filled their hearts. It was true that in some cases, cruel parents had come between; there was Genevieve of Bustefan, whose true love had been forced to become a priest; tbat was indeed a tragedy. Annik told herself it was best to be simple; if a girl were shy and hid her feelings, who could say whether a man, especially an Englishman, might not do the same?</p>
        <p>Surely, surely, at the moment of parting his heart would speak for itself. The word "parting" over- whelmed her with anguish; she believed that no girl had ever so suffered, unless, indeed, Genevieve herself. Annik hid her eyes on her pillow; at last she sobbed herself to sleep.</p>
        <p>It was afternoon of the next day before Annik saw the Englishman. She waited at home, but he did not come; at last .she found an excuse for going into the village. Ralph was standing near the market-place, in the midst of a group of the models he had drawn from during his stay in the village. Everyone seemed to be talking and laughing, and as Annik joined them she saw that each one was shewing to his or her neighbour the gifts which the English- man had brought from Mont St. Michel. Ralph saw her, but he did not offer her a gift. "Good-day, Annik," he called out. "I have come back, you see." Before the girl could answer, Jeanne put her arm round her. "He has come back, Annik, but only for a day; he is off to-morrow for England" —then she whispered—"that foul little island, full of fogs and mud; had he not better stay here, where we all love him, and where there is the sunshine he loves ? "</p>
        <p>Annik saw that Mathurine and her brother Yves were watching her, and she tried to look indifferent.</p>
        <p>"Messieurs the artists always go away when their work is done."</p>
        <p>She turned away, deeply wounded; M. Ralph had spoken as if she were the same to him as the others were, instead of in the kind, gentle manner which she had imagined specialised her as his one chosen friend among the village girls. She felt reckless, and hurried along till she reached the river bank. She began to cross the etre* 7-, springing from one huge grey stone to another; nor did she pause till she was under the deep green shade of the chestnut trees.</p>
        <p>When she came to the prostrate boulder, she paused and looked around her; she was going to take her usual seat on its mossy surface, when she heard a movement in the wood. Was it Barbs? She hurried on to escape a meeting with the old woman. A scornful laugh sounded behind her. At this she turned and made straight for the river bank; telling herself that if she followed on in the track which led deeper into the wood she should put the Englishman' should he come in search of her, in the way of that terrible old raven.</p>
        <p>She soon carne in sight of the sparkling, dashing river, and drew back under the fringe of the wood. She had not long to wait. Presently she saw between the drooping leaves the Englishman strolling in his leisurely way to the water's edge. "Holy Virgin why comes he not more quickly F" the ardent girl said to herself. Than ha anrany across from rock to rock. with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A WIDNES PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Thursday, Joseph Bright was charged with assaulting Mr. W. I-1. Thornton, licensee of the Queen's Arms' and also with refusing to quit the same premises on April 26. Acting-superintendent Pound stated that prisoner had been arrested on a warrant,and would like an adjournment for prosecutor to be repre- sented. Prisoner wanted the case to be dealt with at once. A remand to the cells was granted until Monday _ _ SUDDEN DEATH; OF AN HOTEL MANAGER. _</p>
        <p>Much sympathy has been evoked in Widnes by the rather sudden demise of Mr. Joseph AYManus, the highly-esteemed manager of Mr. Thomas Law's Grosvenor Hotel. About a week ago an attack of dysentery compelled him to take to bed,and on Tuesday hiscondition became worse, with heart trouble, and he passed away at three o'clock. Deceased was a well- known Catholic, and his many acts of charity in a quiet way endeared him to all. 1,4 FIRE A.T RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>About six o'clock on Sunday morning a street sweeper named Samuel Ashley discovered the shop of Mr. Thomas Bold, Bridge-street, Runcorn, to be on fire. There was some difficulty in arousing Mr. Bold and his family,but they were eventually taken out at the rear of the premises, together witk some furniture. Owing to a misunderstanding there was some delay in calling the fire brigade, and the fire obtained a firm hold. The flames reached across Bridge-street, and the shops on the oppositeside were scorched. Mr. Bold's shop was completely gutted, and the contents destroyed. The damage is about £250, and is covered by insurance. PRISONER KNEW THE LAW. - On Wednesday, at Runcorn Police Court, before Messrs. J. Ockleston and E. liaison, Thomas Finn of Chester, with twenty-one previous convictions, was charged with begging in Egerton-street on the previous night.---Evidence was given by Police-Constable Hulse and Mr. H. Corrin,a local journalist, who proved that the prisoner had been very insulting and abusive. The prisoner was sentenced to six weeks' hard labour.—The Prisoner: You cannot give me six weeks; you can only give me a month.—Reference to the Statute Book proved the prisoner to be correct.—He was now arrainged on a further charge of being drunk and disorderly, and he was sentenced to a month on each charge.—Mr. Ockleston : If you had kept quiet you would have saved a fortnight. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WIDNES •BUTCHBR'S FAILURL</head>
        <p>WIDNES •BUTCHBR'S FAILURL</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Bankruptcy Court on Wednesday Mich Eel Wendt, who had carried on business at 85, Widnes-road, Widnes, as a butcher came up for his ex- amination in bankruptcy. The statement of affairs showed liabilities of £B7 and assets of £9.—The bank- rupt, who was examined by the Assistant Official Receiver, ascribed his failure to bad trade, keen com- petition, and bad weather. Prior to 1902 he had been a chemical labourer, and in the year named started the butcher's business in partnership with a Mr. W. B. Davies, but the partnership was dissolved the same year. The bankrupt had kept no books and no account of his receipts and payments.—A cash account was ordered, and the examination was adjourned to the 27th October. DRUNKEN BRAWL AT WIDNIS.—WOMAN STABBED IN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TiE EYE.</head>
        <p>TiE EYE.</p>
        <p>Martha Duffy, 14, Barn-street, was remanded at Widnes, on Tuesday, onacharge of unlawfully wounding Elizabeth Bushell. of 18, Water-street, by stabbing her in the right aye, it is alleged, with a knife. It appears both had been spreeing together all the week-end, and on Monday, about 3 p.m., their jubilation ended in a quarrel, in which Duffy drew a knife from her breast and struck Bushell in the eye with it Information was given to the police, but it was not until eight o'clock at night that Police-constable David- son effected her arrest. Sbe had been in hiding in ash- pits and closets. The movements of the police were handicapped in their search by crowds of children following them about. Vraln being charged prisoner said she did it with a hairpin.—Julia M'Cormick, 15, Water-street, said she saw. prisoner stab Mrs. Bushell with a knife.—Acting-Supermtendent Pound, in asking for the remand, said prosecutrix would not be well enough to attend until a week had elapsed. it was feared that she might lose the sight of the eye.</p>
        <p>Captain J. B. Bowri.ng, of the Indian Army, was murdered at Sarwekai, in the North-West Provinces, while asleep, by a Sepey of the Southern Wazairi Levies. The murderer, who had a rifle and eighty pounds of =munition fired recklessly on all comers for several hours. until he was. Nyounded and finally secured. Captain Bowring was political agent at Warn, in the North-West Provinces.</p>
      </div>
