        <p>4111111111111.0. A mistake through plunging one's hands into the wash-tub at the wrong moment, resulting in severe scalds; a slip on the stairs by which the skin is bruised or the ankle sprained; a nasty fall against the table or wall raising a discoloured swelling on the forehead; the upsetting of kettle or saucepan--these are but a few of the painful mishaps common to all domestic life. Zam-Buk possesses a threefold action when applied to any smarting injury sustained in workshop or home. If subdues pain and inflammation; and being a powerful antiseptic it also thoroughly destroys all dangerous microbes that might otherwise give rise to festering or Suppuration; and thirdly, it heals—and heals perfectly —because of the richness and strength of the medicinal herbs absorbed into the damaged issues directly Zam- Buk is applied. Bruises occurring to a child require more careful attention than in adults. Keep the circulation equable, the head cool, and the feet warm. Bathe the injury with lukewarm water, then, when all visible dirt has been removed, anoint gently with Zam-Buk, and if the bruise is very severe apply a bandage on which more Zam-Buk has been spread. Bathe scalds and burns in cold or tepid water,and remove all dead skin; spread j , quantity of Zam-Buk on a clean piece of lint and bandage up with a view to excluding all air as soon after the accident as possible. Avoid the use of new linen dressed at least once a day ; oftener if desired.</p>
        <p>I NO NOME SHOULD BE WITHOUT A BOX OF ZA M=BUK.</p>
        <p>Mr. John William Hall, a well-known tradesman, was driving his motor car along the Penistone-road, Sheffield, on Wednesday evening, his wife accompanying him. In endeavouring to avoid a heavy four-wheeled vehicle, the motor skidded on the tram fines, and with- out a moment's warning overturned. Mr. and Mrs. Hall were thrown out of the car which was travelling at a fair speed. Both were taken to the Royal Infirmary. Mr. Hall had received severe injuries to the head, and hejwas detained, but his wife was allowed to leave. The motor car was not as badly damaged as were its ejected occupants.</p>
        <p>INSANITARY PROPERTY AT WIDNES. At Widnes, on Thursday, Mr. Craik, deputy to' clerk, applied for a closing order for fourteen houses Walmsley-street, being unfit for habitation, and also respect of their dangerous state. Mr. Craik stated ti upon the report of Dr. M‘Lennan, medical officer health, the matter was laid before the Health Co mittee, and it was decided to proceed against the owr for an order of closure. The evidence of Dr. Stew: and Mr. Low, nuisance inspector, testified to the dela] dated and unhealthy condition of the property. I condition of the whole of the houses was a menace the health of the neighbourhood. The representatio made to the owner had been ignored.—Mr. Math estate agent, Lichfield, Staffordshire, said he had ignor the town clerk's instructions because he thought would not be violating any Of the clauses as the hous were all empty, and had been for seven years. T property was in good order some time ago, but late it had been badly abused by the public. The windoi had been broken, doors knocked in, and bricks Pull' down. He did not think the property had been proper protected..—The Bench granted a-closing order, and tl payment of the conviction costs.</p>
        <p>An apple tree is at the present time to be seen Howden in Mr. H. Harrison's garden, on Selby-road, full bloom—an occurrence which is but rare in tl annals of horticulture.</p>
