        <p>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME.</p>
        <p>BY I'M I CO Xe. M.NT X •</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGIIT.]</p>
        <p>IV.</p>
        <p>SlDE.—continued. The Nap of the Cloth and Its</p>
        <p>Effect on Side.</p>
        <p>The merest novice at billiards knows that slcl4 on a ball alters the angle of reflection so the ball rebounds from a cushion, but the ordinary player has-only a very hazy idea, of the important part the nap of the cloth plays in all strokes played with side. Nevertheless, the nap of the cloth Ja absolutely essential to high class bil- liards, and without it a very great deal of the science of the game as it now stands would never have existed. As is generally known, the nap runs from the baulk end to the spot end of the table. Very often, however, when a cloth becomes somewhat worn, the spot end is made the baulk end, and the nap then runs the wrong way. Worse still, a cloth is frequently turned, with the result that there is then no nap on the playing surface. With the nap running the wrong way, the game loses a great deal, but when there is no nap at all, really high class play becomes impossible. In fact, no really good player who,thoroughly understands and makes use of the...working of side on the bed of the table would care to play on a table with a nap either non-existent or running the wrong way.</p>
      </div>
