      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LIVERPOOL PROFESSOR KILLED</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL PROFESSOR KILLED</p>
        <p>IN WALES.</p>
        <p>Fatal Mountaineering.</p>
        <p>BRILLIANT CAREER CUT SHORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ronald William Henry Turnbull Hudson, Fellow of St. John's College, Cambridge, Doctor of Science of London, and lecturer on mathematics at the Liverpool University, met with a tragic death on Tuesday. It appears Mr. Hudson was spending his holidays in North Wales with a friend, Mr. J.F. Cameron, of Caius College, and reached Penygwryd Hotel, near Bethesda, on Monday. On Tuesday the two young men set forth on a climbing expedition in the vicinity of Devil's Kitchen. It seems that Mr. Hudson slipped and fell headlong down the rocks to a considerable distance below. His death must have been instantaneous,for his distracted friend, accompanied by a search party, found the dead body dreadfully mangled near Lake Idwal. STORY TOLD AT THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The story of the fatality is plain and simple up to a. certain point. It was told at the inquest, held on Wed- nesday at Bethesda by Mr. J. Pentir Williams, coroner for North Carnarvonshire.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Forbes Cameron,of Caius College,Cambridge who was runner up. to the deceased when the latter six years ago was Senior Wrangler, said. he had known deceased for the last, nine years. Deceased was 28 years of age. They came to Wales last Monday, and put up at the Penygwryd Hotel. About 9.30 on Tuesday morning they left the Penygwryd Hotel and climbed the Glydyr from that side, coming down on the north side to the bottom of the Devil's Kitchen. They climbed the first part of the Kitchen together, till they came to a difficulty. There he (Cameron) stopped, but deceased went further. This was about one o'clock midday. Hudson dis- appeared round the difficulty,and Mr. Cameron lost sight of him. He reappeared higher up a quarter of an hour later, on the left wall of the Kitchen. After that Hud- son disappeared, .as it transpired for ever in life, hem his friend's sight. Another quarter of an hour passed in silence, when Mr. Cameron heardanoise, which made him look round quickly. He was just in time to see a huge mass of rock come down with a great crash at the foot of that portion of the chasm where he had last seen his friend. Imme- diately after, almost simultaneously, he saw a smaller body fall down from the heights. That might have been a smaller rock or the body of his friend. He could not say. He shouted his friend's name several times but got no answer, and then sought help. His first help was from a boatman whom he found on the shore of the gloomy lake Idwal, at the foot of the Devil's Kitchen. They went up the hazardous ascent as far as theycould, but could not see the body. Then they climbed up to the top by another route and looked into the dizzy depths, but the boatman thought he could see the body. Cameron could see nothing. They then descended, and at Lake Ogwen got a rescue party, and returned to the Devil's Kitchen, and by means of a ladder two of the men got, over the difficulty and found the body. Replying to the Coroner, Mr. Cameron said deceased had done a good deal of climbing before' chiefly in the Lake District, and was very sure with his hands and his feet. He was very fond of climbing. Did you consider the place dangerous since you did not go up ? asked the Coroner.—l stopped at the rock. I could not go up, replied Cameron. "I am not a climber," he continued, and my friend knew that, and before he went further he knew I was not .coming." Did you consider the place where he went was danger- ous ?asked the Coroner.—The last ascent of the Kitchen is practically impossible, Cameron replied.' don't know how far up my friend went. Had he no rope ?—I think it is possible he fell through the rock giving way under his feet. I don't think it was the result of any error of judgment on his part, or of his strength giving way. What point he fell from I am unable to say. Replying to a juror, witness said they had a rope which would have been used had he accompanied deceased. He did not know whether. Hudson attempted the last ascent, but knew for certain that he did not intend to do so when he left him.</p>
        <p>1 John Roberts, 28 years of age, a fencer in Lord Penrhyn's employ, told how he recovered the body. A farmer's daughter came racing up to him on her bicycle and told him of the accident. With several others,whose names he mentioned, he went to the spot. He took a ladder with him for the purpose of carrying the body if necessary. He carried the ladder two miles. He found it very useful. He climbed up the chimney as far as he could, taking the ladder with him. He too,like Cameron was faced with an impassable "difficulty." He got over it by means of the ladder,which he laid slantwise across the chasm. He crawledacross and then under the boulder the further slippery face of which he then crept, and found himself at the foot of a tremendous slab of rock which ran slanting upwards from the rocky level on which he stood to the foot of another inward and upward running slope, far above which he could see the jagged top of the Chimney. Peering about in the gloom he found poor Hudson's body. It was resting on its knees, but the face was buried in a pool of water at the foot of the giant slab of sloping rock. Hudson was quite dead. The pool of water seemed to be a pool of I blood. Hudson's head on the left temple was crushed , like an eggshell, his right arm was I fractured at the elbow, and had a compound fracture it the wrist ; the left leg was broken. He felt deceased's hands and ears; they were quite cold. But as he raised deceased by the waist Roberts felt the body was still slightly warm. That was about five o'clock. He called another man to come to his assistance, and Robert I Thomas, a Bangor rate collector, who chanced to be ' about, made the perilous ascent, and by means of a rope ' they lowered the body down to the lower levels and finally carried it reverently on the ladder to the lake, across which it was then ferried, and so to Glan Ogwen I Church. Roberts remarked that deceased's jacket had j two large cuts at the back, as if caused by the deceased sliding down the face of the rough rock on his back. He found nothing to indicate whence the deceased had , fallen. He himself, though well acquainted with the mountain, could not have got to where he found deceased but for the ladder. I The jury of which Mr. Hughes Tyamser, was foreman, ' returned a verdict of "Accidental death." I Mr. Griffith Roberts (a juryman), in a Welsh speech, , hoped the press would take notice of what had happened : and call attention to the very dangbrous character of ' the scene of the present fatality. People who visited that district seemed to choose the most dangerous places to go to, and that without guides, of whom there , were plenty available. I The jury passed a vote of sympathywith Mr. Hudson's ' father, who was present, and with his friend Mr. I Cameron.</p>
        <p>lOn Wednesday afternoon, at Deadman's Crossing, on ' the London, Chatham, and Dover Railway at Strood, Gertrude Perry, aged 16,wh0 had just taken her father's dinner to a cement factory near, by nas knocked down and killed. Several accidents have occurred at this spot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>DLiGUA.II I/.</head>
        <p>DLiGUA.II I/.</p>
        <p>lA. half ball stroke played with the nap of the ekrth. The continuous litioshows tbe path of the cue ball after a plain hail stroke, and theAinter- fleeted lines show how side_affects the cueMbeThe natl. 'The intersected -circle shows the cue ball at the moment of contact.</p>
        <p>The action of siderworking with the nap of the cloth is shown in diagram /1. The stroke is the half ball one, and the.micklle linekshows the path of the cue ball's divergence from the line of aim when the stroke is played at recaps' pace, and without using any side. When running side (right hand side in the diagram) is used the angle of deflection is increased, as shown by the thick interdecled line, and when check side (left hand side in the diagram) is used the angle of deflection is diminished more or less as shown by the thin intersected _line, the amount of increase or diminution of the angle of deflec- tion depending upon the quantity of side im- parted to the cue ball. The-action of side working with the nap of the cloth is only, appreciable when a stroke is played at slow or medium pace. When a stroke is played at a fast pace, the nap of the cloth has little °nolo effect on the course of the cue ball. It wilLfherefore be seen that side on the cue ball does not cause that ball to be thrown-off an object ball at a wider or narrower angle but that the nap of the cloth affects the path of the cue ball after contact.</p>
        <p>.. , •./ / / I , , ....si I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DIAGRAM</head>
        <p>DIAGRAM</p>
        <p>ha.lf ball stroke played with running side from the baulk end of the table; that is with the naP of the cloth. The intersected circle shows the cue ball at the moment of oontact, and the intersected line A C shows the way the, cue ball is pulled out of the plain half ban path A B</p>
        <p>• Diagram 12 shows this in a somewhat exagger- ata form.-:iA half ball stroke isplayed from the baulk end of the table, that is, with the nap of the cloth, and the cue ball is struck with plenty of running side (right hand side in the diagram). The intersected circle shows the cue ball at the moment of contact, and the continuous line shows the path that the cue ball would take as the result of a half ball stroke without side. As a matter of fact even when running side is used, the cue ball practically takes this line to begin with as it leaves the object ball, but from the very moment it has left the object ball the side working with the nap of the cloth pulls the ball farther and farther out of the line of' its original path. Thus in diagram 12, although the cue ball laden with plenty of running side may travel from A to C, it does not travel in a straight line from Ato C. Just as it leaves th object ball it is travelling towards B, and at any time before,C is reached it is travelling towards some point between B and C. The path of the cue ball from A to C is really a curve, though of course the curvature is but slight. ~If, however, the stroke be played very Slowly on a cloth with plenty of nap, the way the wide pulls the ball is very noticeable to the eye,</p>
        <p>12. ; • / ,A •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>DIAGRAM tl.</p>
        <p>a half ball stroke played with check side with the nav of the cloth. -The intersected line A C shows tbe way the cue ball is pulled Out of the plain half ball path (A 13).</p>
        <p>When the cue ball is struck with check side (left hand side in diagram 13), the cue ball after con- tact with the object ball will be pulled out of the ordinary plain half ball path in the opposite manner to what it is when struck with -running side.</p>
        <p>In diagrams 12 and 13 the intersected line AC shows how the cue ball is pulled out of the plain half ball line AB. When playing from the baulk end of the table, the following may be taken as an in- variable rule:—Right hand side will pull the cue ball to the -right of the ordinary half-ball path and left hand side to the left of this path, and the more slowly the stroke be played the more will the side pull the ball. (To be Continued.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Rise Levi will be pleased to answer in these columns all questions relative to the article appearing weekly, and as far as possible any questions on other points in connection with the game of Billiards. Envelopes should be markea " Billiards."</p>
        <p>B • • • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ANSWERS :CO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>ANSWERS :CO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>In reply to several correspondents asking for a list of the principal breaks made by Stevenson and Dawson last year, I give below a list of all the big breaks (400 and over) made last season by the leading professionals: 788 Stevenson. 505 Dawson. ' 703 Stevenson., 476 Cook. 622 Diggle% 463 Stevenson 549 Dawson. 450 Diggle. 545 Bateman. 437 Dawson. 539 Stevenson. 427 Stevenson. 531 Dawson. 423 Stevenson. 523 Stew eson.</p>
        <p>Bateman's break of 545 is his record, and the 476 break is Cook's record. When Stevenson made his 703 break this constituted his own record, but his break of 788 is the highest break ever made at English billiards under the present rules, the previous best baying been Dawson's 722 made in 1899. In point of merit, however, Stevenson's break of 703 must rank above his 788 break, for whereas the latter contained a glaring fluke at 510, the former was an absolutely flawless break from start to finish. I had the pleasure of witnessing every stroke in the 703 break, and a more perfect break it would be impossible to imagine. It may not be generally known that Stevenson made this break with crystalate balls, thus proving how thoroughly reliable these balls are. Steve- nson has often told me how partial he is to crystalates. Most of his matches this season will be played with these balls, and it is not very long odds against his best break reaching the coveted four figures. A. C. Reade (Peterborough).—John Roberts will be playing in Edinburgh for a week commencing October 31st. As far as lam aware he is going to play in the provinces before appearing in London. E. K. R. (Gloucester).—Vol. 1 of "The Strokes of the Game" will be ready about the middle of October. Price is.</p>
        <p>W. Inglis (Rothesay).—Roberts' record break of 1,392 was made before the push stroke was barred. It is impossible to compare this break with Stevenson's 788 push barred, though probably it was of at least equal merit. It must not be forgotten that in the days when Roberts was making his big breaks with thepush stroke no other rplayer was making anything like the same breaks, and In my opinion had the push stroke been abolished ten or twelve years ago, when Roberts was at his very best, he would still have made some very big breaks.</p>
        <p>Answers to several correspondents are unavoidably held over till next week.</p>
        <p>RISCO LEVI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>'i:MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>'i:MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>The opinion is being very generally expressed that 600 miles, run off in twelve separate sections, was not a sufficiently severe mileage for testing the real merits— or defects—of the light cars during the recent official trials.</p>
        <p>A tour, covering one thousand miles, such as was arranged by the Automobile Club in 1900, would certainly be a great improvement. Considering the con- dition of the steering-gears alone on some of the voiturettes at the conclusion of the trials, it is ex- tremely doubtful whether these could have managed even another hundred miles without very serious mis- hap, so that at the conclusion of a one thousand miles tour it is probable that those which showed up but badly at the beginning through slight stoppages and temporary failures would have achieved the greater distance with more credit than has now been secured by others. The condition in which were a few of the cars at the completion of what was really a very moderate mechanical test was not such as to reassure the nervous amateur on the look-out for a really reliable motor car.</p>
        <p>The tact that tyre troubles were few and far between is doubtless owing in part to the good condition of the Herefordshire roads, and partly to the fact that suffi- ciently heavy tyres had prudently been fitted to the majority. If heavier tyres were to be more generally adopted upon all classes of cars, motorists would certainly find themselves repaid for the slightly increased cost of initial outlay in theadditional comfort and com- parative immunity from trouble they would enjoy.</p>
        <p>Dr. Sophia Jex-Blake has contributed to the column of the "Times "a strong letter in condemnation of the motor-car. She asserts that" for the pleasure of an infinitesimal section of the population, the national roads are being rendered no longer available for the use, safety, and comfort of all the rest of the kingdom," and goes en to declare that it isnot safe to send children out on messages, nor allow them to ride their ponies unattended, and that the country lanes are impossible for invalids and elderly persons who have hitherto enjoyed quiet saunters in their pony-carriages. The terror and nervous shock, too, to which delicate people are exposed constantly by motors are, she ieclares, from a medical standpoint, most serious.</p>
        <p>M This may all sound most convincing to the anti- motorist; but it is just as well that the reverse side of the canvas should be shewn, and this has been most ably done by the Managing Director of the Motor Car Co., Ltd., Mr. Moffatt Ford, who has indited a moderate and well-considered reply. He points out a fact which is systematically ignored by those who maintain that the motor car is merely the rich man's toy." Think, says he, in effect, how many people are employed in produc- ing a car! There are the inventors, designers, engineers, tool-makers, iron-founders, mechanics, labourers, coach- builders, painters, trimmers, clerks, and many, many more besides. The encouragement to and demand for home labour that has been created with the advent of the motor-car can hardly be estimated by those of us who are outside the industry itself; but it is a very serious consideration in the present state of general depression. In a completed state a high-powered car may be the rich man's toy; in the course of its construc- tion it is bread and cheese to very many of the middle ad lower middle class, and, therefore, does not by any means concern only an "infinitesimal section of the population." There is an Act, to deal with those who drive improperly or to the danger of the public, and a very stringent Act it is. The police have ample power to take into immediate costody those who transgress severely, and to this most capable body the public may safely leave the management of hooligan motorists. The ' justice of the case will never be adequately e ealt with by any newspaper tribunal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 24, 1904. ,avi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>NEW WORK FOR YOUNG MEN.</p>
        <p>The National Free Church Council is sending out proposals for the formation of a Young Men's Auxiliary in connection with their local Councils. Young men are to be banded together, taught Free Church Principles, and instructed in the best ways of spreading those Principles by public speaking, village work, &amp;c. It is anticipated that by this means a valuable addition will be made to the Free Church forces of the country.</p>
        <p>WELSH EDUCATION CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Welsh Councils and the people they repre- sent are taking measures to fight the Govern- ment's Coercive Act, and in their opposition will have the fullest support of the Free Churches. Mr. D. Lloyd George, M.P., and Mr. Herbert Lewis, M.P., laid the whole position before the National Free Church Council recently, when a resolution moved by Dr. Clifford was carried unanimously, approving of Mr. Lloyd George's policy.</p>
        <p>AUTUMNAL MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Now that the summer has gone the Free Churches are looking forward not only to a good season's work but to the Autumnal Meet- ings, which are a feature of this and the follow- ing months. The Congregational Union Assembly, under the chairmanship of Dr. Albert Goodrich, is being held in Cardiff the last week in September. Undoubtedly one of the principal items for discussion will be the Reconstitution scheme, to which two mornings are allotted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>BAPTIST UNION MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>BAPTIST UNION MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>The Baptist Union the following week will hold its Autumnal Meetings quite close to those of its sister denomination, its rendezvous being Bristol. One of the features in connec- tion with these gatherings is to be the Minis- ters' Conference, at which the question will be dealt with of "How can a Baptist minister keep abreast of Modern Scholarship." This discussion is to be opened by Rev. IV. Ewing, M.A., B.D.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>MIM6TERS AT OXFORD.</head>
        <p>MIM6TERS AT OXFORD.</p>
        <p>For the last time in its present form the Rev. R. J. Campbell's Ministers' Conference was held at Oxford. At Mr. Campbell's special request, the Rev. F. B. Meyer, BA., as presi- dent of the National Free Church Council, presided at these gatherings. Mr. Campbell has long desired that the National Council take over these meetings, and it is very probable that that organisation will now do so. The future gatherings however, will not be held only at Oxford, but at several important centres throughout the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SUMMER CONVENTION IDEA.</head>
        <p>SUMMER CONVENTION IDEA.</p>
        <p>Summer conventions have become quite popular of late. Conferences were held recently under the direction of the National Free Church Council at Scarborough and Blackpool. These were so successful that it has been de- cided to turn what had been an experiment into a permanent feature of the work. On different lines has been held the British Chautauqua, which has spent a month at Aberystwyth. The meetings on the whole were exceptionally successful. There would seem to be a great future for Summer. Conventions, and next year should see developments in this de- partment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>GIPSY SMITH'S HOME-COMING.</head>
        <p>GIPSY SMITH'S HOME-COMING.</p>
        <p>Gipsy Smith has had in South Africa perhaps the most successful series of meetings he has conducted in the whole of his career. At Johannesburg recently he was entertained tiP lunch by Lord Milner, who expressed greAt interest in his work. At Cape Town the members of Parliament held a reception in his honour, and at various other places he has been highly honoured by municipalities and citizens. Gipsy Smith is at present on his way to England, and is expected here on October 8. A great welcome home meeting will be held at Exeter Hall, Lon,don, on October 12, at which Mr. W. H. Jude, the celebrated preacher-musician, is to take part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>STORY OF DR. HORTON'S ILLNESS.</head>
        <p>THE STORY OF DR. HORTON'S ILLNESS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Horton gives a remarkable account of his long and trying illness in "The Open Secret: A Manual of Devotion," which the National Free Church Council is shortly issuing. He relates how he was arrested at his work by a failure in his sight, and had to lay still for weeks without working. He tells how this led to the production of a devotional manual. "I began to ask," he says, "whether God had in it some design, and gradu- ally the conviction was impressed upon my mind that He had, and that the design primarily was, to set me in such conditions as would make the preparation of this book possible. The selection of those conditions was so remarkable and unforeseen that I could hardly have been more clearly directed if I had heard a voice from Heaven saying, 'write." Those who have been able to see advance sheets of the book, produced under such provi- dential circumstances, say that "The Open Secret "is destined to become one of the classics of devotional literature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WELSH NONCONFORMITY.</head>
        <p>WELSH NONCONFORMITY.</p>
        <p>In no part of the United Kingdom is Non- conformity so strong as in Wales. In the series of "Eras of Nonconformity," which the Rev: C. Silvester Horne, M.A., is editing for the National Free Church Council, the Rev. H. Elvet Lewis has just contributed a volume entitled, "Nonconformity in Wales." Mr. Lewis speaks with ability and knowledge, and at the present state of the religious controversy in the Principality the work will be of timely interest.</p>
        <p>OPEN-AIR CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>The great prominence which has been given of late to the problem of "How to Reach the Masses" has had beneficial results in many quarters. Some splendid work has been done in Open-Air Missions by the local Free Church Councils in many parts. In some cases a. regular campaign of village work has been in- stituted by a town council, an itinerary being drawn up to include a different village every night of the week. In view of the revival of interest in open-air work, a very useful article on "How to Work an Open-Air Service" appears in this month's Free Churchman. Rev. Thomas Champness, so long associated with the Joyfnl News Mission, is the writer. He gives some sound Advice which should be invaluable to those engaged in the work.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER WESLEYAN MISSION.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyans with their forward movement are making great strides in the work of reaching the masses. An interesting account of their Manchester Mission appears in this month's Free Church Chronicle. They have twelve centres in Manchester where services are held on Sundays, and at the Central Hall alone there are sometimes as many as twenty engagements for one Sunday.</p>
        <p>THE NEXT FREE CHURCH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Manchester, as most Free Churchmen are aware, is to be the centre for the next National Council of Evangelical Free Churches. Although the date is still far distant, preparations are busily going forward, both at the National Council offices in London and the local Council's Committee in Manchester. A record gathering is anticipated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BRONCHITIS AND ASTHMA.</head>
        <p>BRONCHITIS AND ASTHMA.</p>
        <p>G. H. SHARPE, F.C.S., Analyst, 11 and 12, Great Tower Street, London, says,in his certificate of Analysis —" lam of opinion that VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE will prove a valuable remedy in the cure of coughs, colds, asthma, and all bronchial complaints." I Vmrces LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is the purest and most efficient remedy procurable for coughs, colds, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh and children's coughs. Price 90., Is. lid.,and 2.8. 9d.,0f all chemists and drug stores.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>For BILE, LIVER ACID</head>
        <p>For BILE, LIVER ACID</p>
        <p>UP-TO-DATE PRIMROSE LEAGUE IDEAS</p>
        <p>Expounded by the Vice-Chancellor at</p>
        <p>Stretton.</p>
        <p>The Vice-Chancellor of the Primrose League, Mr. I George Lane-Fox, addressed a meeting of the Appleton Habitation in the Stretton Schools on Saturday evening. I He said he had the honour a short time ago of a con- I versation with Lady Acton—(applause)—and he could assure ti.e habitation that he found her sympathy with everything taking place in that district, and especially in connection with the habitation, had been in no way diminished by her recent marriage to a member of the so-called Liberal party. Ile did not think there was much Liberalism now left in Lord Acton—(laughter)— and there would be still less left after a long time of married life—(renewed laughter and applause)—as his lordship had such a splendid specimen of the Primrose League as his wife. He noticed that she was wearing a more than usually pretty adornment, and he asked: "Where did you get it?" and the reply was that it had been given to her as a wedding present from the Appleton Habitation. And she added: "It is what I value most." What they wanted now, continued the Vice-Chancellor, was up-to-date Primrose League ideas. It was certainly some consolation to them to know that their method of carrying on their work was so thoroughly appreciated by all parties, especially by Radicals and such-like people, who imitated everything they did. (Laughter.) At the next general election, it might be next year or the year after, they would find all over the country that places which had hitherto been considered quite safe would be attacked; those places that had been asleep would find the grave danger they were in. There would be some eye-openers at the next election, and places which) ,id been considered safe would ho very much surprised at the results of the elections. The three principles of the league were quite as much needed now as ever, especially the first of them, religion, and it ought to be made clear that they asked all who had any I respect for religion to unite. The speaker alluded at some length to the Education Act and said that if they removed all definite religious teaching from the schools a gross injustice would be done to poor little children all over the kingdom; he spoke especially of the chil- dren of the poor, as the rich people could look after themselves by providing private teachers. The Chris- tian people of this country of all denominations, especially Church people, ought to realize how extra- ordinarily important it was that definite religion should not be taken away from the school. in none of the speeches which had been made by Dr. Clifford had he ever seen a sigu of the Gospel of the Lord; it was always ",righteousness, independence." Those who sold heir goods had never done a thing for educa- tion, and now they turned round upon the Conservatives and said they were not going to pay for the endowment of religion. Education without religion was atheism : it was boundito be. They could not give in one iota in the matter of religion. They had given up all they could possibly give up in the recent Act of Parlia- ment. They had, in fact, given up ten times more than any of the fellows who had been jabbering and selling their teapots. If they gave up anything more it would be simply secular education, and that meant ruin to England. (Applause.) The Vice-Chancellor defended the need for tbe existence of the House of Lords. The Estates of the realm were its backbone, and if they did away with the House of Lords, whether right or wrong, they would do away with the British Constitution and 111 that meant. Everything in this world might be changed, possibly for something better, but so far as they knew, so far as history told them, and sb far also as they could, judge, I the constitution they had suited them the best, and therefore, they, as members of the Primrose League, maintained it, and would never vote for men, however clever, good or worthy,who were in the so-called Liberal party, pledged to do away with the House of Lords. Dealing with the Heal controversy, he said they were all looking for further light, and he expected they would get it. It amused him to see so-called advanced Liberals appealing back to the game that went on 60 years ago. Mr. Cobden was, no doubt, a splendid man, and did a great deal for this country, but he was dead, and what was the use of talking about him now? He never saw a motor car or the telegraph probably ; certainly never a telephone A change had taken place since Cobden's time, and yet the advanced Liberals said : "We must not go back a yard beyond Cobden." The Conservatives were really the progressive party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>PABGATE.</head>
        <p>PABGATE.</p>
        <p>THE SEWERAGE QUESTI(M-Dr. Bennett presided at a special meeting of the Poulton-with-Fearnhead Parish I Council held in the National School on Friday evening. I There were also present :—Messrs. R. Denham, James' Houghton, G. Walker, and the Clerk (Mr. F. Wright).— The Surveyor (Mr. Newcombe) attended, and explained his scheme for the improvement of the sewage of the township, and, after fully discussing the matter, the Council unanimously decided to adopt the scheme and recommend it to the Warrington Rural District Council. —The Clerk brought forward the demand for the county rate, which showed a considerable increase on the pre- vious half-year. The members expressed the opinion that, while the Education Act was responsible for a considerable portion of the increase, the lavish expendi- ture of the County Council (Lancashire) in other direc- tions was also a contributary factor to the increase in the rate. Thanks were accorded to Mr. Newcombe for his attendance and for his lucid explanations.</p>
      </div>
