        <head>FOUND DEAD.</head>
        <p>FOUND DEAD.</p>
        <p>''INanANFORD'S POISON for Killing Rats answers well. " After one dressing I found 135 dead next morn log. "J. BLISS, Helsthorpe, Leighton. "I killed 200 rats with Sanford's Poison."--0. COOK, Farmer, Rilesmere. "I stroyed 145 rats with it."—O Virtr,soN. Offord.—Send is Id SANFORD &amp; SON, Sandy, Beds. Sold by Chemists</p>
        <p>ELECTIONEERING in THE THANET the Isle of Thanet ELECTION, presents an entirely</p>
        <p>novel issue. The Unionist candidate, Mr. HARRY MARKS, is dis- tasteful to some of the Unionist electors. Certain incidents in his career appear to them to need more explanation than he has given. Mr. MARKS, once upon a time, was plaintiff in a libel suit. The jury gave a verdict for the defendant. In another action the presiding judge said some disagreeable things about Mr. MARKS and his methods of business. When these bygones were raked up in the Isle of Thanet, a committee of inquiry was appointed. The inquiry was conducted with closed doors ; but it is understood that Mr. MARKS produced his documents, and satisfied the committee thai he was entirely blameless. He pointed out that in the libel suit the judge did not agree with the ver dict ; and he maintained that the remarks from the Bench in the other case were wholly unfound- ed. So the committee decided to commend Mr. MARKS to the Unionist electors of Thanet as a fit and proper champion of the great cause. But the Unionist electors who are not satisfied with an inquiry conducted in this manner declare that if Mr. MaRKs should go to the poll they will abstain from voting, or even vote for the Liberal candidate. In this distressing situation the Unionist journals, for the most part, observe an embarrassed silence. The "Morning Post " will commit itself no further than to admit the possibility "that some personal questions which have arisen may upset calculations based on the expectation of normal conditions." This is decidedly wary. Nobody can deny that the posi- tion of Mr. MARKS as a candidate is far from normal. Moriover, it is clearly abnormal to find thA 'Times "advising the unionists of Tha net not to vote for Mr. MARKS. The inquiry behind closed doors, it is pointed out, will not do. "He should tender his explanations in such a:form that:they can be publicly judged. Se long as he refrains from taking this course, it is not only the right, but the duty, of Unionists to refuse him their support."</p>
        <p>Never was a candidate saddled with such a responsibility. The " Times " virtually invites Mr. MARKS to give alseries of addresses to the electors on those. striking incidents in his career. We can Imagine him beginning every speech like this: "Gentlemen, before I proceed to shew you that the salvation of the Empire depends upon the adoption of the Fiscal policy associated with the illustrious names of BALFOUR and CLIAM-</p>
        <p>BERLAINt let me prove to you how entirely wrong was the verdict of the jury in a certain libel action of which I daresay you have heard." Now, it is well known that personal questions at election time are always more interesting to public meet- ings than the broad issues of national policy. There is a danger, therefore, that the Thanet elector will be much more curious to hear how Mr. MARKS proposes to retaliate on the " Times " than to hear how the country is to retaliate on the foreigner who imposes duties on our goods' Of what avail is it for the "Morning Post" to tell the electors that "Mr. MARKS is a candid advocate of retaliation against foreign countries which impose heavy duties for the protection of their own trade at thelexpense of ours" ? Mr. MARKS may be very candid on the issue; but what the " Times demands from him ia a candour of a much more personal kind. "He is in full sympathy with the proposals Mr. BALFOUR and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN have laid before the country," cries the faithful "Morning Post," desparately striving to keep everything on a purely normal basis. Yes; but are Mr. BALFOUR and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN in full sympathy with Mr. -MARKS ? "It is better to lose a seat," says the "Times," "than to associate the cause of Unionism " with this particular candidate. After that we are curious to know whether the Prime Minister will regard the conditions in Thanet as so normal as to require rom him the usual message of good cheer to the Unionist champion.</p>
        <p>How to deal with under- FREE MEALS FOR fed school children is me CHILDREN. of the most difficult of</p>
        <p>our social problems. It is urged that compulsory education carries with it logically the obligation for the community at large of feeding the starving scholars. If children are forced into school when they are not in a physical condition to learn, is it not the duty of the State to make them sound in body as well as in mind? Such a course may weaken the sense of responsibility in the parents; but that was effectively done when the State undertook to teach the children without any regard for parental wishes. Another stride in the direction of Socialism was taken when this education was made to a large extent free. The argument no is that to give free education when it is of no use, but physically a positive harm without free meals for children who are suffering for actual want, is to strain at the gnat after swallowing the camel. Such briefly stated, is the position taken up by eminent educational authorities like Sir JOHN GORST, and a distinguished physiologist like Mr. JONATHAN HUTCHINSON. MT. HUTCHINSON meets the contention that free :meals for the children of careless and vicious parents will only confirm such parents in their carelessness and vice. If a drunken father spands on his own indulgence :the money that should go to feed his children, he will be delighted to know that the community has relieved the necessity which might have stung him to shame. But, asks Mr. HUTCHINSON, is there any chance that .remorse will ever be operative in such a mihd ? "IC pay be doubted whether the penalty of starvation inflicted on his children ever yet succeeded in reforming a drunkard, and meanwhile it is indisput- able that the children are starved." If you take it as proved that the callous indifference of some parents, and the grinding poverty of others, com- pletely nullify the education of so many children as gravely to c.ompromise the welfare of the people, then the case for free meals is strong.</p>
        <p>But, on the other hand, it is argued that the evil is greatly exaggerated. The testimony of Miss MARGARET FRERE, who has had a large experience in one of the poorest districts of London, is rather striking. "For seven winters I organised and managed in the Tower-street Board School a system of free dinners; 240 meals were given four days a week for five months to children selected by the three head-teachers as 'necessi- tous.' in 1898 I closed this dinner charity, finding it was not valued by the children or their parents. This led me to suspect it was not wanted, and this I found to be the case." A relief committee visited all the "necessitous " homes. "We found the majority of parents able and willing to feed their children ; the mothers were, many of thein,ignorant and foolish but we found they welcomed our visits, and were often quite anxious to do their best for their children. No one complained over the loss of the free dinners, which was remarkable, as the dinners were good and nicely served." As all this happened in such a district as Seven Dials, it suggests that the picture of the starving school children of London is rather overdrawn. Miss FR.ERE, not without reason, is " sceptical as to the existence of those 122,000 unfed school children in London alone, of whom we have heard sal much lately. The returns of underfed children, on which the calculations are made, ' require, I think, to be critically examined by ex- pert visitors among the poor before they can be of any use for statistical purposes." Probably it will be found that the :majority of the underfed chil- dren suffer not so much from sheer lack of food as ' from the kind of diet provided by "ignorant and foolish mothers. Their ignorance of cookery can scarcely be said to throw upon the ratepayers the obligation of providing dinners that are "good and nicely served." Rather is it an argument for the compulsory teaching of cookery to girls of the poorer classes. If they were as competent in this most important department of life as French girls, there would be an enormous improvement in the health and habits of the people.</p>
        <p>Trig supporters of THE "REGION OF the Government, as FANCY." might have been expected, do not greatly relish Lord ROSEBERY'S speech at Lincoln. We are told by the "Standard" that Lord ROSE- BERN misuses his gifts. He was meant to be a writer of "sparkling essays." But he does not write them; he addresses great political meetings, and shoots the arrows of flippant ridicule at serious statesmen like Mr. BALFOUR and Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN. Worst of all, the arrows stick and ranicle. Lord ROSEBERY transfixes an opponent quite playfully. For example, he said at Lincoln that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S exercises on the Fiscal question 'belonged to the "region of fancy." Could anything be more fauciful than the assurances that the new tariff will benefit our farmers without adding to the burdens ot the consumer ? The farmer is to grow wheat at a handsome profit; he cannot do that without a rise of prices; but a rise of prices is not to cost the consumer anything. The Colonies are to send us so much wheat that prices will be kept down; and yet the land which has gone out of cultivation in England is to smile once more</p>
        <p>with golden grain. Mr. BALFOUR is also in the "region of fancy," for he keeps on telling us that he is a Free Trader who sympathises heartily with the objects of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S policy. Lord ROSEBER,Y says that after the Prime Minister's closing speech on the subject last Session, and after Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S speech at Welbeck, he despairs of understanding either of those great men. The "Standard" does not regard this as flippant. It is in entire agreement with Lord ROSEBERY on the Fiscal controversy. But when he demonstrates that the general record of the Government is just as bad as its Fiscal record, he ceases to be the searching critic, and becomes the sparkling essayist out of his native element. Although Mr. BALFOUR staves off the evil day when he must appeal to the constituencies, every- body knows—none better than the " Standard '. —that he is a beaten man. There is not a single 'item of his domestic policy that excites enthusiasm Lord ROSEBERY is reproved for showing that the Army Reform scheme of the Government is just as unreal as anything else. Nobody feels its, unreality more acutely than Mr. ARNOLD-FORSTER who is apparently preparing to retire from :Parlia- mentary life at the General Election. He is weary of a cabinet which wants 'neither his scheme nor any other scheme. Mr. BRODRICK was shunted out of the War Office into the India Office. So fat as India is concerned, he is now a nullity, for it is the ;Viceroy who controls , the region of fact, while the Indian Secretary of State poses in the "region of fancy." But Mr. BRODRICK is still able to keep his old Department in a stagnant condition. He has seen his own Army scheme melt into thin air, but he prevents Mr. ARNOLD FOSTER'S scheme from taking sub- stance. Thus we have a Government which is manifestly incompetent to apply the lessons of the War Commission, and to give the country any satisfactory equivalent for its vast expenditure on our military organisation. We :are presenting at this moment, as Lord ROSEBERY says, sucth a spectacle to the great military Powers as cannot fail to prejudice our position in Europe. Lord ROSEBERY adheres to his belief that instead of sending Lord KITCHENER to India the Govern- ment ought to have installed him at the War Office with full powers for reorganising our military system. Even in kis present post, it is hinted,his advice is not taken. Probably his advice is neutralized by the chaos at the War Office; for when there is no working scheme for making the British Army at home a vital thing, it is plain that the Indian Army, dependent upon drafts from England, must suffer in proportion. The Government pays no heed to these considerations because it dwells in the "region of fancy." Its su cessor will have a heavy responsibility; but it makes the need for an early change of Govern- ment all the more urgent.</p>
        <p>The municipal elections in Warrington this next November are likely to be more than usually interesting. In addition to the retiring councillors who may seek re-election there will probably be two Labour Candidates anxious to join Councillor Stott and help him to fight the battle of the work- ingman in the Council Chamber. Bewsey Ward will have two vacancies, and there are numerous and varied rumours in regard to the prospective candidates. Beside what is already known or surmised we venture to predict an out- burst of Non-Trading activity. The report of the Non-Trading Commissioners will be issued ere long, and there is no likelihood of it taking the same line as the complacent document issued by the Corporation representatives. Revelations and strong condemnation of some peculiar methods of trading are promised, so we learn, and it is more than likely that there will be a renewal of the strife between opposing factions. In this event the municipal elections may be as keenly contested and fraught with as much sensation as those of 1902-3.</p>
        <p>To the man whose vote is only worth a pint of beer owl three-penny bit, the doings of the Revis- ing Barrister are not of much concern, but to a man who takes an interest in the welfare and good government of the nation the franchise is a very precious thing. Such a man, we should say, is the Rev. J. S. Hughes of Stockton Heath, whose vote has, in the short space of five days, been lost and found again. Mr. Hughes is a passive resister, but this fact alone would not bring him into conflict with the Revising Barrister so long as distraint was executed before July 15th. In Mr.. Hughes' case, however 1 it was not put in force until about twelve days had elapsed and the fatal 15th was long past. The fault appears to lie at the door of the police and the overseers jointly, and there are not a few people ready and willing to suggest that there was a certain amount of political trickery asso- ciated with the affair. On Saturday success appeared to have attended the little dodge for disfranchising the Rev. J. S. Hughes, as the Barrister could not be made to see that the edu- cation rate and the poor rate were divisible, and he therefore disallowed the vote. Mr. Hughes was the recipient of much sym- pathy and a few covert smiles. In the mean- time the Barrister has slept on the question and in the kindness of his heart found a way of allowing the vote after all. The reversed decision was communicated on Thursday, and Mr. Hughes therefore rejoices. He may, if so inclined, crow over his political enemies, for never was the truth of the saying "he laughs best who ',:laughs last" better exemplified.</p>
        <p>There was an engaging frankness about the address of the Vice• Chancellor of the Primrose League which he delivered in Stretton Schools for the edification of those who dwell in the Appletor_ Habitation. "What they wanted now," he said were up-to-date Primrose League ideas." This is certainly refreshing from a high-placed official. Primrose League ideas are so often of the antique and crusted Tory type that it would be interesting, to say the least,to hear of a new development. It would be interesting, for instance, to learn from the lips of a Vice-Chancellor whether the Primrose League has been captured by Mr. Chamberlain for the furtherance of tariff reform. But on perusing the Vice-Chancellor's address the only idea which strikes us as bong up-to-date—and shall we say prophetic—is the sentence in which he assures us that " There will be some eye-openers at the next election, and places which have been considered safe will be very much surprised at the results of the elections ! " The .Vice• Chancellor may have had the Knutsford division in his mind, or he may not, but Mr. King, who is attacking this Tory stronghold with such pluck and energy on behelf of the Liberal party will no doubt derive considerable encouragement from this Primrose League pronouncement.</p>
        <p>On the fiscal questiop, the Lord Chancellor says he is looking for further light. This is rather a surprising admission, seeing that the member for the division has already committed himself to Protection. In a sporting phrase, the Hon. de Tatton Egerton, the member for the division, once !spoke of Mr. Chamberlain tss the "faster horse," and there and then proclaimed himself a solid backer of the aforesaid quadruped on the question of Tariff Reform. The Primrose Leagne is evi- dently not quite so up-to-date. The knights and dames of this distinguished league have evidently found that a tax on food it. not a popular cry even in the most Conservative of country districts, and have come to the conclusion that it is better to express no "idea "on this subject for the present at all events. Speaking on the retention of the House of Lords, the Lord Chancellor was more emphatic. "The estates of the realm were its backbone," he said; "and if they did away with</p>
        <p>the House of Lords, whether right or wrong, they would do away with the ;British Constitution and all that meant." This is the first occasion .on which we have heard of the House of Lords being the embodiment of the British Constitution. But it was evidently thought good enough to frighten any poor Hodge who dared think for a moment of interfering with the privileges of the irresponsible lords who sit in the gilded chamber.</p>
        <p>A remarkable zeal for reform has taken possession of the members of the Cons?rvative Labour Party. At the open-air meeting on Thursday, Mr• Fearneley gave quite a long recital of the griev- ances under which the working classes lacour, and even went so far as to advocate old age pensions. Granted—and readily too—that the injustices exist, working men will be most concerned with the remedy. The Government has had ample time in which to demonstrate its anxiety to improve the lot of the people. Yet, not one of these great q uestions has been faced. It is but a short time since Mr. Shackleton's Bill affecting trade disputes was rejected,and amongst those who voted against it was Mr. Pierpoint, M.P. How, then, are trade unionists to hope for redress from the Conservative party? Mr. Fearneley does well to emphasise the need for sweeping reforms but if these are to be accomplished there will have to be a change of representation. Recent bye - elections have shown that this feeling is gaining ground throughout the comatry, and there are indications that when the time comes Warrington electors will cast in their lot with the true progressive forces.</p>
        <p>In sanctioning the erection of two new elemen- tary schools, the Education Committee took distinct step forward on Monday evening. Tb° accommodation difficulty has been a pressing one in Warrington for many years past and it is one of the legacies of the voluntary system which has been handed down to the new authority. Taat it will prove a costly legacy there is no room for doubt. During the past week a deputation has been visiting several neighbouring towns with the object of acquainting themselves with the latesu developments in the building of schools. It is to be hoped that the Building Cornrnit.ee will n(A strive after elaborate ornamentation or eilec.t. They have a striking object lesson before them in the notorious "Palace of Justice." A plain and substantial school building, with modern applianceo for lighting, heating and ventilating is what le wanted and this is quite as much as Warrington can afford at tile moment. When it is considered that no less than seven sites have been securod. for new schools, and that the cost of two schools Is likely to total up to £20,000, some idea of the expensive nature of the woik in front of the com- mittee may be obtained.</p>
        <p>The " Brodrick" cap is at last to disappear. Few will mourn its loss, least of all those unhaPPY soldiers who were condemned to wear it. From the first day when it burst upon an astonished public,a mixture of sailor's and fireman's headgear, its ugliness has offended; and subsequent atten3Pts. at improvement, by the addition of a peak, a khaki covering, and what not, have entirely failed to redeem it in the eyes of the nation. Yet it bad its rises. It succeeded an exceedingly smart headdress,known as the field-service cap,the main defect of which was that its shape, like that of the once popular "Glengarry," requircd that it should be, worn at such an angle on the head as to affora absolutely no protection to the wearer against sun or rain. The" Brodrick " cap, offensive as it TO to the artistic sense, did at least give the soldier a p_oper head covering. That however, is the best that can be said for it. Its ugliness was irredeeln able. It sat upon the head like a fat cushion, or pointed skyward in front like the prow of a mediaeval galley. No man looked smart, anicl, most men looked hideous in it ; it a13°11,7 the appearance of pretty unifurms,and accentuateu the defects of ugly ones. In a sense, too, it vo° symbolic. It expressed the attempt to graft on t° the British military system parts, none too voselYt chosen, of that of Germany, and, like that attanil) it failed because it did not harmonise with thek rest of the body on which it was clapped. B!1" while no one deplores its demise, many fear its successor. The Pall Mall milliners have perpe trated too many atrocities for the public to har any confidence that at last they have come to t.ne.tr senses. The Army and the nation alike await 10 fear and trembling the next evidence of their zeal at " Army reform."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. At Bold-street Wesleyan Church to-morrow (SuadaY) the Rev. Marshall Hartley, ex-president of the confer- ence, will preach the anniversary sermons. There will be an augmented choir and orchestra and di? special music will include several anthems. Mr. Leo Richardson and Mr. W. H. Wallington will be the soloists. An open meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society will be held to-morrow afternoon. Mr. A. J. Jolley, J." will preside, and Mr. A. C. Bidgood, of Manchester; will be the speaker. At Cairo-street Chapel to-morrow, harvest thanks' giving services will be held. The Rev. C. Harver Cook will preach morning and evening. The offer- tories will be giren to the District Nursing Association. At Wycliffe Church to-morrow the preacher will be the Rev. Professor Armitage, M.A., of Bradford. , Harvest thanksgiving services will be held ab regh-street Primitive Methodist Church to-morroW, and the Rev. J. F. Matthews will preach mornieg and evening. In the afternoon the harvest cantata, "A daughter of Moab" will be rendered by the eh°lt and friends. On Monday evening a social iTathering will be held.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Church Congress at Liver" pool a public meeting for men only will be held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey street, on Monday, 3rd. The speakers will be the Right Rev. • Bisbgl) Thornton; the Rev. T. Boughton and the Rev. ff• Sanders. October</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with the St. Jolla Ambulance Association will be held at the Gymnasillra on Friday evening next with the object of forming 5 ladies' class in first aid. a Under the auspices of the League of Pity, a sa-e cakes, flowers, and dairy produce in aid of tt National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty zu, Children will be held in the crush rooms at the P 3 the Hall on Tuesday next. Miss Broadbent will open sale, to which admission tvill•be free.</p>
        <p>The Blind Walker Missing. A correspondent states that James O'Neik t,1113, blind pedestrian, who undertook to walk from 1137.0 busland, Scotland, to London, is missing. Ere 171;i5 last heard of at Warrington,. and, judging previous rate of progress, he should be now witili.rd fifty miles of London, but not a word has been he 3-4 of him at home, and it is feared some mishap ur,i't have befallen him, as up to the time of /1.1 telegram he had been careful to keep his friendS from. .0 advised of his movements.</p>
