        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 24, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 24, 1904.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE. 2. Women's Chat and Fashions "A Transformation," by Mrs. J. K. Spender. 3. Events of the Week (Illustrated). Football and Bowling. Runcorn Rural Council. Runcorn Board of Guardians. Our Local Churches. 6. Police News. Warrington Publican Fined. Rev. J. S. Hughes' Vote. Runcorn and Widnes. Short Story by Katherine S. Macquoid. 7. The Free Churches. Primrose League Ideas. Motor Notes. Billiards.</p>
        <p>Child Drowned at Bold. The County Coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse) held an inquest at Bold on Wednesday on the body of Violet Woolaston, two years old, daughter of Samuel Woolaston, a gardener. It was stated that on Satur- day the child's brother and sister, aged five and three years respectively, were out with the; deceased in the roadway, and the boy returned home with the state- ment that the child was in some water. The body was recovered from a pool about 30 yards away, and it was found that life was extinct.—The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death." Marriage °tithe Rev. A. Inglis. There was a quiet wedding at Christ Church, Claughton, Cheshire, on Wednesday, the contracting parties being the Rev. Arthur Inglis, MA, who has 'recently been appointed to the curacy of Winwiek,and lass Ethel Lucy Marion Williams, daughter of Mr. ID. P. Williams, C. 8., Dorlcote, Oxton. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, vicar of Holy Trinity Church, Warrington, with which Mr. Inglis has been connected during the past three years, officiated, and was assisted by the Rev. Canon Robson, *vicar of Christ Church, Claughton. k number of Mr. Inglis's Warrington friends were present at the cere- mony. Afterwards a reception was held, and the newly-married couple departed for the Lake District where the lioneym@on is being spent. The Liberal Bazaar. We would remind our readers that the grand Liberal Bazaar in Parr Hall will be opened on Wed- nesday week. The Hon. Mrs. Emmott Barlow will perform the opening ceremony, while the chair will be taken by Sir J. T. Brunner Bart. M.P., supported by Messrs. J. Emmott Barlow, M.P., and A. H. Cros- field. Mr. and Mrs. C. N. Nicholson will officiate on the second day, and Mr. W. H. Lever of Port Sunlight fame, on the third day. Mr. A. J. King, the Liberal candidate for Knutsford Division,will also be present. From our front page announcements, readers will be able to glean fall particulars of the Bazaar's great attractions and the prices of admission for the three days. They oin rely on getting full value for their money, as nothing equal to the side shows and chamber concerts has been seen at any previous Warrington Bazaar. Bold-street Wesley Guild. • To inaugurate the winter session a successful con- cert was held in Bold-street Lecture Hall on Thursday evening. An interesting programme was well received by a large audience. Miss Ball had the honour of a recall for her rendering of " Good-bye " (Tosti) and responded with "Love, the Pedlar." Mr. Rassall sang in pleasing style "The English Rose" (from " Merrie England") and "The River of Dart" (Cooke), and Mr. Clay gave creditable interpretations of "Thy Sentinel am I" (Watson), and "The Jolly Sailor ' (Squire.) Miss Amy Pigot delighted the assembly with the recitation" land my father-in-law" (Childe-Pemberton) and in acknowledgment of repeated applause gave "Tie stuffed owl." Miss Bouchier executed the violin solos " Tarantelle " (Papini) and "Brindisi Valse " ( Alard) very skilfully and was heartily encored. The choral scene "The gipsies' holiday" (Hessey) was submitted by the follow ing :—Sopranos, Misses Ethel Robinson, Ethel Taylor, Jessie Ball, Minnie Ball ; contraltos, Misses Norah Carnson, Lilian Pigot, Amy Pigot ; gipsy. queen, Miss Emily Taylor. The item proved a popular one, and Part of it was repeated. The two-part chorus Wanderer's night song" (Rubenstein) and the • " Japanese wedding chorus" were well sung by the same young Ladies. Daring an interval refreshments were served and Mr. Davies delivered a short speech commending the work of the Guild and expressing the hope that the winter session would be attended With success. Warrington and District Baptist Union. The annual meetings of the above Union, (the first meetings held since its evolution from the Hill Cliffe Sub-district), were held at the Baptist Church, Little Leigh, on Thursday. The session was opened by a Meeting of committee at half past three. The officers for the year are Rev. J. S. Hughes, president; Mr. W. Rough, vice-president; Mr. B. Few, treasurer; and :Mr. S. Fernley, secretary. At 4.15 a public meeting was held presided over by the president, at which a very able paper was read by Rev. J. C. Elder,dealing With the work of woman in the church. The essayist showed how from the earliest times :woman's Work in the service of the church was not only indis- Pensable but of a most pleasant and successful nature. Many instances were given, cited from both old and new testament records and some of much later date, all tending to show that there was equal necessity and scope tor Woman's work as well as for man's, and that in reality there was no difference in God's sight. After a brief debate on the subject of the paper, the friends adjourned to tha schoolroom and partook of a very well provided tea, for which the ladies providing the name were heartily thanked by Rev. H. Dunnico. In the evening a public meeting was presided over by the Rev. A. Hum :pastor), who after according a hearty welcome to the visitors called upon the Rev. 3. S. Hughes to deliver a presidential address which in its opening phrases contained a high tribute of regard and esteem to the late Rev. John Aldis jun., formerly pastor of that church and originator of the Union under its late title of sub-district. The burden of the address was to call for a deeper earnest- ness and more determined attitude in the present struggle for religious freedom. The speaker showed /low that there had been handed down to us by our forefathers a glorious heritage and demanded that we lose not the privileges so dearly bought but that we hand them down unimpaired to our children, even if they be retained at as great a cost and sacrifice as they Were first secured. After a solo rendered by one -of the members of the church, the Chairman introduced the Rev. E. E. Walter,, Moderator of the Lancashire and Cheshire As3ociation of Baptist Churches, who delivered a brief and stirring address after which the meeting was brought to a close. UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOM—Advt. Animated Pictures at the Parr Hall. The large audiences which have assembled in the Parr Hall during the past week have been loud in their praises of the entertainment provided by the New Century Animated Photo Company. It is questionable Whether a better collection of pictures has ever been Seen in Warriugton,for the proprietors have taken full advantage of all the latest developments in animated. 13hotography. Events of national and world- wide interest are faithfully reproduced, and -thrilling scenes in the Far East give one an idea of the magnitude and bitterness of the campaign. To come nearer home, the recent visit of the King and Queen to Liverpool forms the subject of a remarkably good series of views. The brilliantly decorated streets and vast crowds of I.43ecta.tors, the picturesque assembly in front of St. ticorge's Hall, the royal progress through the city, and the laying of the foundation stone of the Cathedral are all depicted, and the series forms a fitting record cf an event that will live long in local history. The andience also had the pleasure of seeing the children 2f Liverpool disporting themselves in the park in Jaonour of the King's visit, and the revels attendant Illpon the crowning of the rose queens show what a peat day this was for the little ones. To inland- Fwellers the picture of a prompt lifeboat launch possesses considerable interest, as illustrating the expeditious way in which the gallant fellows who are so often called upon to face the perils of the sea gO about their task. The evening's enjoyment is enhanced by the inclusion of a number of humorous productions, and the onlookers have roared with tighter at the chapter of accidents which befel Brown family before they departed for their nolidaye. The adventures of a couple of mischievous Zenngsters drive home the truth of the saying that ~..bOys will be boys." Capital songs beautifully t!-lustrated with lantern slides are rendered by Miss Vorence 3lorrell, and the selections by the Volunteer under the conductorship of Bandmaster 4.‘lchardson, call forth general encomiums.—At the tnatinee on Thursday the children of the Blue Coat 'en 00l and Padgate Cottage Homes were entertained 'lee of charge, and the thoughtfulness of the pro- Pietors was fully appreciated. There will be another .-stinee to-day, and the concluding exhibition will be Riven this evening.</p>
        <p>Personal. Lord Newton, who is giving hostages to modernity by installing an elaborate system of electric lighting in his five-century-old mansion at Lyme Park, and sat for nearly thirteen years in the House of Commons as the Hon. Thomas Wodehouse Legh, once quenched a bore in the House of Commons. Mr. Legh, as he then was had been speaking at great length on the Army Esti- mates, and had made a dramatic pause just before he reached the end of his speech. "If the honourable member has quite finished," interjected the bore," I shall show him that his contention is ridiculous." "Then, for the sake of the House," said Mr. Legh, "I will go on " ; and he did, for three-quarters of an hour. Mr. John Murray, the well-known publisher, announces the impending publication of a new book by Mr. Alfred W. Rees, who for years has written the special nature articles for the "'Standard," and who is a brother of the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees, of Bewsey- road Wesleyan Church, Warrington. The volume, entitled " Janto, the Old Fisherman, and other sketches of Country Life," will be illustrated by six photo- gravures, and of these two—one of which is the frontispiece—are from drawings by a local artist, Miss Florence H. Laverock, of Warrington. BASSMETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. The Mayoralty. A caucus meeting of the Town Council will be held on Monday evening to take into consideration the Mayoralty of the Borough for the twelve months com- mencing November 9th. We understand that there is a disposition to ask Alderman Bolton to accept the posinon for another year. His Worship, however, has intimated to his friends that he does not wish to accept the honour.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for Bas BOOTSin E LANCASHIRE.—Advt. A Warrington Man's Vote. At the Bath Revision Court, the Radicals objected to the vote of Mr. thomas Leopold Akery,of Warring- ton. The qualification was in respect to a share in the ownership of Nos. 1 and 13, South-parade, Bath. This was not the first time that the vote had been objected to, and Mr. Thomas Akery, of Chard,attended on his son's behalf, and gave evidence. —Mr. Ames,th e Radical agent, raised the question of value, but the Revising Barrister ha v mg been satisfied on the point, allowed the vote—Mr. Akery asked for expenses and the Rad ical agent was ordered to pay him 12s. 6d. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Adsitr The Widening of Warrington Bridge. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee held on Monday afternoon at the Town Hall, under the presideney of the Mayor the tenders for the widening of Warrington Bridge by the introducton of cantilevers were opened. That of Mr. Harry. Fair- dough, of Warrington, for £3,263 was unanimously accepted. Mr. Fairclough's tender was not the lowest but as there are only a few shillings difference, it was deemed advisable to give it to the local man. There were 11 other tenders from different parts of the country. Mr. Webster, has prepared the plans and specifications for the work.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Alleged Theft of Tools. At Warrington Occasional Court on Tuesday, before Mr. F. Monks, Thomas Trelfall, alias Trelfa and Trelfer, was iemanded to Daresbury Sessions on a charge of,stealing two handsaws and a leather belt, of the value of Os., the property of the Postmaster General. The evidence showed that prisoner, or a man greatly resembling him, was seen near a tool box at Acton Grange, from where the articles were stolen on Friday last, and subsequently offered the handsaws for sale to a bargeman. The belt was found in the house where he was residing, 19, Wivern-street, Run- corn. He has been previously convicted eleven times for felony as well as for other offe&amp;es. FOR CLASS AND CHINA. of every description try J. S. GA.RLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Rev. A. F. White. The Rev. A. F. White who for some years has had charge of St. George's Mission, and is leaving to take up a curacy at Woolton was on Monday evening pre- sented by the congregation with a purse containing The presentation was made at a meeting held in the mission. The Rev. Canon Willis presided, and spoke in feeling terms of Mr. White's faithful dis- charge of duty, and also of his kindly, tactful bearing to all. Mr. Wilson made the presentation, and the Rev. A. S. Cameron and Mr. Turton alluded to the deep affection felt by all for Mr. White,an.d the general sorrow felt at the severance of his connection with St. George's Mission. The scholars of the Sunday school have also shown their appeciation of Mr. White's work by presenting him with an oak stationery cabinet.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERA WATERS.—Advt. Property Under the Hammer. At the Lion Hotel on Wednesday evening, Mr. T. Sutton offered for sale by auction several lots of pro- perty, but only one lot found a purchaser, the others being withdrawn or sold privattly. The lot sold was the dwelling-house 2, Tilley-street, and it was knocked down to Mr. J. Shaw, of Warrington, for £l3O. Five £5 per cent. preference shares in the Manchester Ship Canal Company were purchased by Mr. Alfred Smith at 325. 3d. per share and ten ordinary shares in the same company found a purchaser in Mrs. Naylor of Warrington, who bid 225. 6d. per £lO share. Two lots of shares in the Warrington Land and Property Company were withdrawn, and lot seven, comprising a freehold dwelling-house, 186, Knutsford-road, was sold privately. The property offered for sale, but withdrawn, included dwelling-houses in Lockett- street, Latcnford, Rose and Crown-street, Litton's-row and Kerfoot-street, Warrington. Messrs. Jeans and Son, solicitors, Winwick-street, were the solicitors to the vend'irs. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC IS the KING Off TONICS. I 63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. The Kubelik Concert, Next Friday evening Warringtonians will be afforded an opportunity of hearing Herr Kubelik, the world-famous violinist, and judging from the rapid sale of tickets there should be a crowded attendance at the Parr Hall. The great artiste will be accom- panied by his wife, and we are informed that he will play on a 1,000 guineviolin. Those who attend the concert are promised a rich musical treat, as is evidenced by the remarkable popularity which Kubelik's talents have gained for him. The programme is an excellent one, and will include the following selections by Kubelik :- -" Concerto in D Minor," Paganini ; Concerto in G Minor," Man Bruch ; " Triiumerei," Schuman; "Bohemian Dance," A. Randegger, jun. ; "La ronde des Lutins," Bazzini. There will be songs by Miss Muriel Gough (soprano) of Continental fame, and pianoforte solos by Miss Katherine Goodson who was 'taught by Paderewski's master, and has played at the London Philharmonic and toured in America three times Herr Ludwig Schawb will be the accompanist. Admir- able arrangements have been made by Messrs. R. H. Pearson and J. P, Johnson, and there is every proba- bility that complete success will crown their efforts. FIRE GRATES of every description.-1 S. GAR LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-streetWarringion.— Advt. Band of Hope Union. Mr. Thomas Dale (chairman of the Executive Com- mittee) presided at the annual meeting of this Union which was held at Gregory's Cafe. There was a good attendance of Members. The Hon. Secretary (Mr. F. Gandy) read the report of the Executive Committee, which was adopted. Mr. R. Pierce Williams sub- mitted his report as representative to the Executive Committee of the Lancashire aud Cheshire Band of Hope Union, for which he received the thanks of the Council. The following officers were elected : President, Mr. R. A. Naylor; vice-presidents, Revs. J. S. Hughes,j. Yonge,G. H. Carnson,R. Wilkins Rees, J. F. Matthews, Harry Wall, C. L.Stowe, J. Manning; Mrs. Garnett, Mrs. A. Simpson, Miss Naylor, Miss Dutton, Miss Whitfield, Mr. Henry Taylor, Mr. A. Mounfield, Mr. W. H. Sherbum and Councillor Brough ; treasurer, Mr. R. Pierce Williams ; hon. secretary, Mr. F. Gandy ; hon. assistant secretaries, Messrs. Harry Woolley and R. E. 'Jones; auditors, Messrs. W. Wtight and G-. Whitfield ; representatives to Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union, Messrs. R. Pierce Williams and Joseph Owen ; accompanist, Mr. F. Podmore, M.I.U.M. ; conductor, Mr. A. Spruce; organizing and visiting committee, Messrs. H. Wardman, G. Whitfield, R. E. Jones, G. Colley, Joseph Owen, E. Mairs, and J. W. Roberts. Several ladies and gentlemen, whose names are given in the above list were elected subject to their consent. It was decided to form a ladies' auxiliary and to pro- mote a " social" for Band of Ll.,pe workers during the autumn.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Skelton's Premises. Mr. T. Sutton (Messrs. T. Sutton and Sons) offered for sale by auction on Wednesday evening, the premises known as. Jubilee Buildings, occupied by Messrs. Skelton's, Limited. The premises are situate at the corner of Horsemarket-street and Lyme-street and comprise a large shop, with offices, warehouses, store-rooms and workshops. There was a good attendance at the sale. Only one bid, and that for £5,000 was made. This did not reach the reserve price and the lot was withdrawn. Messrs. Davies and Forshaw, Suez-street, were the solicitors to the vendors.</p>
        <p>Cycling Accident in Bridge-street. A somewhat serious accident occurred in Bridge-st on Thursday afternoon. At 2.25 Miss Amelia Lawless, daughter of Mr. Joseph Lawless, 270, Knutsford-road, was riding a free-wheel bicycle down Bridge-street when she lost control and collided with an electric car which had stopped near the Lion Hotel to pick up passengers. At the same moment a car from Latch- ford came along, and Miss Lawless was thrown against it. She was picked up and conveyed to the Infirmary in a cab, and it was found that she had received a wound on the temple. She was detained in the institution.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S.A. Society. The half-yearly distribution of prizes took place on Sunday in the presence of a large number of mem- bers and friends. The Chairman (Mr. E. Osborne) gave an encouraging address on the books chosen by members and the kind of literature which they should read. The lesson was read by Mr. W. Shaw. The soloist, Mr. G-. Ratcliffe, rendered "If with all your hearts" and "My hope is in the everlasting." Miss M. Plinston, A.L.C.M., was the accompanist. At the close of the meeting, Mrs. E. Osborne presented the prizes, which numbered 200, to the members.</p>
        <p>The Water Supply to Manufacturers. At a meeting of the Water Committee held on Thursday, steps were taken to meet the difficulty which has arisen in consequence of the low level of water in the reservoir at Appleton. Owing to the dryness of the summer, the water at the Appleton reservoir has become insufficient to meet the demands of manufacturers, who use this water exclusively for trade purposes. The Water Engineer (Mr. Peas) was instructed to connect the new supply at Delph-lane with the Appleton mains. At the present time Delph- lane water is running to waste owing to the shaft- sinking and tunneling in progress. By diverting the water to the mains which supply Appleton water it will meet all the needs of manufacturers and relieve the committee of their difficulty.</p>
        <p>Interesting Exhibits at the Museum. . At the Museum on the West Staircase can be seen an interesting exhibit in the form of a slow-worm. This is a good specimen measuring we should say 15 to 18 inches in length. As its name implies it is very slow for if you watch it, it gazes on you. and remains motionless until you become weary at waiting to see if it will move. The creature is of interest in not being a worm as the popular name suggests, but is, as the label states" a lizard without external limbs," for lizards are as a rule furnished with prominent limbs. Close to this are "the three British Sundews " from Delamere. These plants are noteworthy on account of their blood-thirsty, habit of capturing small insects that are unfortunate enough to come in contact with their sticky tentacles. The general show of wild flowers is good considering the autumn is well upon us, and includes such forms as, harebell, knapweed, tansy, sneezewort, yarrow,. foxglove, woodsage and many other interesting species.</p>
        <p>The Dean School Exhibition Charity and the</p>
        <p>Winwick Educational Foundation</p>
        <p>_ The examination for the two Dean School Exhibi- tions and the Winwick Exhibition, offered by the trustees and governors respectively, was held on Saturday, the 3rd September. On the report of the Examiner, Mr. E. S. A. Robson, M.Sc., the following candidates have been awarded exhibitions tenable at any schools, other than public elementary schools, approved by the trustees and governors :—Dean Exhibitions, each of a yearly value not exceeding £25: John Sephton, District School, Earlestown ; John Edward Arden, District School, Earlestown. Win- wick Exhibition, yearly value £l3 : Thomas Henry Humphries, St. Peter's School, Newton-le-Willows. Mr. R. Barton is hon. clerk and treasurer to the trustees; and Mr. T. Ormand, hon. secretary and treasurer to the governors.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF Toncs.-63. SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Lancashire inebriates' Board. A meeting of the Lancashire Inebriates' Acts Board was held on Wednesday at Preston, the Right Hon. Sir J. T. Hibbert (chairman), presiding. The report of the House and Farm Committee stated that there were now 31 inmates at the reformatory, and their conduct had been satisfactory. The report of the special committee appointed to consider the terms upon which paying patients might be received at the reformatory states that after full 'consideration the committee had come to the conclusion that it would not be advisable for the Board to take into the reforma- tory any inmates other than those convicted under the Act of 1898. It was therefore resolved to rescind the resolution passed at the last meeting of the board, asking the clerk to make application to the Lancashire County Council for a licence to keep a "retreat " at Langho. This entirely disposes of the question of receiving paying patients at the reformatory. The board decided that three patients be accepted from Westmorland on payment of £1 per week in each case,on the ;understanding that if the board find them- selves pressed for accommodation they can at any time give notice that they will not accept any further patients from Westmorland.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCIL—Advt</p>
        <p>Is Cheshire Cheese Superior to American P The merits of Cheshire cheese were called into question on Wednesday at a meeting of the Wirral Board of Guardians held at Clatterbridge, under the chairmanship of Colonel J. C. Lloyd.—A special com- mittee had been appointed to adjudicate upon the tenders received in connection with the supply of goods for the workhouse, and the only difficulty they had concerned itself with cheese. "We found," remarked the Rev. P. C. Robin, "that of the two samples of cheese submitted to us the American was superior to the Cheshire. • However, there was such a strong feeling we should have the Cheshire cheese that we decided to have the American one month and Cheshire the next." " It has always been a strong point with us," said Mr. H. A. Latham, " to support home industries." " But I don't see the object of supporting home industries " said Mr. Thompson, "if by doing so we get a worse article." He went on to say that some American cheese he recently had was superior to the Cheshire he had tasted, and had he been on the committee he would have voted for the American.—Eventually it was decided to accept the committee's recommendation. Sankey Harriers and Athletic Club. The annual meeting of this Club was held on Thursday evening at the Club Room,Black House Inn, Sankey Bridgas. The captain (Mr. Joseph Riley) presided. There was a good attendance. The annual report was submitted by Mr. Praeger (hon. secretary). There were 84 members, he said, the number being about!the same as last year. There was keen competition for the prizes presented which included three gold medals, four gold-centre medals, three silver medals,a solid silver, lever watch, and the usual Christmas handicap, when 29 prizes were presented. R. Allen won the novice handicap, and H. Waring and A. Wilcock each secured gold medals in the five miles road race handicap. Gold-centre medals were secured by A. Whittaker (for the fastest time in the novice handicap), L. ,Edwardson (2) and A. Wilcock. The silver lever watch was won by Jack Riley, the club champion of the year. In the Christmas handicap, the first prize was secured by T. Minshull, the second by L. Edwardson, and the third by L. ' Green. In commenting upon the past season, thelon. secretary said the club -went through the whole of the season without a defeat in the club runs. The follow- ing clubs were defeated twice : St. Helens, Liverpool Sefton, Warrington and Winnington. In the Northern Championship, the club did not do as well as antici- pated owing to the unavoidable absence of five of the best runners, through injuries and illnesses. The Ifinancial statement showed that the year commenced Iwith a balance in hand of £2 Bs. and closed with a 'balance of £2 10s. 101d.—The reports were adopted. —lt was decided to make a presentation to Mr. E. , Hodkinson, in recognition of his past services as Isecretary.—The election of officers resulted as follows: ;President, Mr. J. Roughley ; vice-presidents, Messrs. J. Horlock and R. Perdue; committee, Messrs. J. Hughes, J. Gorst, R. Taylor, A. Howard, A. Warren; captain, Mr. Joseph Riley; sub-captains, Messrs. Levi , Green and R. Gibson;handicapping committee, Messrs. J. Riley, and J. Potts ; hon. secretary and treasurer, Mr. L. A. Praeger, assistant secretary, Mr. A. Kinsey.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park. The Volunteer band will play in the Park to-morrow at 3 15 p.m. Programme :—March, " Invincible " Cope ; sacred fantasia, "The Day of Rest "Newton ; stlection, "The Creation" (Oratorio) Haydn; fantasia, " The Village Bride" R. Smith ; selection from Beethoven's Works, C. Smith; march, " The Relief " Holloway ; "God Save the King." Bandmaster, Mr. J. Richardson.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. John Peers. The death took place on fonday at the advanced age of 78 years, of Mr. John Peers, of Liverpool-road. The deceased was a very old resident, and at one time was the licensee of the Roebuck Hotel. By trade he was a bricklayer, and was foreman brick- layer at Whitecross works for many years. He was a member of St. Luke's Church. In politics he was a Conservative, and was one of the first directors of the Warrington Conservative Club Buildings Company, which was formed in 1884. He leaves two sons and four daughters. The funeral took place on Thursday at the Warrington Cemetery. The Rev. H. R. Wild officiated. The chief mourners were Mr. John Peers and Mr. James Peers (sons), Mr. and Mrs. Timmings, Mr. and Mrs. Wright, Mr. and Mrs. Dickenson and Mr. and Mrs. Andrews (daughters and sons-in-law). The coffin was borne to the grave- side by five grandsons, namely : Messrs. S. Hall, A. Hall, John Gould, James Gould and J. Dickenson. A large number of wreaths were received.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. (By " Ovalist.") Warrington's first visit to Yorkshire has not proved as fruitful as was expected. Instead of winning at Hull on Saturday, Warrington returned defeated by 8 points to 7 points and with a feeling of mortification in their hearts. Last season Hull was the only place in Yorkshire Warrington obtained two points from, so that the record so far is considerably worse. Only three matches have been played in this season's tourney, and Warrington have obtained three points less than they did from the corresponding engage- ments a year ago. Most of the Club's supporters, however, have sufficient confidence to believe that these results do not accurately reflect the strength of the team. Just now the side is considerably handi- capped with the result that the form shown at Leigh has not been maintained. Still, I think Warrington's bright day will dawn ere long.</p>
        <p>We must look at the Hull result charitably. The " Third Port " men had lost both their preceding matches and matters were getting rather critical, for the crowd there soon finds other attractions if the football does not come up to expectations. A win over Warrington was hoped for but not expected, and the success will do the " all blacks "a power of good Warrington should not. as a team, be any the worse either. Having tasted the bitters of defeat they will perhaps play up more desperately in order to avoid a. repetition of the dose. They were somewhat humili- ated on Saturday, for there is little question that the poorer side won. Warrington should really have scored double the points they did, and in the first half when the men were fresh they secured a com- manding position many a time only to lose ground by tumbling:and a want of understanding usually foreign to the backs. Dickenson was very faulty in taking the ball and lost several glorious chances of penetrat- ing the Hnll defence. In fact, with the exception of Fish the whole of the Warrington backs took so long to gist going that the match was lost when they had found their feet.</p>
        <p>The incident of the game was undoubtedly Fish's try, which delighted the home crowd almost as much as the Warrington spectators. Bate really deserves most of the credit, however.. He got away from the scrum with a feint worthy of" Smack" and ran down to Taylor before handing to Fish who completed the effort behind the posts and kicked a goal. Hull made great efforts however, and Hall taking advantage of a misunderstanding between the Warrington halves dribbled over and made the score level. Very soon after the same player got another try from a pass near the line. How he eluded Fish at the corner was a puzzle, but he scrambled over and that sufficed. It is rather a curious thing that the ex-Gloucester half, a particularly slow man to play on the wing, should have scnred both Hull's tries,but alone he did it !—to Warrington's discomfiture. Still, the points should both have been prevented had the Warrington men been on the qui vire. Thus at half time Hull held a lead of 8 points to 5 pints.</p>
        <p>Warrington had the wind behind them in the second half, but the forwards had to play secand fiddle. They found the Hull front rank particularly " warm " and were frequently beaten back. This ;in itself ldssened Warrington's chances, but they had sufficient to win by if Dame Fortune had been the least bit kind. Fish kicked a penalty goal and brought the Warrington score within a point of Hull's, while Dickenson was within a ace of scoring also. Fish got another kick at goal and actually hit the crossbar, the ball rebounding back. This was cruel luck,but it was more mortifying still to see three Warrington forwards, who were all up, miss the ball with the line at their mercy. Victory was not to ba for Warrington, and though they kept hard at it, the Hull men maintained their narrow, but all-sufficing lead to the end.</p>
        <p>Hull are hardly likely to get the better of many teams this season, I should think. Their back play is poor, but the forwards will need some reckoning with. It seems odd that Warrington should fail where Widnes signally succeeded. The return with Hull is on January 14th, when Warrington should easily square the season's account, and have a balance on the right side.</p>
        <p>Hallam played an improved game, but he has not yet got into that form which, last season, made him a candidate for county honours. The sandy-haired Hull custodian was the better man of the two on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Some of the Warringtonian's heaviest engagements are coming on just at the time the team is partially crippled. Bradford to-day, and Leeds the following week, are large orders and will test Warrington to the utmost. As a matter of fact, however, there are no easy games now and the team that gains points with consistency has to fight jolly hard to get them.</p>
        <p>Oldham are the only side who have won every match—three —and they, of course, bead the table. This is nothing new for the men of Spindledom, but in recent years they h4ve been unusually unassum- ing. One does not mind seeing Oldham at the head of affairs if one can't be there one's self for they do play good football. St. Helens are not enjoying their promotion very much yet, and if Hull Kingston Rovers were not in the same plight, the Saints would feel very lonely at the bottom of the ladder with no points whatever.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows :-- -For— —Against— P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts. Pte. Oldham 3... 3... 0... 0... 4 8— 32... 5 0— 10... 6 Bradford 3... 2... 0... 1... 1 6 20... 0 3 9... 5 Wakefield 3... 2... 1... 0... 6 1— 15... 4 4 20... 4 Salford 3... 2... 1... 0... 3 5 21... 3 5-21... 4 Swinton 3... 2... 1... 0_ 3 6 21... 3 2 12... 4 Leeds Hunslet Wigan 3... 2... 1... 0... 2 5 19... 3 2 12... 4 Warrgtn 3... 1... 1... 1... 4 5 23... 2 2 10... 3 Widnes Bro'Vn R 3... 1... 1... 1... 2 2 10... 5 1— 13... 3 Hal:fax Runcorn 3... 1... 2... 0... 1 4 14... 6 5 26... 2 Batley 3 1. 2. 0. 5 4 22... 4 8— 32... 2 Hull 3... 1... 2... 0... 3 4 18... 4 8— 32... 2 Leig h 3... 0... 2... 1... 3 2 12... 5 6—.28... 1 Hull K,R 3... 0... 3... 0... 3 0— 6... 1 5 17... 0 St. Helens 3... 0... 3... 0... 6 2 18... 4 10— 38... 0</p>
        <p>Bradford share with Oldham the honour of being un- defeated, so that Warrington will require to reproduce a little semi-final form in order to secure the points at Wilderspool to-day. The Park Avenue men are going very strongly again and the way they polished off Salford last week shows that they are not going to mince matters in their endeavour to retain the championship. Null, after the way Warrington routed the Bradfordians at Huddersfield last April, •there should not be much fear. Anything like the same dash and determination will do the trick, and then again, Wilderspool must not lose its fame as a burial ground for the hopes of visiting teams.</p>
        <p>There are changes from last Saturday's team. Kenyon did too much fly-kicking at Hull, and Hockenhall will play on the wing in preference. The half back vacancy has been given to J. Davies' the Penygraig youth, who has shaped well in his trials and , scored cleverly for the " A team on Saturday. It is a severe trial for him to face Ilarsden and his burly crew, but pluck can do a good deal, and the young Welshman seems well supplied. The full War- rington team will be:</p>
        <p>Hallam, full back, Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Hoekenhall, threequarters ; Bate and J. Davies, half backs; Boardman, Shu.gars, Belton, Thomas, Cook, Jolley, Naylor and Alorrison forwards.</p>
        <p>Harris is still at Allison's'and making good progress. There is just a chance of him being fit for the Leeds mafch, but" I hae ma doots."</p>
        <p>Association Football.</p>
        <p>I The following are the results of Saturday's matches in connection with the Warrington and District League :—Stockton Heath 5, Orford Barracks 2; Thelmall 1, Ea rlestown Y.NI.C.A., 1; Warrington Albions 1, Warrington St. Mary's 0; S. Paul's 2, St.</p>
        <p>Barnabas' 5; Vulcan 1, Stockton Heath Reserve 2; Daresbury 1, Penketh 1; St. .11ary's Reserve 0, Albion Reserve 0. To-day's fixture's are :—First Division —Thelmall v. Warrington St. Mary's, referee, C. Taylor; L ttch- ford Britannia v. Warrington Albionk referee, W. H. Crowther. Second Division—Albion Reserve v. St. Paul's, referee,—Davies. Stockton Heath Reserve V Central,referee J. Mather ;Christ Church v. Widnes St. Ambrose, referee, F. Clegg; St. Barnabas' v. Vulcan, referee J. Lloyd ; St. Mary's Reserve v. Daresbury, referee, J. Parry; Penketh v. Crosfield's Reserve. referee, W. Nicholson.</p>
        <p>. Hockey. Although two full elevens did not turn out at Stockton Heath last week, those who did played up well, and there would be some stiffness about last week end.</p>
        <p>--- The new members all showed keenness, and this year the Heathens appear to have some valuable recruits. Of those who turned out S. Baxter was the best, being full of dash, and showing a knowledge of how to command the ball, while W. Longdin and E. R. Hartley both showed good form.</p>
        <p>Of the old players W. Richardson and R. Pierpoint in the Ist team forwards, both showed that the summer season has not impaired their hockey. About the Ist team halves who turned out, very little can be said, see- ing that 'hey were not properly extended. L. Fair- dough played a sound game at back, and his partner who has evidently now found his right position, showed improved form. The club this year has a lot of good half-backs, and the Selection Committee will not have an easy task when picking the Ist team for the match against Bank- field away, on October Ist. I learn that Bank-field (Liverpool) are a nevv club formed this year, so that the result will not be able to be proved by statistics.</p>
        <p>There is a practice match this afternoon, the Ictst before the first match, and it is hoped that all the mem- bers will turn out. Bully off: 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Angling. An event of more than usual interest took place in. the Assembly Room in connectioniwith Crosfield's Ang- ling Society on Tuesday,when the gold(open champion- ship) medal offered by the Worsley Canal Fishery Board, was presented to Mr. J. Mather, a member of the War- rington Angling Association, and Works Society by Mr. Henry Roberts J.P., who complimented the winner on his successful achievement, in winning first prize out of a total entry of over 400. Crosfield's team which entered to fish for the Worsley Challenge Shield, were also successful in placing themselves sixth in the elm- petition, thus creating a hope that next year they may become the possessors of this much coveted trophy.</p>
        <p>The principal event last week end so far as local anglers were concerned, was the contest to become holders, for the ensuing twelve months, of the handsome shield put up for competition by the War- rington Anglers' Association. For the contest, teams of six, representing various local angling organi.sations competed,and it is satisfactory to note that twenty nine teams put in an appearance. The venue was the length of the Bridgewater Canal at Moore, and two hours' fishing was allowed. The arrangements were superintended by Mr. P. Knight and everything worked out in an admirable manner. The contest resulted in a victory for the Victoria Angling Society represented by the following, the A-eights recorded being :—W. Hamblet,llb, loz ; E. Gilbert, 2oz, 7drms ; T. Tustain, —J. Green, ]oz F. Jolly, 3oz, 11 drams; T. Stuart, 7ozs. The aggregate weight was 11b. 15ozs, 2drms. Only sizeable fish were eligible to be weighed. The other results were Association Team No. 1,11 b. 14ozs. ;3rd,Mersey A.5.,11b. 7ozs. ldr. •, 4th Rope and Anchor A.5.,11b. sozs.Bdrs. ;sth,Bowling green A.S. No. 4, lib. 2ozs. 10drs. ; 6th, Association Team No. 2, 11b. 2ozs. 2drs. ; 7th, King Edward VII. A.S, 11b. loz. Bdrs. ; Bth, Latchford A.S., 11b. 13drs. The lowest recorded weight of any team was 9drs., and the total weight caught in the contest was 211bs. 13ozs. 14drs.— a very good result and eminently satisfactory.</p>
        <p>-- The Latchford Excelsior Angling Society visited Broken Cross, Northwich, on the 19th inst. to fish their annual contest, forty-one making the journey. A fairly good contest resulted, the following being declared winners :—T. Leigh, 14oz. 4drs. ; W. Woods, J. Orford, J. Haynes, S. Leigh, J. Robinson, J. H. Hackney, W. Bodle and J. Wolfenden. The prize distribution was undertaken on Monday evening at the headquarters of the club, the Roan Cow Inn, after which a very harmonious evening was spent.</p>
        <p>The annual contest in connection with the Workingmen's Angling Association was fished at Acton Bridge on the 18th inst. There was an entry of 86 con- testants and a capital list of prizes was offered for competition. The usual two hours' fishing was allowed and the following result declaredl-1, T. Tustain, 15oz. 10drs. ; 2, J. Baker, 1 [oz.; 3, J. Green, 9oz. Bdrs.; 4, J. Briars, Boz. 14drs.; 5, F. Singleton, 7oz. 10drs.; 6, W. Taylor, 7oz. 6drs.• 7, W. Brookes, 7oz. sth-s.; 8, A. Hamblett, 6oz. lidrs.; 9, J. Clare, 6oz. 6drs.; 10, J. Samson, 6oz. There were 47 competitors who registered fish and the prizes which numbered about 40 will be distributed on 11Ionday, October 3rd.</p>
        <p>The members of the Mersey A.S. fished their annual match in conjunction with the Workingmen's A.S. match as above, Messrs. J. Sanson and T. Robinson securing first and second prize respectively.</p>
        <p>Fishing results were not very grand last week end, though a great number of anglers were out. The Old River at Lymm was productive of very little sport indeed and the same may be said of the Bridgewater Canal if gudgeon be excepted. A few rudd have recently been captured at Preston Brook. The river Gowy has been fairly well patronised and a few roach and dace have been creeled. The fishing yielded by the river Weaver has been much of the same character for some weeks, whilst the river Dee has not yielded a great deal of sport to local rods.</p>
        <p>An open contest will be decided at Agden Bridge to- day and entries will be received on the banks up to three o'clock. An open match in aid of the Warrington Dis- trict Nursing Association will be fished at London Bridge, Stockton Heath, on Saturday next. The Latchford Angling Society fish a match to-day at Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>Cycling. On Saturday the members of the Warrington Cycling Club held their annual fishing match at Acton Bridge. The afternoon was an ideal one for cycling, and the roads were in fine condition. Nearly thirty members turned out. The fishing match was keenly contested,there being twenty-one entries. Numerous fish were caught and J. Podmore was declared the winner with four roach, G. Smith taking the second prize, and F.Ga.rnett and W. Taylor the third and fourth respectively. The weighing-in process was productive of much amusement a pair of potato scales withs6lb. weights being brought out for the purpose. Tea was served at the Leigh Arms and a convivial evening was spent. Warrington was reached in good time after one of the most successful afternoons of the season.</p>
        <p>Tuesday evening witnessed the closing night run and afterwards between ttventy and thirty members sat down to supper at Sankey Chapel House Inn. After the tables had been cleared the Captain (Mr. D. Hodge) presided over a smoking concert. The artistes who kindly entertained the company were Messrs. W. H. Ireland and W. A. Clay (vocalists), A. 0. Clay (elocutionist), R. Foster and A. Bennett (violinists), and J. Grounds (accompanist). All the items were creditably rendered and greatly appreciated. The hon. secretary (Mr. E. Pickavanee, jun.) proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the artistes. This was seconded by Mr. Hodge and carried with acclammation. The singing of the National Anthem brought ton close a memorable series of night runs.</p>
        <p>The members of Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Cycling Club journeyed to Leigh on Saturday. The outward run was made via. Padgate and Culcheth and the party returned through Newton and Wiawick, the outing proving very enjoyable.</p>
        <p>LIGHTLNG-UP TIME.</p>
        <p>----- To-night Sunday 7.4 Monday Tuesday 6.59 Wednesday Thursday Friday Cricket.</p>
        <p>. The Moore and Daresbury Cricket Club terminated its matches on Saturday after a very successful season. The total number of matches played was 21, won 13, lost 6, drawn 2. The highest score the club made was 159 as against 112. The highest individual scores were made by Captain Wyatt 58, and George Wright 54.</p>
        <p>The following are the averages : BATTING. Times Most No. of not in an Total Aver- inns.</p>
        <p>out. inns. runs. --age. Captain Wyatt C. B. Allen George Wright 2O 1 54 247 13'00 Rev. F. Clifton-Smith 16 0 38 146 9•12 W. Antrobus T. Forster H. Taylor J. Smdb. lB 2 18 84 3'25 S. Taylor lB 0 14 90 500 G. Gandy J. Knight R. Newall J. Millington 9. 2 6 20 2'8.5 C. Kilshaw l4 6 4* 22 215</p>
        <p>*not out</p>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>Overs. 31dns. Runs. Mts. Aver. H. Taylor C. B.lAllen 5O 12 96 25 384 S. Taylor 151 48 283 63 4.49 W. Antrobus T. Forster</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Forthcoming Elections.</p>
        <p>TWO WARDS TO BE CONTESTED.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council was held on Tuesday evening. Mr. Podmore presided over a good attendance of delegates.</p>
        <p>THE CORPOR:ATION CONTRACTS CLAUSES</p>
        <p>Councillor STorr reported that six of the clauses for the governing of Corporation contracts were adopted at the last meeting of the Town Council. One of the clauses, which had been termed the penalty clause, was rejected by a small majority. Members of the Town Council objected to the term of five years being fixed, during which period no contractor should be allowed to tender if he had broken the conditions of a contract. The secretary further remarked that the members of the Town Council seemed willing to accept the clause if the term of years was reduced to a reasonable number, and he suggested that two years should be substituted for five. After discussion by the delegates the secretary's suggestion was unanimously adopted. CounPillor STorr, at the request of the delegates, promised to submit the amended clause to the next meeting of the Town Council. In its amended form it now reads "Any contractor having been proved by the Council to have broken the conditions of a contract, the Council shall not con- sider any tender submitted by him for the carrying out of any work for a period of two years from the date of the contract having been broken."</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR STOTT THANKED.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. RATHBONE seconded by Mr. SMITH, a unanimous vote of thanks was accorded the secretary (Councillor Stott) for his work on behalf of Trade-unionism, since being elected a member of the Town Council. In reply he stated that the best thanks that could. be given him would be to send more lab Dur representatives to assist him. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS</p>
        <p>In connection— with the forthcoming municipal elections a resolution was carried in favour of the Labour Representation Committee contesting two of the wards in November next. The secretary read correspondence which he had received from the president of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants (Mr. Loraine of West Tjartlepool) and from the secretary of the National Labour Representation Committee relating to statements made in correspond- ence which had appeared in the local press in con- nection with the National Labour Representation Committee and its representatives. Councillor STOTT remarked that the statements which had appeared were likely to injure the Labour Repre- sentation movement if allowed to go unchallenged. He therefore as secretary of the Trades Council (which is an affiliated body) had 'written for information which he had embodied in a letter to the Press. The secretary's action was approved of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Probable Contests.</p>
        <p>It seems a far cry to the Ist of November, but steps are already being taken to select candidates for the Municipal Elections. The Trades and Labour Conn- ell has decided to support the Labour Representation Committee in contesting two wards and it is probable that there will be contests in most of the nine wards. In addition to the usual contests, there is an additional vacancy in Bewsey Ward to be filled up, owing to the retirement of Mr. Samuel d-ough from the Council on his being found guilty of bribery at his election. The councillors who retire in November next are as follow:— Ward. Representative. Town Hall W. J. Forshaw (C) Whitecross W. Brough (L)</p>
        <p>Bewsey . • W. Roge—rs CI; Orford J. Wilkinson (L) St. John's H. Thornton (NT)</p>
        <p>Fairfield</p>
        <p>D. S. Duncan (L)</p>
        <p>Howley St. Austin's</p>
        <p>S. Davies (C) T. Burton (!.,)</p>
        <p>Latchford</p>
        <p>L. Ellison (L)</p>
        <p>It will be seen that five of the retiring Councillors are Liberals, two Conservatives, one a Non-Trader and one an Independent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ENDING SEPTEMBER 17th, 1901._</p>
        <p>Daring the week, the following births and deaths were registered :—Warrington Registration District births,4o ; deaths, 33. The excess of births over deaths was 7. _</p>
        <p>• _ The death-rate from all causes was equal to 38.0 per 1,000 against 20.9 the average of the previous 4 weeks and against 232 the corresponding period of last year. Thu,. death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 91 per 1,000, against 110 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against 7•7 the corresponding period of 1903. The ages of mortality were:- 0-1 1-5 57.15 15.725 25-65 65—up. Total</p>
        <p>9 10 2 1 7 4 33 The deaths included-1 from measles, 11 from diarrhoea, 2 phthisis,'2 from other tubercular diseases, 2 from heart diseases, 1 from suicide, 14 from all other causes. _ _ _ _ __ - -</p>
        <p>The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during tlle week :—Scarlet fever, 30 cases; enteric fever, 1 case ; erysipelas, 3 cases. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending September 17th, 121. METEogoLoGv.—The mean temperature was 57 Fah. and therefore 1 deg. higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 70 F., was recorded on the 16th inst.,and the minimum:temperature 42F.,0n the 17th inst.</p>
        <p>The rainfall measured '3O inch. The general direction was S.E.</p>
        <p>J. GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
