      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Kin. in Scotland.—Glenquoich Forest, where the King has been on a 'Visit to Lord and Lady Burton, is in that part of the Highlands which is but little known to his Majesty. The Lodge is beautifully situated on the shore of Loch .Quoich, and the neighbourhood provides some Of the grandest scenery in Scotland. Like Queen Victoria' the King is never so well as when in Scotland, and of late years his Majesty has grown more fond of life in the Highlands, which formerly he was disposed to vote a little dull. His Majesty has effected many improvements at Balmoral, both inside and outside the Castle, which help in making life there more comfort- able and enjoyable. The golf links for instance have been put in thorough order, and though the King rarely, if ever, touches the clubs, these facilities are much enjoyed by visitors. His Majesty has also had first class links laid out at Windsor and Sandringham, but the best private links are said to be those at Chatsworth where the Duke of Devonshire has spared no expense in making them as perfect as possible. Heirs and Princes.—The birth of a ;on and heir to the King and Queen of Italy is a happy sequel to the ' recent advent of a son in the House of Romanoff. jfr' di Awl:session of disappointments, good fortune has come' to both these royal families, and ono can imagine the jlond of sympathy and joy there must be between the llmpresS of RizA4 Rd Queen Elena of Italy. It was thought that italy'a heir would have been known as the ' Prince of ROme, but this might not have been altogether pleasing to the Pope, and he has been named Htimbert, .I°.rince of Piedmont, Queen Elena is noted for he intense love of children, a trait for which the Italian people are distinguished, and the advent of the Prince will make her more popular than ever. As is well known, she is one of the large family of the Prince of Montenegro, which boasts a connection with this country, as the eldest son Prince Danilo, is married to Princess Melitza of Mecklenburg-Stralitz. There are seven or eight daughters, all noted for their beauty. Princess Xenia was recently betrothed to the Grand Duke Michael, brother of the Czar, but Vera the youngest, is till heart and fancy free. An 01d-fashioned Harvest Home.—lt recalls oldtimes I tn read of the harvest-home festivities on the Eoglefield estate of Mr. Benyon, Lord Lieutenant of Berkshire, who gave a whole day holiday to all his employees, and after a thanksgiving service in the parish church, enter- tained them all most hospitably with a dinner, concert, and other delights. Unhappily the majority of farmers I have not much heart for such festivities as used to attend the bringing in of the harvest, when foreign competition was less severely felt, and wheat could be sold for something like twice as much as it realises at present. It has always been said, rightly or wrongly-, that the farmer would exercise the privilege of gram- Ming in any circumstances, but we cannot expect very sunny optimism in a man who knows that if the weather is against him, he may experience loss, and that at the best his profit must be a small one. Their Palmy days are Over.—Time was when palmists of both sexes, always with mysteriously sounding names, could ply their calling with impunity, and receive without risk fees from the credulous, or seekers after amusement, but now the palmist has joined the ranks of police-dock characters. There is still the same amount of credulity in this old world of ours, and the law has at last felt it its duty to protect easy dupes,but at the same time there are many who think that if well- to-do people like to spend their money in "having their fortune told "they should be allowed to do so. Senseless Entertainments.—Many people besides Addison have to deplore the exhibition of senseless and dangerous feats, and just now the attention of such censors is directed to the entertainment afforaed by the lady known as "La Goulue " who was so terribly ' mauled by a lion at St. Cloud. Something must be said of this lady's courage,but it is a great pity that ' she had not some more useful opportunity of displaying it than was afforded by the attempt to tame a fierce lion. It is sufficiently obvious that lions were never intended to play leap frog with human beings, and if their feral nature is so completely ignored,it is scarcely a matter for wonder that the results are not satisfac- tory. In all cases some blame attaches to the public among whom there are too many people of the character of Eugene Sues, englishman, who followed a lion tamer from town to town, with the morbid desire to be present when the mishap he anticipated should occur. As in most other matters, the demand creates the supply, and as long as there is a passion for unhealthy excitement,so long will men and women be fo-and to risk their lives in feats on the trapeze,parachnte ascents lion-taming and other such entertainments which serve no useful purpose. Long Gold Chains worn again.—The long dangling souvenir chain seems to be once more coming into favour and is being worn by women of fashion. It is an appro- priate ornament, but one needs a lot of acquaintances to fully equip a souvenir chain, as each of the numerous little attachments should be a gift, to recall an event or a visit of pleasant memory. The idea is a charming one in every way, but it is to be hoped it will not lead up to the overloaded specimens of a few seasons back,when the family jewels—ancient and modern—were hunted over for all the little ornaments that could possibly be strung on to these chains. The flexible chain bracelet which has been with us so long, has gone out of fashion and there is a return to the plain round solid band, fastening with a clasp. Mostly they have a small gold chain attached, to which a favourite charm or bauble of some kind can be fastened. Autumn Millinery.—High pottle crowns are a prominent feature in autumn millinery, but the style lias been somewhat overdone, and there is little doubt that smart milliners will discard it for other and more attractive modes. Nor will this be regretted, as, with exception for a very few, the high crown is not the most becoming form of headgear. The hats and toques worn at the moment are mostly trimmed with velvet, many of the autumn straws having open mesh work through which a velvet band is threaded. For orna- mentation they depend chiefly upon clusters of fruit, such as plums, peaches, nuts, and blackberries, also made of velvet. They are exquisite imitations of Nature, as also are the many varieties of autumn leaves</p>
        <p>and flowers, which adorn the present modes in the millinery world. I Winter Coats.—The new" fur cloths', as they are styled,promise to enjoy a great vogue in the mantle and coat department this coming winter, especially the seal- skin and moleskin varieties. For the former,the correct colour has been considered best. The latter,however, is not being presented in the natural mole shade but in a more becoming and altogether prettier tint, viz.,bronze. Concerning the cut of our winter coats, we are un- questionably going back to the tightly-fitting, long basqued style, though the useful sac is by no means banished yet. In travelling wraps, the Inverness has quite taken the place of the circular golf cape,and truly wonderful to relate, it is quite possible for the middle aged or elderly woman to purchase a suitable mantle. But in these days when grandmothers and grand- daughters dress very nearly on the same lines, the idea of stocking garments suitable only for the elderly,seems a risky proceeding from the shopkeepers' outlook. Excellent for the Complexion.—Take the whites of four eggs, add a grain of Alum, and boil in half a pint of rosewater, double strength. Beat well until the mix- ture is thick, then place it away to cool. Spread this paste on the skin every night for a week or two, when it will be found to have greatly improved the complexion.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>" SUMMER-END " FAG.</head>
        <p>" SUMMER-END " FAG.</p>
        <p>Bile Beans Tone up the System.</p>
        <p>At the end of summer the body, run down by summer's heat, needs to be toned up and prepared to withstand the trying autumn and winter seasons. The liver and digestive system are deranged by summer's heat, and their imperfect operation gives rise to that weakly, " tired-out " feeling, which is now so common, and may be called "summer-end fag." Bile Beans act directly on liver and stomach, and by thoroughly stimulating them tone up the body and prepare it for the coming season. Thousands of cases prove this. lIIMrs. C. Garner, of Rotbley, Leicester, says: "At the end of last summer I was completely run down and in a most depressed state. The doctor prescribed for me, but no relief was obtained, and I continued languid and out Of sorts for months.l lost appetite,lost strength, and had attacks of palpitation upon the least effort. My nerves gave way to such an extent that the least noise frightened me, and I dared not stay in the house alone. The doctor having failed to give me relief, I made up my mind to try bile beans. After I had taken a few doses there was a perceptible change for the better. Then I began to improve more rapidly, and it was not many weeks before I was changed from a weak, nervous, depressed, spiritless woman, into a bright, healthy, active person." Bile Beans worked this change. They also cure indi- gestion, constipation piles, headache, female ailments, wind, debility, and all liver and kidney ailments. Of all chemists, or post free from the Bile Bean Co., Red Cross Street, London, E.C., upon receipt orprices, is. Lid, or 2s. 9d. for a " family size " box (2s. 9d. box contains three times Remember that as "a stitch in time saves nine," so, to prepare the body for the coming trying season by taking a course of Bile Beans, is the surest way of escaping illness. Disease fastens itself upon weakly systems only. If you feel traces of summer-end fag, your system is weakly. The cure lies in Bile Beans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SMART GIRL THIEVES.</head>
        <p>SMART GIRL THIEVES.</p>
        <p>Extraordinary St. Helens Case.</p>
        <p>Along series of serious charges was laid at St. Helens Police Court on Monday against two young girls and their mother. Margaret Sefton, aged fourteen years,and Annie, her nine-year old sister, of 55, Victoria-street, were first charged with stealing Ll is. 10id., from Mr. 0. M. Barton's house, 158, Boundary-road. Mrs. Barton said that on Saturday morning she and the servant were in the front part of the house tor sometime. When she went into the kitchen she saw the two girls ran- sacking the drawers and missed the money. Detective Staveley went to the Seftons house, and the girls said they only got 7s. which was in a purse under the table- cloth on the table. The two girls were further charged with breaking into 36, Wolseley-road, on Saturday, the 10th, and stealing £2 10s. Mrs. Andrew Fair said that on this Saturday she left the house, and on her return fouiad the sitting-room window, the back door and the yard door open. When arrested the elder girl said, "There was only me and our Annie in there. She got through the window and unlocked the back door and let me in. We went upstairs and got 50s. out of a drawer in the front bedroom. We took it home to our mother." Annie said the same. The mother, when charged with receiving said "I got the money, but I don't know where it was from." There were also other charges of a similar nature against the girls. The Chairman said it was a most, disgraceful thing on the part of Mrs. Sefton. She hada husband who worked hard and supported the family, and here she encouraged her children to go about the place stealing. It was a most scandalous thing. If it was not for her present condition they would give her much heavier punishment. Mrs. Sefton: • I didn't encourage them. The Chairman: It is nothing in but encourage- ment. I have not the least doubt mmy own mind that you actually sent them out to steal. You will go six weeks to gaol. The girls were bound over with their father to be-of good behaviour. •</p>
      </div>
