      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 24, 1904. FASHIONS OF TO-DAY. Blouses are quite the most popular item of dress, but as the season advances and an autumnal chill creeps into the air the airy muslins and diaphanous laces will be replaced by heavier and more durable materials, such as crepe de chine or Oriental satin. For morn- ing wear cashmere and cream white alpaca are greatly in demand, simply made and neatly fitted, while a soft turnover lace collar tied by a silk cravat finishes the neck. Many of these are made in a similar style to a Norfolk jacket, while nearly all boast deep yokes of various shapes, as whether pointed or ronnded ,or adorned with numerous scallops they find a place in fashionable favour. The thinner blouses which were used without a coat all summer can now be worn under a light bolero or white cloth coat, and can be, on bright days, worn even without these coverings when arranged over an underslip of silk, or should the material not be very open, one of sateen</p>
        <p>1: was taken up to see ficr in her bedroom. -1 can't bear it; I can't bear it; it fills me with horror; you must keep him away."</p>
        <p>I tried to make her understand that such a posi. tion was quite unreasonable, and that the solemn vows that she had taken upon herself could not be put aside for a mere accident or an altered face. "You yc-irself mght have had small-pox or you inighitavt been burnt, and he would have cared as much for you. You must remember that what you undertook was for better, for worse, ' " was one of my very ordinary and hackneyed arguments, _ which, of course, had no effect at all upon her." _ _</p>
        <p>She had quite forgotten her dignity in her out- burst of horror, but I saw that the horror was unfeigned, and that she could hardly control it. The affection for her husband which had always been easily chilled, and to a certain extent unde- veloped, seemed now to have vanished altogether. Sometimes she would put a stress upon herself and try for a few minutes to overcome her feelings, but the effort was too great for her. She was invariably taken with shivering fits and attacks which turned her hot and cold whenever he tried to approach her.</p>
        <p>The curious thing was that Colwyn, knowing bow much she worshipped beauty, had been so little prepared for this crisis. The poor fellow had really believed that his wife would have been as thankful as he was for his narrow escape from death, and—attaching little importance to his own handsome looks—had taken it as a matter of course when he was told that he had lost them. First of all he had hoped that the change of feeling in his wife would wear off after a time, or could be treated like a woman's caprice. But after weeks had passed he began to suffer as keenly as she did. Her glance of aversion and her avoidance struck him to the heart, and he needed nobody to tell him that the very look of him made her shiver. Her attempts to- control herself were certainly not successful, since it is recorded that on one occasion, even in the presence of a servant, when he came close to her to arrange a cushion for her on a sofa, she cried out: "Go away! go away! I cannot bear it!"</p>
        <p>He sent the girl out of the room before he remonstrated. Through the graciousness of his manner there had latterly been an undercurrent of restraint, and he was able to restrain himself still as he raid:</p>
        <p>"I have been hoping against hope that you might remember it was your duty to behave to me differently. But now I think I had better take you at your word and go. If your conscience were to awake it might not be any better. Heaven preserve me from a mere conscientious affection; I don't want any up-hill love. And I have my pro- fession—a profession which I was u fool to sur- render for your sake." She felt the deserved reproach and let the flood- gates of her passion open. It was nOt her fault, she told him, that his presence inspired her with dread. Why, oh why, for the sake of a foolish pastime, which did no one any good and endangered lives for nothing, had he placed this barrier between them ? When the floodgates were once open she could not stay her wrath, but added that, though she would always be faithful to the memory of their love, no law could oblige her to live with him as he was at present. Then she collapsed into one of her attacks of shivering and shaking which were so far physical that it was impossible to reason with her about them. The passion had passed, but it had left a swell behind. "You are not yourself," he said, in a cold, hard tone, as he went out into the garden. "But I make excuses for you. Since I have had a good stare at myself in the looking-glass I begin to understand it. I used not to trouble to look in the glass. I did not believe it mattered so much." Then he smi!ed faintly—an acute obsel,rer might have said a little contemptuously—as he passed out through the French windows. He did 'not look ronnd him. Spring flowers and summer roses would never bloom for him in the old sense again. In the sudden devastation that had come into his life everything seemed to him ragged, rusty and faded. There was a breath of autumn in the air and rain was drizzling, which increased to a soft downfall. He did not even hear the drops pattering down on the yellowing leaves as he devastated the hedges with his stick and stamped/murderously on the toadstools which had taken the place of mushrooms. - -</p>
        <p>When, aboui half an hour afterwards, he was sbewn into my consulting-room, I saw that he, too, was not himself. It was not only the strange new ugliness which, to my eyes, had nothing repulsive about it, but a look of despair in the expression of the face.</p>
        <p>He said: "I shall go abroad immediately and perhaps lead a wandering life for a year or two, and then I may return to my profession, but I shall never come back to this place again." It was of no use to reason with him. He was absolutely determined, as hard as a stone and quite inflexible. He had even made up his mind never to return again to his wife. I was, therefore, much surprised when he burst in upon me on the follow- ing day, not only excited but elated. "Doctor," he said, pointing to a paper which he held in his hand, "read this and tell me what you think of it.." Under other circumstances I should have laughed, for it was a newspaper cutting, purport- ing to give an account. of a new operation performed by an American surgeon who had devoted several years to the delicate work of transforming the ears, mouths, and noses of patients who objected to the features With which Nature had endowed them. The processes by which a turned-up nose was to be made long, or an aquiline one short were described at some length and involved a considerable amount of torture in the cutting of cartilages and sewing together the points of junction. "Ugh!" I said, "why did you bring this stuff to me? There is no saying what quacks May not attempt, but you can't expect a fellow of my age and experience to--'' "Quack or no quack," he exclaimed, "I'm off to-morrow to Chicago. I go to seek the services of that man."</p>
        <p>I looked up in some surprise. "I should have thought you would have more sense," I cried. "What, do You mean to have a nose-Tice composed of two thin, fiat, semi-circular bars of iron, with screws that can be tightened and all the rest, of it applied to your--" Then I ceased joking. The idea might be ridiculous, but I suddenly perceived all that it involved for him. He would do anything, even if it amounted to absurdity, for the winning of that woman's affection. And he had his retort:</p>
        <p>"You said you could do nothing. You English doctors rarely attempt anything original; it generally comes first from abroad." I was more angry than ever with the lady, and did not mince matters when I informed her a few days afterwards: "He has taken you at your word. He has left you for ever." "Where has he gone ? " she asked. And I thought she changed colour a little when I replied, gruffly: "To China or Japan, for aught I can tell you." I did not mean to let her know the exact place, still less that he was going to be vivisected for her sweet sake; but I kept within my directions as I added: "He may remain abroad for some years. Meanwhile you can communicate with him through your lawyer, and I have promised to keep him informed as to the state of your health." "I know you mean to blame me," she said, the great tears welling up into her eyes. "You cannot blame me more than I blame myself. I can assure you I could not possibly help it. Perhaps in a short time I should have grown accustomed to it. He need not have made it years." "We cannot go over the old ground again. I have simply to give my message," I answered, coldly. From that time she lived like a bird that remains in its cage and will not fly out though the door is always open. Evidently she pined in the pretty little nest, though it had been fitted up with express regard for her own style and complexion. When I found her silent and sad I could not pity her, and wondered if she ever remembered her own probing speeches. "Good Heavens!" I used to say to myself, •" to think of any womitn treating a man who loved her like that!" I was anxious and angry about him as the time passed. The operation which he described would be a ticklish one. Erysipelas might be set up by it, and sometimes •I blamed myself for the responsibility I had incurred by not telling him that the proposal was as atrooious as it was absurd. Other matters in my busy life had put it out of my head, when one day, quite unexpectedly, a stranger, as I took him to be, walked into my rooms. I did not recognise him at first, for though he was still a remarlable-looking man he bore very little resemblance to the James Colwyn who had captivated the youthful fancy of Doris Beaumont and had been as beautiful as an Apollo. He was now distinguished-looking, and though he appeared to be many years older than he really was, it was almost impossible to trace any remains of the disfigurement which had so alienated his wife. In</p>
        <p>- ei..r'. A t'' •• ' * OP' • 7 .e.:-:,, 44 *'?'-' ir ..• ....._ • ........,... / 2111 .e \ I\\‘</p>
