      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SIMPLE AUTUMN TOQUE.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE AUTUMN TOQUE.</p>
        <p>which, of course, is not so costly as the former. These underslips should be closely and neatly fitted to the figure and should have the neck cut high or slightly low according to the make of the blouse which covers -them. A thin net slip will often look more dressy if the under- slip is cut a little out at the back and front. Indeed, most of this summer's afternoon blouses were of so dressy a description that they will be found useful for theatre wear or at small social functions all through the winter. The simple autumn toque sketched is made of soft white furry felt with a very deep brim and a rather flat crown. The only 'trimming it boasts is a large bunch of shaded red and green leaves arranged in the form of a chou at the left side. Otherwise, save for a band of dull green velvet -round the crown, the hat is quite plain and depends a good deal for the smartness of its effect on the way it is purt on. This is a knack which every English woman does not possess naturally, although it wpears to be born with French women, but itos one which can be certainly acquired with c..tre and should be studied by ;every girl who sensibly values her good appearance. A hat or tpque, however costly it may be, will not look ' any- thing if badly ;put on. How often one sees a woman /dressed in the height of fashion, her toilette having evidently been the subject of much careful ,thought with no cachet• or style</p>
        <p>about her, the dowdy effect being generally the result of a badly put on hat. In this way also an entirely opposite result may be arrived at by the girl who knows at just what angle to place her hat to give an air of taste and style which affects the whole of her appearance, for, as most of us have experienced, a sinart hat will often carry off a shabby dress. _ _</p>
        <p>My second sketch shows a smart navy blue serge costume trimmed with gold buttons and a very fine gold cord. The latter is almost imperceptible, but somehow, though slight, it lightens the general effect of the toilette in a wonderful manner. The coat is cut on fquite the latest lines, and the skirt, though simple, is altogether fashiona*., Serge is a very useful material at this season of the year as it is light in weight and yet warm. White serge, of course, finds its admirers both summer and winter, and indeed contrasts most pleasingly with all varieties of furs, but it is so easily soiled that most of us consider it too much of a luxury for us to affect. In-some of the shades which approach closely to white it is less easily soiled, and champagne or pretty coloured serge will find many admirers during the ensuing months. Navy blue and red serge though hot so dressy are smart looking and exceedingly durable. Grey is another colour which tever seems to p out of fashion and is very generally becom- mg. It is used to fashion the smart coat illus- trated, a light cloth of a medium shade of</p>
