      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>USEFUL COAT.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL COAT.</p>
        <p>colour being employed. The black velvk t colour contrasts pleasantly with the grey and the cape is not only fashionable, but provides extra protection to the shoulders, chest, and arms. It is not an afternoon coat for visiting such as one so often sees as it is not sufficiently elaborate, but for useful every day wear it would .be found very serviceable. I should advise that one of the light rainproof materials was used in its construction as these can now be obtained in different pretty colours and make the coat a useful substitute for the ordinary less sightly waterproof. The three- quarter coat is not very comfortable wear over a long skirt as if renders the latter most difficult to manipulate, although it is certainly more graceful when the skirt can be permitted to trail, but in wet and showery weather the short sensible skirt will be found preferable as it is all save two or three inches at the hem, protected by the coat from contact with rain or mud. Indeed, a skirt of this style is in- valuable for autumn wear.</p>
        <p>For afternoon tea very dainty little cakes known as "almond kisses" can be made by mixing i lb. of castor sugar, lb. of ground almonds or almonds chopped very fine, half a teaspoonful of essence of bitter almonds, and one beaten egg. Work into a stiff paste, and if necessary add part of another egg. Shape the mixture with the fingers, and bake the tiny cakes in a slow oven for twenty minutes. Do not allow them to get brown, only delicately tinted. By substituting desiccated cocoanut ior almonds you will have a variety of ceke.</p>
        <p>COQUETTE 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>(apyrigZt.)</head>
        <p>(apyrigZt.)</p>
        <p>A TRANSFORMATION.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. J. K. SPENDER,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Death-bed Confession," "An Un- canny Experience," "Raising the Dead," "A Mad Ride," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>I never met with a young woman who cared more for appearances than Doris Beaumont, who was an heiress in a small way and had been spoilt from childhood. Fastidious was not the word for it, and though she had had many offers of marriage, being a general favourite in the county, she was getting on towards thirty before she accepted any of them.</p>
        <p>I, who had been her medical attendant since her babyhood, and knew how quickly she tired of a new doll or a tempting toy, was scarcely surprised when she found some fault with all the young men who worshipped at her shrine.</p>
        <p>She had known me so long that she gave me a good deal of her confidence, and used to say laughingly:</p>
        <p>"It is dreadful to suppose that love can be an illusion which should be in any way destroyed by marriage. Yet it seems to me that sooner or later all illusions are destroyed." "Oh," I answered, laughing at her in return. "You will always manage to find a crack in the china—one man's nose is too dominating, another has a mouth too wide. It is always china, you know."</p>
        <p>For it had been an old joke between us that she would be as valuable as an auctioneer in apprais- ing the value of blue china. "Some women live for china, you know, and you are one of them," had been, I must confess, a hackneyed joke with me. Fond as she was of novels, the conduct and sentiments of an ugly heroine would never have been interesting to her. Everything had to be beautiful; she surrounded herself with lovely things, with pleasant scents, and everything delightful to the senses.</p>
        <p>I confess 1 th-ought it a pity when James Colwyn, who had hitherto neglected women or treated them with a general impartiality of cold- ness, fell vehemently in love with Doris Beaumont, and I thought it a still greater pity when I heard that he had given up his ambitions for her sake. I could scarcely believe it, since when I had seen him but a few montl:s before the object of his existence had seemed to be to eat his way to the Bar, and try to do his best to reach the woolsack. As he was a man of sufficient ability, and had an uncle who was a judge, to say nothing of solicitor friends who 'would be likely to supply him with briefs, this seemed to be a possibility, and I pitied him when I heard that for the sake of pleasing Miss Beaumont he had determined to give up all his prospects and to live upon her property. In fact, he had fallen, like Ulysses' companions might have done, under the spell of the sirens, not having taken the precaution to stop his ears with wax.</p>
        <p>For Doris Beaumont's attitudes were graceful, her voice was musical, and the sweetness of the perfumes in her beautifully decorated rooms were apt to steal in a subtle way through the senses and to take possession of a man almost against his will. When I saw Colwyn a short time afterwards, I doubted how the thin. would end, for I knew that though for the time he might have been yielding to a kind of lassitude •which was not without plea- surable sensations, there was too much latent energy in his disposition for him to be any woman's puppet long. During the beautiful summer evenings which followed their marriage, the roses bloomed abundantly in the cool depths of the fragrant garden, and the stars looked down upon the lovers in the intense silence of the mild, soft, clear nights. For a time Doris was contented with loving her husband and trusting him as she had never before trusted any other man, and then she began to be fastidious as usual. She would ask herself if she were any happier to-day, for instance, than she was yesterday. She had found out that her husband liked turnips and mangold wurzels. better than stained glass and bronzes, and preferred to read Darwin and Carlyle instead of Tennyson or Swinburne.</p>
        <p>He would come in splashed and disreputable from long country walks, and took an interest in beetles and caterpillars and other loathsome crawling things. He did not know a Titian from a Greuze, though he raved a good deal about snow mountains. And he laughed at the time wasted on manicure, refusing to practise it himself, though he still admired his wife's fingers, which were not only beautifully modelled, but slightly roseate :and waxen.</p>
        <p>Already her piece of perfec.tion, her handsome husband, was proving to be of Brummagen china, or like the deceptive blue glass vase for which little Rosamund in Miss Edgeworfh's story had sighed. He who was puzzled by her womanish fancies, tried to attribute these to her health.</p>
        <p>"She weeps about nothing; at this moment she Loves me," he thought, "but to-morrow—a hair's breadth of difference of opinion or taste will separate us ; are all women like that ? " The thraldom was oppressive; he began to kick over the traces. A thin outer sort of society pellicle always adhered to her, and now that it was the season she began to sigh after London. He Longed for mountain climbing. It seemed to him that nothing short of the air of the mountains could blow away the unhealthy vapours which, man- like, he hated. He admitted unwillingly, "I should loathe Hyde Park, kid gloves and tight boots. It's all very well for women who make their nests so pretty, but men like to be out in the air."</p>
        <p>"There is nothing like a little change, a separa- tion for a short time." I admitted from my profes- sional experience. "'You will come back freshly to each other."</p>
        <p>And so it was arranged that she was to spend a few weeks visiting some friends in London, while he joined some bachelor friends in mountain climbing. When they met again she would probably be less disposed to be captious, and he more inclined to humour all her whims.</p>
        <p>The season had been a wet one, and there were more avalanches than usual, so that most unfortunately, after Colwyn and his friends had been for some time at Grindelwald, a catastrophe occurred which entailed serious consequences, though it was scarcely important enough to be even mentioned in the newspapers. I forget whether they were intending to climb the Wetterhorn or the Silberhorn, but anyhow they were overwhelmed by an avalanche. Nobody was killed, and the accident was in some way ludicrous, since the men, who were swathed completely by the snow, were thrown out of it or emerged from it in time to recover their breath. The wonder was that they were not suffocated, though they were carried some hundreds of feet, but it was no question of digging them out, since in the jerk which came at the end of the catastrophe one man's head was jolted, out, and he pulled at the legs of his companions, whose feet were sticking out. All were more or less bruised and bleeding, but Colwyn was the only one who had sustained some injuries. He had been holding up his ice axe when the avalanche caught him and the consequence was that the ice tom had repeatedly come into collision with his face, he grasping it tightly during a period of unconscious- ness.</p>
        <p>His nose had been broken, and his upper lip had been badly cut, whilst from the forehead to the chin the flesh had been torn open and was left jagged and bleeding. "My dear fellow," said one of his companions, "it makes one shudder to look at you. .I am afraid that, little can be done to restore your pristine beauty." .</p>
        <p>Colwyn laughed ; he had cared little about his looks, and at that moment whilst he was congratu- lating himself on his marvellous escape, it did not occur to him to reflect how the alteration in his appearance might affect the happiness of his life. The services of a 'Swiss surgeon were speedily secured, who sewed up the lips, plastered the face, and tried in vain to set .the nose. To do Colwyn justice, he made efforts to be obedient, and even remained some days in bed, but the Swiss doctor always complained that lie found it in vain to insist on his keeping still, and that the tossings to and fro of a man who slept heavily and who felt the heat of the weather, prevented the setting of the nose from being so successful an operation as it might otherwise have been. Meanwhile Mrs. Colwyn, who had been enjoying herself with a round of balls and theatres in London, had returned to their little country house in Devonshire, quite inclined to make herself charming to her husband. But I was sent for in a hurry after he first presented himself. His altered appearance having given her such a shock that she was taken with a violent fit of hysterics. "Don't Ist. him near me." she cried. when enm,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>ract, had it not been for the voice I mignt nave been puzzled about his identity. There was no trace, for instance, of the gash on the nose. It was, perhaps more what is called the Conqueror's nose, the fracture having been so skilfully managed that it was slightly higher in the bridge, giving hint a more dominating if not more haughty,appearance. As to the mouth, it was more difficult for me to judge of the operator's skill, since James, who had before been clean shaven, had now grown a becoming moustache, which happene'd to be several shades lighter than his dark brown hair. But I could see that an ugly Wound at one corner of the lips had now entirely disappeared. It was perhaps owing to the lapse of time that the marks on the cheeks and forehead could no longer be recognised. The man himself seemed to be altered, as if there had been a sort of hiatus in his life,. during which he had reconsidered things and had readjusted them for the better. I remembered that during our last interview, when his whole being had been seething and boiling in a state of fermentation, owing to the wrongs which had been inflicted upon him, I had been obliged to make an appeal: "Dear old fellow, for Heaven's sake don't give way like that," and he had answered weakly, "It's of no use; I am doomed. I Must at any rate go into exile." But nobody would have thought of apostrophis- ing the new-comer with his dignified presence by the name of "Dear old fellow! " or adjuring him not to give way. As soon as we approached, the subject of his wife, I found that he was no longer passive, but inclined to be aggressive. He declared that he had no wish to make himself known at once, that he intended to go to London and see what progress he could make in his profession, and that afterwards, when he had made his mark, it would be time enough to intrude upon her.</p>
        <p>A couple of years had passed away, and Janies Colwyn was beginning to succeed. He had worked day and night, had been fortunate in obtaining a brief, owing to the illness of a junior which he had managed with singular success. He was talked of as one of the rising men, and it was said that he meant to contest the neighbouring borough at the next election. Meanwhile he bad assumed the name of Edward Delmar, the name of his grand- father on the maternal side, and his wife remained in complete ignorance of his reappearance. As time passed, she drooped and lost her health, being attacked when she was in a weak state with inflammation of the lungs, and becoming for a little while seriously ill. When she recovered it was necessary to cut off her beautiful hair. She herself alluded to her loss of complexion and declared that she was prematurely aged when she looked into her mirror and saw that she was pale and faded.</p>
        <p>"It is like a retributive fate, doctor," she said to me one day, and I noticed that her eyes sought the photograph of her husband, a photo which had been taken soon after their marriage, and which had always occupied an important. place on In z bedroom mantelpiece. "But you know I could not help it. If it were all to come over again, I believe I should do the same. It is impossible for me to explain how absolutely he terrified me." After that having once broken the ice, she alluded more constantly to her trouble, asking me if I did not think it odd that neither she nor her lawyer ever heard from him. "Perhaps if he were to come back," she whispered one day in a voice which was weak through her illness, "I might get reconciled. I am older you know, and growing plain myself, and perhaps I should not be so foolish as I was." Her clasped hands trembled as they lay thin and helpless on her lap, and her fascinated eyes wandered again to the photograph of her husband on the mantelpiece. I was fearful of the extreme tension of feeling, and I took the opportunity of saying that she could now pay a visit to her friends in London. "What was the good," I argued, "of her lingering in a house which was like a pretty gilded and decorated cage, when even the birds had their companions." I wrote at the same time to Colwyn and told him that there was now a good opening for him since, for the first time, I imagined that he might successfully carry out his project of getting intro- duced to his wife, and calling upon her as if he were a perfect stranger. Her visit was not a long one. She came hurry- ing home and sent for me at once. I saw when I obeyed her summons that something unusual had happened. She was excited and her eyes glittered, while the colour had returned to her face. -</p>
        <p>"Dr. Carr," she said to me in her trembling voice, "did James ever tell you .that he had any relations? I knew that he had no brothers, but had he, for instance, a cousin? A man not so handsome as he was, but. somehow very striking, with my husband's dark blue eyes, and occa- sionally, now and then, just his tone of voice? I—l thought it was better to come home, for the resemblance was so striking I could hardly bear it, and I don't want to be like a grass widow, junketing about without my husband.' I wit home and wrote a letter to catch that night's post. "Come home; she has fallen in love with ycu over again." [TnE END.]</p>
        <p>THE CORONER'S PROBLEM FOR TEETOTA LER,. Mr. Brighouse, the county coroner, held an inque Widnes on Wednesday, on the body of Catherine Morgan aged thirty-six years, who resided at 41, Travis-street, and whose death occurred during the early hours c!f Sunday morning.—From the evidence of her husband, it appears that she came home drunk at 8-30 on Saturday night, and at 9-30 when witness came in, he had words with her, and then went to bed. She slept on the sofa all night, and at 6-30 in the morning upon corning downstairs, he found her dead on the floor.— William Morley, of 10, Bedford-street, said that be met the deceased on Saturday afternoon, and they went into a house, and had a quantity of drink together. On leaving the house he was so drunk that he fell down in the road,and she had to go home herself.—The Coroner: A case of the blind leading the blind. They talk about publicans, and run them down, but it is not nearly so bad as private house drinking, where people can drink at leisure,and where they are not watched by the landlord and by the police. If they i could stop private house drinking, and let them drink n public-houses it would be much better. This is a problem for teetotalers.— Dr. Lindsay said there were no marks of violence, and death was due to an overdose of alcohol, 'causing asphyxia.—Cozoner : You don't wonder after the quan- tity of drink consumed. I am surprised there are not more cases.—A verdict in accordance with the medical evidence was returned.</p>
      </div>
