      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BECK'S DOUBLE.</head>
        <p>BECK'S DOUBLE.</p>
        <p>After many years, tardy justice has been 40ne, and one of the meanest scoundrels of ,Itodern times in the shape of William "thomas, alias "John Smith" and a score of Other names, the " double " of Mr. Adolf 41 edit has been sentenced to five years penal &amp;ervitude. Brought up • last week at the Central Criminal Court the man, who is a well turned out and decently looking man of gentle- manly exterior, pleaded guilty before Mr. Jus- tice Phillimore to the crimes for which he stood charged in re- pect of defraud- ing Miss Beulah Turner and other girls of various articles of value, and to</p>
        <p>WILLIAM THOMAS, being</p>
        <p>"John</p>
        <p>ALIAS JOHN SMITH. Smith" of the 1877 conviction, b„nf he did not make any statement as to Lu'e Crimes for which Mr. Adolf Beck was Zrongfully convicted in 1896 and 1904. 'lfter pleading. guilty he asked the judge to ..*oint him counsel, but the application was :efused, Mr. Justice Phillimore promising to „take note of any statement he might make. made none, however, and when brought 'I) for sentence on the following day Mr. "\latthews, for the prosecution, made a very ,14rong speech, reviewing the whole case. Mr. ViStice Phillimore, in passing sentence of five ,Jears' penal servitude, said that he did not .I,llsider it sufficiently proved that William vi-I,onias had committed the offences for i,ufch Beck had been wrongly convicted, and at he had only allowed the counsel for the brown to make the statements he had done tecause it was in the nature of a justification Beck, and he did not, therefore, take them Cao account in passing sentence. On every I,nd. however, general surprise has been ex- Tressed at the lightness of the sentence, as a :1" bigger one was expected, and it has been -4ked why should Beck receive seven years in 4(196 and Thomas only five in 1904 for „ten ticall y the same thing, especially in view the fact that the latter has an incontest- w4 previous conviction against him for a uliarly low form of larceny and has been •hcived up to the hilt a dangerous adventurer? q. us the curtain falls on another act in the Beck</p>
        <p>Poidevin, with Cuttell, Brearley, and Ker- mode, under the ever brilliant captaincy of "Archie" Maclaren, who has himself been unfortunate this year, make the backbone of a team worthy of the championship. K. S. Ranjitsinhji heads the batting averages with the splendid average of 74.17 for 2,077 runs, and his captain, C. B. Fry, is not far behind with 70.60 for 2,824, while Tyldesley comes third with 62.53 for 2,439 runs. Next tome the two great Notts batsmen, A. 0. Jones and Iremonger, both averaging sixty; and sixth is Hayward, who has been the main- stay of Surrey and is the only batsman to score over 3;000 runs. His average is 54 for 3,170, and at one time it looked as though he were going to beat the record for aggregates. Next comes Hirst, who has again shown himself the best all 'round cricketer in the world by scoring 2,501 with an average of 54 and taking 132 wickets for 21 runs apiece. J. T. Hearne has taken most wickets (145) and practically tops the bowling with an average of 18, and another Middlesex man to distinguish himself is B. J. T. Bosanquet who has scored 1,405 and taken 132 wickets. J. N. Crawford, of Surrey, who is still a schoolboy, has proved the most promising all round cricketer dis- covered for many years, and has taken just on fifty wickets for less than 17 runs apiece. The South Africans have a splendid record and under the captaincy of J. H. Sinclair have shown themselves an absolutely first class team, especially strong in bowling.</p>
