      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>DEATH OF PRINCE HERBERT VON BISMARCK.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF PRINCE HERBERT VON BISMARCK.</p>
        <p>Prince Bismarck, who expired after several days' unconsciousness, last Sunday at Fried- richsruh, was the eldest son of the "Iron Chancellor," being born at Berlin on the 28th December, 1849. He succeeded his father in the title in July, 1898. In June, 1892, he married in Vienna the Countess Mar- guerite Hoyos, who was born in IB7i, and by whom the late Prince is sur- vived together with three sons and two daughter s. Prince Herbert was one of the first to go to the front when the Franco-German war broke out and received a</p>
        <p>serious</p>
        <p>wound in the thigh in one of the early en-</p>
        <p>THE LATE PRINCE BISMARCK.</p>
        <p>gagemen-ts. He kubse q uen tly held several diplomatic appointments, and in 4883 was First Secretary of the German Embassy in London. His political career in Berlin commenced in 1885, and he soon began to exercise much influence in the Reichstag. In 1886 he was Secretary of State and Asses- sor to his father, the Chancellor, and on the sensational retirement of the famous states- man, Count Herbert, as he then was, was provisionally charged with the direction of foreign affairs. It was not long, however, before he followed his father into private life. Prince Herbert held the lank of Lieutenant- Colonel in the German Army and was a keen sportsman. He frequently visited this country as the guest of Lord Rosebery, with whom he was on the closest terms of friendship.</p>
        <p>$. j • . /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RIOTS AT ASHTON-UNDER-LYNE.</head>
        <p>THE RIOTS AT ASHTON-UNDER-LYNE.</p>
        <p>Last week saw one of the strangest strikes which has ever been known in this country, and seldom has feeling run so high. The scene of the strike has been Ashton-under- Lyne, and the centre Curzon Mill. At this mill only peculiarly fine Egyptian cotton is spun, and consequently, to maintain the quality, the officials decided that it was necessary for the mills to be stopped at certain times of the day, to be cleaned. As the hands work by time, this new rule meant enforced idleness and smaller earnings, and no compensation was offered. Therefore the workpeople came out on strike. The owners decided to fight and imported hands from other. towns. So high ran the feeling in Ashton that it was found necessary that the male workers should be fed and sleep in the mill itself, but there was no room for the women, who for the most part came from</p>
        <p>_ 1 : L._ ' .11 ,_ • -. 1 11 ri rl nll nn , I'd 4 _-,--- ------ 1111, wolf •-tii4,l7-"Yt:-.... II fl !Lil , l 1111 li II "111 -=---, _ .. .... : ft ip ; „irk '.:4° L,Vi 7 , 11:•".• i'ii6i!, t . _ 0'• ''' •-- - . 4 . 04; 7,, e), 4. ~, ~ • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CURZON MILL.</head>
        <p>CURZON MILL.</p>
        <p>Oldham. Therefore they had to be conveyed home nightly. Great crowds gathered round the gate of the mill as the women were brought mit in " armoured " 'buses under an escort of mounted police. Stones and bricka were thrown nightly at the 'buses and at the train, of which the windows were smashed, and on one occasion bags of cayenne pepper were thrown in, which all but blinded the girls and caused them great agony. At Oldham, too, angry crowds awaited them, and many cases of violent assault took place. The, police were forced to resort to all sorts of devices to get .the " blacklegs " safely home, and the trains from Ashton to Oldham had to be run by circuitous routes. Last Saturday the Mayor of Ashton offered his good offices, and it is hoped that a settle- ment will soon be arrived at in this fierce and lamentable dispute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>REMARKABLE INVENTION.</head>
        <p>A REMARKABLE INVENTION.</p>
        <p>A wonderful invention is at the present moment being tested, and its success will not only create a revolution in modern naval warfare, but will reveal all the hidden riches of the sea. Cavaliere Pinto, an Italian, has invented a submarine telescope, which he calls a hydroscope, with which he can see into the</p>
        <p>depths of the sea to the very bottom as easily</p>
        <p>\-IS/ Ntil /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>as a man. can</head>
        <p>as a man. can</p>
        <p>lOolc-through an ordinary t ele- scope on land. He has further invented a sys- tem of 'raising</p>
        <p>sunken ships by compressed air,</p>
        <p>and he is .ex-</p>
        <p>perimenting with the two CAVALIERE PINTO.</p>
        <p>together in Vigo Bay. In that bay in 1702 there sank a Spanish fleet of galleons loaded with treasure to the corn- puted value of close upon thirty millions. He has located a number of these galleons, and proposes, under an arrangement with the Spanish Government, to raise them and re- cover this in mense fortune. If he can do so successfully it opens an enormous field before him. Billions of pounds' worth of property lies buried under the sea, in addition to such considerations as pearls, coral, guano, and the very shoals of fish themselves. At Port Arthur alone several million pounds' worth of Russian men-of-war have been sunk dur- ing the present war; and in many other places the approximate position of sunken treasure is known. This water telescope and the compressed-air raising apparatus should, if successful, yield to the inventor a fortune vast beyond computation. Apart from this comes the question of locating submarines asi easily as though they were above the water- line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 245 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 245 1904.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>FATHER: "Tommy, stop pulling that cat's tali" Tommy: "I'm only holding the tail; the cat's pulling it."</p>
        <p>" HIRE's an account of a girl who climbed to the top of Snowdon and sang Rule Britannia." "She had some sense, hadn't she? It's too bad, other girls are 'not so thoughtful when they want to sing." Box VIVANT who had consumed seven bottles : "How many have I had ? " Landlord to lady in a whisper: "Tell him to count them himself; he'll pay for fourteen,"</p>
        <p>'OCSTOMER : "Waiter, I cannot eat this par- tridge ; bring me some veal cutlets." Waiter: "Very sorry, sir, veal cutlets is—off, sir.'? Cus- tomer : "So is this partridge. Very much so." SCARED.—" Have you caught that murderer yet ? " "No," replied the detective. "We haven't caught him, but we've got him so scared he doesn't dare show himself when we're around."</p>
        <p>"Miss VAN SLocum has a wonderful eye for colour." "Has she? What is her other eye for ? "</p>
        <p>JACK: "I'll never ask another woman to marry me as long. as I live." Jim: "Refused again? " Jack: "No, accepted."</p>
        <p>_ KIND OLD GEN4EMAN : "Why are you crying like that, my boy ? " Boy: "'Cause it's the only way I know how.'</p>
        <p>MASTER: "Now, Robert, what is the shortest day in the year and what is the longest ?" Robert: " The last day.of the term and the first day after the holidays, sir." 'WHAT do you consider the most absolutely certain way to distinguish toadstools from mush- rooms ?" Cook them, get some other man to cat them, and then watch his symptoms." ANTENUPTIAN ANTICS.—" Do you think they are married ? " No, they are only engaged. She looked pleased when he burned her hand with his lighted cigar." _ _ _</p>
        <p>DR. PILLS T "Who was the most successful of all those girls who were studying medicine with you ?'? Dr. Squills: "Miss Kitchem. She got married."</p>
        <p>MAMMA : "Where's papa? Flossie: "He's downstairs." Mamma: "What's he doing?" Flossie : "His bicycle is out of breaff, and he's giving it some more." OLD BULLION: " What ? You wish to marry my daughter? She is a mere schoolgirl yet. Suitor: "Yes, sir. I came early to avoid the rush."</p>
        <p>A GIRL never believes a man when he tells her he isn't worthy of her love, but before she has been his wife for a year she discovers that he has told her the truth.</p>
        <p>WIFE: "I wonder how you can look me in the face." Husband: "Oh, a man can get used to anything." THAT SETTLED IT.—Jaggers : "So he married the widow! I thought he had his eye on the daughter." Waggles: "So he had, but the widow had her eye on him." CONSOLATORY.—Dressmaker: "So you are not satisfied with the dress, madam. I fear, then, I shall have no more of your work?" Customer: " No ; but I'll recommend you to my friends." "I DON'T know what I want," said the dyspeptic guest, looking at the bill of fare. "I can't eat more than about two bites, anyway." "You might try a couple of our mutton chops, sir," suggested the dignified waiter unbending slightly. THE good lady scrutinised him closely. "Didn't give you a whole meat-pie a day or two ago," she inquired in icy tones. Yes, mum, replied the tramp, "I'm the same party, but I've recovered, and if you make it plain bread and butter this time I'll be much obliged." "AND are you quite sure that you wouldn't mind me as a mother-in-law ? " said Mrs. Sterling- worth, archly, when Mr. Giddings asked . her for her daughter's hand. "My dear Mrs. Sterling- worth,2' the young man replied, "it was for :the very .purpose of acquiring you as a mother-in-law that I fell in love with your charming daughter." A MILLIONAIRE, who was looking at a level tract of land which he had just bought at an extravagant price; said to the agent who sold it to him: "I do admire a rich green flat." "So do I," significantly replied the agent.</p>
        <p>WHY HE ASKED Fon IT.—Seedy Applicant: "Won't you giN'Te me a ha'penny, sir ?" Corpu- lent Gent! "A ha'penny. Why ch you ask me for a ha'penny ? " Speedy Applicant: "Because I didn't think you'd give it me unless I asked for it."</p>
        <p>MISTRESS (engaging cook) "There's one thing I'm very particnlar about. I cannot on any ac- count allow you to entertain policemen in the kitchen." Cook: "You may be quite shore I wouldn't do sich a thing, mum. I can't a-bear p'leecemen, mum. Father was a Sosherlist !" SUBBUBS : I'd like to own a steam yacht and take a six months' cruse round the Atlantic. Howse: Any particular object in view? Subbu.bs : Yes I'd like to show the cook that she couldn't leave us when she pleased. "WITNESS," said the cross-examining lawyer, "are you willing to swear that the prisoner was smoking a pipe at the time ? " "No, sir," replied the witness, I never swear. But lam willing to bet you five pounds to a shilling that he was." CAPTAIN s(to Plashinsky, who thinks the vessel is going down).—" Brace up! Brace up, and die like a man! Plashinsky : "It aind't dot I'm afraid to die, captain; but choost before der voyage I paid me a hundret pounts for a plot in der cemetery, and now, choost to clink, I can't use it. Oh, it vos awful, awful." DIRECTORY CANVASSER: "What is your first name, Mr. Peck ? " Mr. Peck "It was Claude before I got married." Directory Canvasser: "Am I to understand that marriage altered your name?" Mr. Peck: "N -no, but r spell it Clawed now."</p>
        <p>"How long," inquired the Eastern potentate, "has the young man been on the treadmill ? " "Two weeks, 0 conquering King! And he told me yesterday that he was having a fine time, although the scenery was getting monotonous." "Two weeks? Great Allah! Who is he?" "He claims he is a bicycle scorcher; but what that might be I know not." A WELL-KNOWN professor had taken his watch from his pocket to mark the time he intended to boil an egg for his breakfast, when a friend enter- ing the room found him absorbed in some abstruse calculation, with the egg in his hand, upon which he was intently looking, and the watch supplying its place in the saucepan of boiling water, THE PROSPECT.—" I am afraid, .madam," said a gentleman who was looking for apartments, "this house is too near the station to be pleasant." "It is a little noisy," said the landlady, "but from the front verandah one has such a fine view of people who miss the train," she added, with an air of triumph. MoTnEr. : "Where were you during that thunderstorm ?" Freddie: " Over in that field with the big tree in it." Mother: "But I have told you distinctly many, many times never to stand under a tree during a thunderstorm." Freddie: "I didn't. I sat down." _</p>
        <p>LITTLE JAMES had been imparting to the minister the important and cheerful information that his father had cot a new set of false teeth. "Indeed,- James?" replied the minister indul- gently, "and what will he do with the old set?" " Oh, I s'pose," replied Little James, "they'll cut 'em down, and make me wear 'em.". .</p>
        <p>SARAH ANN from the country: Could yew tell me what be the meanin' of that whoppin' big D in that winder? Cockney; Decidedly. That Dis- played D Denotes that the Despairing Domestics of that Detached Domicile Desire that the Distin- guished Dustman, During his Daily Diversions, will Deem it his Delightful Duty to Deliberately Dislodge the Dirty Dust Deposited in their Disgust- ing Dustholes.. Sarah Ann: Lor ! HOTEL PROPRIETOR to guest at breakfast: "Did you enjoy the flute playing in the room next to you last. night? " Guest (savagely) : "Enjoy it? I should think not. I spent half the night pound- ing on the wall for the, fool to stop." Proprietor: "Why, Puffer told me that he played over every piece he knew four times, and the person in the next room applauded everyone." A LITTLE fellow had his picture taken, and when the family got the proof sent on approval they were horrified to se the face of the boy all puffed out arid the eyes bulging, and exclaimed in a body: "Why, what on earth did you do to make your face look like that ? " "Didn't (4o a thing," said the innocent little fellow, "but just see if I could hold my breath until the man finished takin' niv picture."</p>
        <p>At the inquest at Bexley Heath, yesterday, on the body of Frederick Henry Cresford, age nine, shot by his father while rabbiting, a verdict of death from misad- venture was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>15 BUNS FOR A ID.</head>
        <p>15 BUNS FOR A ID.</p>
        <p>You can make 15 delicious Almond, Lemon, or Vanilla Buns from a id. pkt. of Eiffel Tower Bun Flour for a total cost of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE CLUB M.A.lN.—Considerahle interest centred in this main which was decided on Saturday in grand weather. The following were the winners :—D. Rawling, set of bowls and bag; W. Kerfoot, silver- mounted umbrella; %V. W. Westwell. copper kettle; J. Genders, two bottles of wine; B. Leigh, bottle of spirits; A. Bailie, pipe and pouch; F. Ankland, walkin' stick; J. Gittens, bottle of wine. The prizes were pre- sented to the successful players by Councillor Roberts, to whom a hearty vote of thanks was passed, on the motion of Mr. D. Rawling' seconded by Ar. Aukland. Mr. Westwell proposed, and the Secretary seconded a resolution to the effect that the best thanks of the Bowling Green Committee and the players be accorded to the donors of prizes and subscribers to the prize fund. The motion was carried. A hearty vote of thanks was also passed to the Secretary for his services during the past season, which has been one of the most successful experienced by the club. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>_ Oa Sturdy the [nun in connection with theWarrmg- ton and District League, which was commenced a week ago, was continued on the green of the Warrington Cricket Club. The following are the scores :</p>
        <p>SECOND ROUND. J. Lockett, 2 . w.o. J. Allison, Sc alas. H. Daintith, so l5 J. Hill, 2 . l4 J. Swanton, 1 l5 J. Sudlow, 5 l4 F. Millar, 3 J. Houghton, 1 W. Holloway, 4 l5 J. Smith, 3 W. Jones, 4 NV. Swanton, 1 . J Roberts. l, J. Forster, 3 W. Daintith, 3 H. Perry, 5 W. Mees, 2 J. Bowyer, so J. NV. Worsley, 1 J. T. Boardman, 3 l5 J. Massey, 2: . . l4 T. Hackett, so l5 A. Shelmerdine, i T. O'Brien,l J. Jackson,2 l5 C Roberts, s^ 1 E. A. Sanders, 3 l5 .Tos, Jolley, 4 J. Davies, 2.... l5 T. Jones. 4 J. Hall, 1 S. Davies, 5 l5 A Taylor, 5 9 J. Wrench, 2 F. T. Jefferson, so l5 J. Higham, 2 H. Williams, so J. Simister 3 J. Unsxvorth, 1 J. W. Jolley, so. l5 W. Simister, " l4 J. E. Jefferson, 1 ...... ... 15 G. Ball, so ll NV. Eyes, 2 . 15 W. Randles, 1 M. Tobin, 2 l5 H. Rowles, 2 W. Roughley, 5 G. Simcock, 5 l5 A Greenhalgh, so T. Rigby, 2 l5 NV. Carter, 2 J. Weston. 2 . 15, J. Randles, 2 R. Baker, 3 A. Scott;l l5 F. Rathbone, 5 A. Shaw, owes 1 l5 R. Young 3 H. Fogg, 1 w.o. J. Wareham 5 'abs. J. W00d,5 l5 J. 'Hardman. 2 l4 Jos. Harrison 1 J. Ditchfleld, 3 l5 S Clare, 9 6</p>
        <p>THIRD ROUND. W. Eyes F. Forster J.Wrench l5 J. Davies W. Stephenson Jos. Harrison l5 G. Simcock 8 A. Shaw T. Hackett H. Fogg W. Mees THE BLACKPOOL TOURNAMENT.</p>
        <p>There have been a number of local players at the Blackpool tournament, the entries for which closed on Monday. The competitors number 576 against 701 last year, but this time it has been thought better to con- fine the entries within smaller limits in order to get the tournament over a little earlier.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Fair Turner, Warrington, was defeated by J. Taylor, Darwen, 24-11. W. Fairhurst, Ice, triumphed over R. Davies, Golborne, 21.44 while J. Lythcoe, Atherton, beat R. Clarke,Hindley, by a similar margin. I. Mitchell, Northenden, got the better of T. Harden, South Shore, by 2P7, while R. Davies, Newton- le-Willows, had hard lines in being knocked out of the competition by R. W. Hornby by a margin of four, as did G. Chapman, Newton-le-Willows, in being defeated bythree only by W. Hallworth, Hazel Grove.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>"COULD NOT EAT PROPERLY."</head>
        <p>"COULD NOT EAT PROPERLY."</p>
        <p>Appetite Restored : Indigestion Cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>Lost appetite means bad digestion. The proof that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People cure indi- gestion in the rational way is that they improve the appetite from, the first. Mrs. McNulty, 5, Ray-street, Wigan, said: "I often fainted from sheer weakness. In addition to Indigestion, I was a victim to Ana3mia, and had acute spasms round the heart. Every morning I felt as though I had not the energy to rise. My work became a burden. I could not eat properly. Although I went to the most skilful doctor I knew of, if was not ordinary medicine I wanted. I needed new blood. I heard of remarkahle cures by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. When I had tried them [felt at once a change for the better. The dizzy feelings and fainting fits disappeared, and gradually I got back strength and perfect health." Ordinary medicine failed to Cu. e, because ordinhry medicine does not make newblood. Dr. Williams'Pink Pills have, in the same way, cured Anwmia (which is simply bloodlessness), Consumption and Decline, as well as Rheumatism, Paralysis, Gout, Sciatica, and the ailments which women endure in silence. The genuine pills bear Dr. Williams' name, and substitutes should be avoided, as they never cured anyone. Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, London, will send abox post free for 2s. 9d., or six for 13s. 9d., but they can be hadat all medicine shops, if purchasers insist on having the genuine. Sufferers are invited to write to Dr. Williams' correspondence department, 46, Holborn- viaduct, London, with description of symptoms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Science in Relation to Sin.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews preached to a large congregation at the Latchford Baptist Church on Sunday evening from the text Luke xviii. 13: " Gou be merciful to me a sinner." In the course of the sermon the rev, gentleman referred to the emphasis which the doctrine of evolution gives to the idea of heredity. He quoted Clodd, Renan, Davison, and other writers by way of illustrating his thesis. Mr. Matthews further pointed out that science knew nothing of the forgive- ness of sins. At this point the "Revelation of God in Christ reconciling the world unto Himself," came up and secared for man what he was incapable of achieving for himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was an average attendance at the meeting of this society on Sunday, when the prizes awarded for good attendance in accordance with the book scheme were distributed. The chairman was Mr. Ault, of Great Sankey, who spoke upon the brotherliness of the P.S.A. movement. He asked the members to re-consecrate themselves to the work of the P.S.A. and God's blessing would rest upon them.—Hr. Strang. the secretary of a large P.S.A. Society at Wavertree,delivered the addres He based his remarks on the text "Let no corrupt com- munication proceed out of your mouth." He spoke upon the prevalence of bad language in almost every walk of life, and exhorted his hearers, for the sake of the chil- dren around them, to refrain from using bad language. Every member exerted an influence in the sphere in which he moved, and he asked them to let that influence be for good and not for evil.—Hr. B. Farrar (hon secretary) reported that nine new members had .been enrolled. The society sent fraternal greetings to the members of the Wavertree society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</head>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</p>
        <p>The Pastor (the Rev. H. Dunnico) again occupied the pulpit at the above Church on Sunday last, after an absence of several weeks. The church was filled at the evening service, when Mr. Minnie° discoursed upon the very interesting subject "General Booth and the Salvation Army," taking as his motto the words, "They overcame him by the Blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony, and they loved not their lives unto the death." After speaking of the many organiza- tions and the widespread influence at the present day of the Salvation Army, which 30 years ago was not even in existence, the. preacher pointed out some of the secrets of its great spiritual success. First, it proclaimed the Gospel of Jesus Christ and His sacrificial life and death as the one all-sufficient remedy for the ills of a dying world. Secondly, it believed intensely in thp presence and pow3r of the Holy Ghost. Education and other qualifications were often lacking from its ranks, but it was clearly not men of culture who were most needed but men who were baptized with the Holy Ghost. Thirdly, the consecration and absolute surrender of life to God. There was a great deal of difference, said the preacher, between Christians and consecrated Christians, and in closing urged his hearers to consecrate their lives to I Jesus Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HULL.</p>
        <p>Warrington's engagement on Saturday took them to Hull in opposition to the town team, where a crowd of about 8,000 assembled to welcome the popular Lancas- trians. A fair number of supporters of the Warrington club took advantage of the fine day and the Great Central excursion to accompany the team,uut their hearts were not gladdened by the anticipated victory. Mr. F. Kennedy was the referee and the teams lined out as follows :</p>
        <p>Warrington: Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dicken- son and Kenyon, threequarter backs; Bate and Hocken- ' hall, half backs; Boardman, Belton, Cook, Shugars, Harmer, Lunt, Thomas and Jolley, forwards. Hull: Taylor, back; Hall, Cook, Goodfellow and Jackson, three-quarter backs; Goddard and Salt, half- backs; J. Ritson, J. Lewis, G. H. Lewis, Langhorn, Harrison, Kilburn, Hufton and Wade, forwards.' Dickenson started a game of eighty minutes duration with the sun at his back. There was no response to the initial kick, and Bate and Hockenhall were early in evidence in opening out the game, so that the Tykes had a warm time of it. Dickenson, Isherwood and Kenyon in turn failed to grasp openings, and realizing the seriousness of their position the Hull forwards rushed a pack and Taylor eventually kicked to Hallam, who was not over safe in his reply. Once they had the sphere into neutral quarters the home forwards made further progress until they were checked by Hockenball and Belton and Cook led the Warrington forwards back to their original position. During some exchanges between Taylor and Hallam, Thomas charged a kick by the home back, and matters looked ominous until Taylor smartly recovered, and cleared under diffi- culties. Warrington maintained the attack for some time, but the efforts of the backs to break through were upset by the sterling tackling of the Hull threequarter line. Fish and Kenyon were pulled up in turn, and by degrees the Tykes again worked their way to centre field. Here Fish was to the fore with capital defensive play, and on one 'occasion up-set a well-laid plan by Goddard when he kicked across to give Cook and Jack- son an opening. A similar movement was well checked by Hallam, and for some time the Lancashire men had their work cut out to keep their opponents at bay. Hull's pressure was persistent, but the defence was impregnable, and too sound to admit of a score. A sudden breakaway by the Warrington forwards, the leaders being Shugars, Belton and*Board- man, was dealt with by Taylor, and in retaliating the Yorkshiremen again forced their way to the Warrington line. Hallam cleared' well, but a moment later the visitors were penalized, and Jackson essayed a long shot at goal. It was a gallant attempt for a premier point, but better luck attended the kicker a minute later, for after Lewis had placed his side in a favourable position the Warringtonians were penalized in a good position, and cheers greeted Jackson when he opened the afternoon's scoring. Warring- ton responded well from the kick, and taking a strong attacking position, Bate tricked the opposition in splendid style, and serving Fish at the right moment the Warrington crack soon showed Taylor a clean pair of heels, and concluded a brilliant effort, by placing the ball behind the posts. The scorer was loudly applauded and these were renewed when he landed the goal. Both siees entered into the fray with grim deterraination,and for a period exciting play was witnessed at midfield. The home forwards were energetic, and at this period were showing a superiority over the visiting eight. The Hull backs took full advantage of their position, and Hall dribbled over and led up to the scores being made equal. Warrington appeared unable to cope with the vigorous onslaught of the home forwards, and another round of passing near the line led up to Hall scoring a second try. The point was not improved upon,and taking up the attack from the re-start from centre Fish made a great effort to put Warrington on equal terms,superior numbers proving his downfall. Half-time: Hull, one goal two tries (eight points); Warrington, one goal one try (five points), -Resuming, Warrington forced the pace to such a degree that Hull were immediately com- pelled to take up a defending position. Fish and Dickenson were both within an ace of scoring, and the former in particular gave Taylor considerable trouble. The Hull custodian, however, continued to play a masterly game, and extricated his side out of difficult positions, and by degrees the Hull forwards changed the venue. The respite was only momentary, for another movement by the Warrington half backs changed the positions, and Fish placed a penalty goal. With only a point between the sides they both entered the fray with renewed vigour, but the' defence was always a shade superior to the attack, and Dickenson had hard luck in being pulled up near the line. Near the close Fish tried at goal and hit the cross-bar. Warrington pressed con- tinuously, but nothing accrued, and the final score was: Hull, one goal two tries (eight points) ; Warrington wo goals one try (seven points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LEEDS v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>LEEDS v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Played at Headingley, before 8,000 persons. In the first half play was fairly even, though Hughes scored a try for Widnes. In the second half Leeds carried all before them, and tries were scored by Taylor,Llewellyn and Ward, one of which was improved by Mosley. Littlewood kicked a penalty. Hughes scored for Widnes. Result—Leeds, 2 goals 3 tries; Widnes, 1 goal, 2 tries. SWINTON v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At Swinton, before 2,000 spectators. Lewis opened the scoring with au unimproved try for Swinton. St. Helens responding with two goals, 'Jut on by Barnes and Edmonds. In the second half Swinton had matters pretty much their own way, ;Simister, Gartrell, and Albert Valentine each scoring a try, but no goals accrued. Barnes scored for the visitors. Result : Swinton, 17 points; St. Helens, 7 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>RUNCORN V. FIUNSLET.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN V. FIUNSLET.</p>
        <p>Played at Runcorn, before 1000 spectators. Jolley was unable to play for Runcorn, Walker taking his place. Turton scored a splendid try. On turning round Huns- let attacked, and A. Goldthorpe placed two goals. Smith added a try. Burnett and Butterworth were ordered off for fighting. Result—Hunslet, two goals 1 try; Runcorn, 2 tries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WIGAN v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</head>
        <p>WIGAN v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>At Central Park, Wien, before 10,000 spectators Play was fast in the initial half, and Winpenny was al- most over. Carmichael just missed a penalty goal. Wigan attacked vigorously, Voiceyhavi.ng hard lines on several occasions. Hector eventually scored after a fine breakaway by Anderson, no goal resulting. Windsor Jones added another try, Mason failing. Result— Wigan, 5 points; Kingston Rovers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>OLDHAM V. BATLEY.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM V. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>At Oldham, before 6,000 spectators. The first half was fast notwithstanding the heat. Oldham had the best of play, and led by 12 points to 2at the interval. Ferguson kicked four goals and Cash and Spottiswoode scored tries. Hai ns worth kicked two goals for Batley. Play in the second half was rather uninter- esting. Result—Oldham, 17 points; Batley, 4 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>BRADFORD v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>At Park-avenue, Bradford, in grand weather. Brad- ford lost the toss, but playing against the wind had all the best of matters. Eagers dropped a clever goal, and two fine tries were scored by Dechan, Bradford leading by 9 points to nil at the interval. On resuming Dechan scored for Bradford and Lomas for Salford. The home side pressed at the close. Result—Bradford, 1 goal 3 tries (11 points) ; Salford, 1 try, (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>HALIFAX v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</head>
        <p>HALIFAX v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</p>
        <p>At Hanson-lane,Halifax, before 9,000 spectators. The visitors kicked oft, Williams replying. Little kicked a splendid penalty goal. Wakefield equalised quickly with a smart dropped goal. Hartley scored an unconverted try aftergood passing.Aftc the interval Halifax played with more dash, Williams running strongly. Bartle chargeddown a kick, and scored a second try, Little failing. Halifax added five more points before the close Bennett played well foe Wakefield. Result—Halifax,l3 points; Wakefield, 2 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" v. PEMBERTON.</p>
        <p>Sonn capital play was witnessed on the Wilderspool groun I on Saturday when .Warrington "A" entertained Pemb nton. Considerable interest centred in the first appear tnce in the home ranks of Davies, the Penycraig half-b Lek, and a young player named Barnes was given a trial in the three-quarter line. Winning the toss, Pemberton played with the breeze during the first half, and for a time placed Warrington on the defensive. Anderton landed a goal from a penalty.</p>
        <p>This reverse acted as a tonic, and the home team attacked vigorously. Savage and Watkins had hard luck in being held up on the line, and . .</p>
        <p>eventually Knight placed a go. War.rington continued on the aggressive and Davies securing froma scrimmage bluffed the opposing halves and scored in a good position. Knight added the extra points. Half-time arrived with the score: Warrington, two goals one try .(seven points) : Pemberton, one goal (two points). Dunng the second half the play was all in Warrington's favour, and Foster and Brooks scored in fine style. Final: Warrington, 13 points; Pemberton, 2 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Fels-Naptha</head>
        <p>Fels-Naptha</p>
        <p>A fact is like a stone wall; a pretty hard thing to butt your head against. Go by the book. If you don't, it isn't the fact that gets hurt.,</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This authority held its fortnightly meeting at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. S. Woodward presiding. There were also present :—Messrs. G. Pilling, (vice chairman),T. Wright,C. E. Linaker,T. Baxter,J. Hallows, C. Roylance, J. Spruce, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. Bolshaw,H. Eyes,J.Whitlow,H. M. Davies,ll. Mawdsley, E. Bretherton, J. Fryer, William Morris, T. F. Lea, J. Whitley, R. Billington, J. Mounfield, G. F. Ashton (clerk), A. S. Hulse, (acting deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>A_ COMPLAINT FROM GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>A_ COMPLAINT FROM GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Grapnenhall Parish Council wrote calling the attention of the Rural District Council to the rough slate of Bellhouse-lane and asked that the Council should instruct the Surveyor to attend to the matter at an early date. He enclosed a letter of com- plaint, which had been forwarded to his Council. as follows: "We beg to bring to your notice the state of the footpath leading from the cottages in front of the locks to the Bridge which crosses the roadway and Canal. Last week two of the tenants of the said cottages had to remove clinkers, large and dangerous from this path, refilling the holes caused by their removal with cinders Other clinkers are still projecting and are a source of great danker especially to children. The whole of the footpath is in a sadly neglected condition, grass grow- ing out from the fence two or three feet over the path rendering it impassable, especially on wet days. We trust that your Council will take immediate steps to bring the matter before the Rural District Council and that the long standing grievance may be speedily remedied."</p>
        <p>The communication was signed by Messrs. F. Cald- well, G. E. Rigby, N. Seabury, T. McCann, F. Jones, NV. Watson and NV. Humphreys. The Surveyor said he had inspected Bellhouse-lane and found very little cause for complaint. He would, however, have small patches of macadam placed on the road. Steps would be taken to improve the state of the footpath near the locks.</p>
        <p>HEALTH OF TIIE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported three ca.,es of diphtheria at Preston Brook and one at Weston ; four cases of scarlet fever at Grappenhall and four at Stockton Heath ; and one cas of typhoid fever at Halton. He remarked that Warrington was very full of scarlet fever and he thought that accounted for a fair amount of disease in the neigh- bourhood.The ease of typhoid was the first he had had to report for over a year. Complaints had reached him with reference to the emanations from a slaughter- house at Stockton Heath. A child had died at Lumb Brook and a rumour was current that very offensive smells arose from the brook which ran past the house. He had visited the place and found the brook perfectly clear. He cross-examined the neighbours, and there was a consensus of opinion that a very offensive smell had been prevalent at certain times. He inspected the slaughter-house in question and from what he saw he recommended that Mr. Farrington should be instructed to examine fully and report as to the status and sanitary condition of the building. This course was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>TUBERCULOSIS IN CATTLE,</head>
        <p>TUBERCULOSIS IN CATTLE,</p>
        <p>A long letter on this sutject was received from the Local Government Board urging the desirability of uniformity in the practice of inspectors in dealing with carcases. It was recommended that inspectors should act upon the lines laid down by the recent Select Com- mittee as to tho degree of tubercular disease that was considered sufficient to condemn carcases.</p>
        <p>AN INCREASED CALL.-NOT THE " POPULAR DONKEY</p>
        <p>THIS TIME.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported tl-iWt--th-e-PrObable expenditure for the half-year ending Lady Day, 1905, was £5,459, made up as follows :—General expenses (public health), £807; highways expenses, £3,37; and balance of con- tingency, £1,295. Deducting the probable balance at Michmlmas, 1901. of £l,OOB, the total amount required from the overseers was £4,451. A rate of 61d. in the on the assessable value produced £4,407, and it was recommended that a call for £4,451, an increase of a penny in the £ on the call for the corresponding period of last year, be made upon the overseers. The amount required for each parish was as follows :—Acton Grange, £100; Alvanley, £43 ; Antrobus' £67; Appleton, £170; As:on, £123 ; Aston Grange. £l2 ; Bartington, £18; Budworth, £55 ; Clifton, £9B ; Crowley, £37; Daresbury, £34; Dutton, £2Ol ; Frodsham, £345 ; Frodsham Lordship, £228; Grappenhall, £204; Fialton, £132; Hatton, £39; Helsby, £270; Keckwick, £92; Kingsley, £149; Kingwood, £4O; Latchford Without, £74; Manley, £42; Moore, £125; Newton-bv-Daresbury, £33; Newton-by-Frodsham, £2l; Norley, £9O ; Norton, £157; Preston-o'th-Hill, £95 ; Seven Oaks, £2l; Stockham, £l6; Stockton Heath. £325; Stretton, £54 ; Sutton, £163; Thelwall, £134 ; Walton Superior, £5B; Weaton, £356 ; Whitley Inferior, £l3 : Whitley Superior, £47. The Chairman: Is education the explanation of this? I believe that is the popular donkey which carries all these burdens. The Clerk: Not in this case. __</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitley wished to know whether the additional expenditure was necessitated by the erection of new schools or the increase of officials' salaries.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: lam afraid I must apologise to Mr. Whitley. It was a humorous remark. I meant that the popular educational donkey was carrying the burden. The state of our balances accounts for this. We have had to levy the increased call to bring up our balances to a satisfactory state. Mr. Whitley: It is a very serious matter. Our rates will have to go up threepence in the £ at Stockton Heath. Voices: You won't be alone.</p>
        <p>The estimates were passed. THE FINANCES. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright reported that there was a balance in hand at the last meeting of £209 18s. 9d. The receipts dur- ing the fortnight totalled £949 12s. 11d, and the cheques drawn that day £241 Os. 6d., leaving a balance of £9lB 11s. 2d. There was a cheque of £5OO to Messrs. E. Marshall and Sons on account of the new isolation hospital building. The townshins which had not yet paid the calls were Frodsham, Grappenhall, and Keck- wick. At the last meeting he reported that Newton by Daresbury was a defaulting parish, whereas it should have been Newton-by-Frodsham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ST. HELENS SENSATION. The Second Body Exhumed. RESULT OF THE ANALYSIS.</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS SENSATION. The Second Body Exhumed. RESULT OF THE ANALYSIS.</p>
        <p>Chief-Constable Wood, of St. Helens, has received a communication from Mr. Herbert E. Davies, of Liver- pool, the analyst engaged in the alleged poisoning case, and it is stated that this communication is to the effect that practically no definite traces of arsenic or other poison have been found in the remains of the child Joseph. It will be remembered that the child was only three months old when he died on September 7th, 1900. Almost exactly four years afterwards, viz., on the 6th of September last, the child's body was disinterred from the grave in the St. Helens cemetery, where it had been buried along with the bodies of twenty-two other children under one year old. The coffin was in a remarkably good state of preservation, notwithstanding the four years that had passed, but when it was opened it was found to contain nothing but a few dry bones, and it became necessary for Mr. Davies, the analyst, who was present at the exhumation, to have the coffin and everything taken to his laboratory in order that he might, by all the scientific means at his disposal, endeavour to make the dry bones speak, and, if they had anything to tell, that they should contribute to the evidence in the case which has moved the district so pro- foundly. The presence of any poisonous compound in the body of a child so young and harmless would have been most startling evidence, and would have thrown a terrible light on its young life, but, happily for the good name of the family, this has not proved to be the case. Joseph Burndred was born in June, 1900, and, so far as can be ascertained, a doctor was called in to see him when he was about two months old. The mother said she was afraid the child was going to die, but the doctor said he could find nothing wrong with him, and for some reason or other the mother changed her doctor , and the child was buried on the certificate of Dr. Jackson, that he had died from inflammation of the , stomach.</p>
        <p>When the jury meet next week they will be told what steps the analyst has taken in the matter, and the coroner will no doubt formulate some formal verdict, upon which the remains will be again laid in the cemetery. The county coroner has, it is further stated, decided that he will not order the exhumation of any of the other twelve children that the Burndreds have buried, but he will report the whole of the circumstances to the Home Oface,and leave the Home Secretary to decide whether the matter shall be gone into any further in this direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ANOTHER REAAND.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER REAAND.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Burndred were again brought before the magistrates on Tuesday, but the proceedings scarcely lasted a minute. Colonel J. W. Myrear was chairman, along with Ur. J. Crook. Mr. W. H. Andrew (town clerk) appeared to prosecute, and Mr. P. C. Kelly. who succeeds Mr. H. L. Riley, defended. Mr. E. W. Swift was also present, watching the case on behalf of the guardians, who boarded the girl Jones out with the Burndreds.</p>
        <p>Mr. Andrew said :—ln this case, your worships, you will remember that the prisoners were remanded until to-day, and I have t 6 ask if you will kindly remand them for another eight days. Further inquiries have been made, but we have not yet received the detailed result of the analyst's investigations, nor has the coroner's inquiry been finished. Mr. Kelly said: I appear on behalf of the prisoners, and Ido not propose to oppose the applica- tion.</p>
        <p>- The Chief Constable intimated that he desired a remand for eight days, until the 28th, and the chairman said prisoners would be remanded for eight days. They were then removed from the dock. MRS. BURNDRED AND THE "GOOD NEWS."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Burndred's solicit—or visited Walton Gaol on Monday, and during his interview communicated to her the announcement that nothing of a poisonous nature had been found in the coffin of her baby Joseph. She was greatly pleased with the hood news,and relieved to find that in the matter of her own child, at all events, ' the police could find nothing to charge against her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MR. JUSTICE</head>
        <p>MR. JUSTICE</p>
        <p>FINNEMORE</p>
        <p>Judge of the Natal Supreme Court, Sends the Following Remarkable Testimonial</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>TO CUTIOURA</head>
        <p>TO CUTIOURA</p>
        <p>The World Is Cuticura's Field, Used Wherever Civilization Has Penetrated.</p>
        <p>"I desire to give my voluntary test!. mony to the beneficial effects of your</p>
        <p>Cuticura Remedies. I have suffered for some time from an excess of uric acid in the blood; and since the middle of last year, from a severe attack of Eczema, chiefly on the scalp, face, ears and neck and on one limb. I was for several months under professional. treatments, but the remedies prescribed were of no avail, and I was gradually becoming worse, my face was dread- fully disfigured and I lost nearly all my hair. At last, my wife prevailed upon me to try the Cuticura Remedies and I gave them a thorough trial with the most satisfactory results. The disease soon began to disappear and my hair commenced to grow again. A fresh growth of hair is covering my head, and my limb , (although not yet quite cured) is gradually improving. My wife thinks so highly of your remedies that she has been purchasing them in order to make presents to other persons suf- fering from similar complaints, and, as President of the Bible Women's Society, has told the Bible women to report if any case should come under their notice when a poor person is so afflicted, so that your remedies may be resorted to."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ROBERT ISAAC FINNEMORE</head>
        <p>ROBERT ISAAC FINNEMORE</p>
        <p>(Judge of the Natal Supreme Co), Pietermaritzburg, Natal, Oct. 29, 1901. Sold throushont the world. Cuticura Resolvent. 2e. at. (in form of Chocolate Coated Pills. Is. I id. per vial of 60), Ointment, 2s. at., Soap, Is. Depots: London, 27 Charter- house Sq.; Paris, 3 Rue de la Palk; Boston, 137 Colum- bus Ave. Potter Drug &amp; Chem. Corp.. Sole Props. Send for" All About the Skin and ScalD."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Increased Rates.</p>
        <p>• The fortnightly meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday under the presidency of Mr. A. R. Norman. There were also present :—Messrs. T. Baxter, T. Wright, S. Wo:)dward, C. E. Linaker, J. Spruce, G. Egerton-Warburton, C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, J. Whitlow, ff. M. Davies, J. H. Davies, H. Mawdsley, G. Piling, E. Bretherton, J. Fryer, Wm. Morris, T. F. Lea,J. Douglas, J. W. Temper- ley, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, R. Billington, .T. Afounfield, G. F'. Ashton (clerk), A. E. Hulse (acting deputy clerk), T. Cook, T. Riley, and W. R. de Prez (relieving officers.)</p>
        <p>TILE ESTIAIATES-INCREASED RATES. Mr. Woodward submitted the estimates of receipts and expenditure. It was recommended that a call of is. id. in the £ producing £11,830 be made upon the ov.erseers being an increase of 2d. in the £ over the corresponding period last year to meet a net estimated expenditure of £11,972. It was therefore proposed to call upon the overseers of the several parishes for the under-mentioned amounts payable by instalments on the Ist November 1901, and Ist February 1901:—Acton Grange £207 ; Alvanley, £B9 ; Antrobus, £142 ; Apple- ton, £338; Aston, £250; Aston Grange, £27; Birtington, £32 ; Budworth, £ll2 ; Clifton, £195 ; Crowley, £75 ; Daresbury, £62 • Dutton, £420; Frodsham, £650; Frodsham Lordship, £412; Grappenhall, £420 • Halton, £264; Hatton, £BB ; Helsby, £500; lieckwick, £180; Kingsley, £264; Kingswood, £B2; Latchford, Without; £140; 11Ianley, £B7; Moore, £245; Newton by Daresbury, £66; Newton by Frodsham, £5O; Norley, £183; Norton, £316; Preston-o'-th'-Hill, £193; Run- corn, £3,188; Seven Oaks, £5O • Stockliam, .£4l ; Stockton Heath, £580; Stretton, £110; Sutton, £350; Thelwall, £270; Walton Inferior, £270; Walton Superior, £122; Weston, £710; Whitley Inferior, £92; Whitley Superior, £lOO. Having presented the detail; Mr. Woodward moved the adoption of the estimates with the remark "It is not that I like it, but we have to take it."</p>
        <p>Mr. Roylance asked for what purpose the increased call was necessary. :qr. Baxter: Education, I should think. The Clerk: It is all on account of the Education Act, Mr. Chairman. Continuing he said that last half year it was expected that the Board would require for the county rate the same amount practically as for the previous half year. Instead of that the call went up considerably, bringing down the balance estimated to be in hand at the close of this half year to £O6l Before another call could be made the Board would be called upon to pay in salaries and for provisions, &amp;c., some- thing like £1,500 which would entail a deficiency before they could get the next rate collected. In making the present call they had to provide for a balance at the end of the half year sufficient to meet the demands upon them. The increased amount was due to the education rate going up. The Chairman: That is the story all over the country. WELL-KNOWN RUNCORN INH XIiITANT'S D E A.TH _ _</p>
        <p>The Clerk produced a notification of the death in the County Asylum of James Herringshaw. The deceased, who was seventy-two years of age, had attained a more than local reputation as " the Ship Canal poet."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>TIME OF MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE TIME OF MEETING.</p>
        <p>It was decided that in future the House Committee should meet at 11.30, and Mr. Woodward gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that the Board should meet during the winter months at one o'clock. FINNNCIAL. Mr, Woodward reported that the cheques drawn that day included payments to the late school attendance officers of the union of the amounts deducted from their salaries on behalf of the superannuation fund as follows: —Mr. George Jones, £4 14s. 9d.; Mr. Samuel Tomlin- son' £2 9s. Bd.; Mr. Brimelow, £2 Os:7d.; Arr. Wilkinson, £3 13s. od. There was a sum of £2 16s. due to the Clerk in connection with the same fund. The balance in hand at the last meeting was £336 ss. 6d.; the receipts during the fortnight totalled £lO7O ss. 4d.; and the payments that day £l4l 14s. 9d.; leaving a balance of £592 ss. id. He found that the Board had been credited with £32 which ought to have been credited to the District Council, and therefore the balance was reduced to £560 ss. Id. The only defaulting township—and he was sorry the representative (Mr. Broome) was not present as he would rather have enjoyed the duty of reporting the fact in his presence—was Keckwick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NEW BOILER.</head>
        <p>A NEW BOILER.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the Visiting Committee. Messrs. Timmins and Son, of Runcorn, were instructed to prepare plans &amp;c. for a new boiler in place of the one which has been condemned. SUPPLIES TO THE HOUSE. The Boardnccepted the followiag tenders for supplies to the house :—Groceries, Messrs. J. and G. Kydd, Frodsham ; meat, Mr. H. Singleton, Warrington ; fish, Mr. Horace W. Hill. Grimsby; drapery, Messrs. Proud and Pollard, Frodsham ; clothing, Messrs. Bradley, Runcorn; boots and shoes, Mr. J. A. Shallcross. Run- corn; coal, Messrs. R. Evans and Co.; coke, The Frod- sham Gas Company; drysaltery, Mr. H. Gibson, Run- corn; coffins, Mr. T. Barrow; carting, Mr. W. Grounds, Aston; cabs, &amp;c., Mr. Thomas Booth, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WEAR AND TEAR OF CITY LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE WEAR AND TEAR OF CITY LIFE.</p>
        <p>Why do so many of our City men look so sickly? Because they drink too much tea, and when they are fagged out cannot take more substantial and nutritive food. The system thus gradually becomes weakened and debilitated; there is no relish for food, no energy for work, and the whole of the physical and mental condition becomes seriously deranged. One City clerk—Mr. H. W. Elliot, 23, Norfolk Road, Canonbury, N.—writes :—" I work late hours, but I find Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa a delicious and stimulating drink My doctor, grocer, and chemist speak very highly of it."</p>
        <p>And if you use Vi-Cocoa regularly you will speak very highly of it too. Sold in 6d. packets and 9d. and is. 6d. tins everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>-- Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. dip Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not later than Thursday.</p>
        <p>A Pound Plea : An Appeal to Women. Sir,—As members of the Ladies' Committee in connec- tion with the Special Appeal Fund for the Samaritan Free Hospital/or Women, we desire to make a very earnest appeal to fifteen thousand women of the English- speaking races, both at home and beyond the seas to subscribe one pound each, to provide the £15,000 still -required to build the new Out-patient Department, Nurses' Quarters, new Operating Theatre, Amesthetising Rooms, etc. It is not necessary to emphasize this appeal, not seek to make it impressive with painful details. All the cases dealt with are difficult and intricate, and over seven thousand poor women are treated in the out-department alone every year. What this means can hardly be realized by those who are enjoying God's choicest gifts of health and strength. All this work has been carried on in a few dingy rooms in a small house adjoining the Hospital; and presents a well-nigh impossible task to the eminent medical men who give their services. The Hospital is " free " in the fullest sense of the word; no letters of recommendation are necessary, poverty and illness being the only qualifica- tions required; no one deserving is refused, and a special enquiry officer is constantly employed to guard against abuse of the charity. Of the whole amount of £20,000 required, £5,000 has been generously given by friends of the Hospital, headed by a donation of £2OO from King Edward's Hospital Fund,and we feel sure that there mast be thousands of women outside the Hospital circle who will gladly give one pound towards this splendid work. As evidence of the wide-spread area of the Institu- tion's usefnlness,it may be stated that nearly two-thirds of the In-patients come from different parts of England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales, London and the suburbs accounting for the remaining third. Remittances should be sent to E.DOUGLAS WircrE,Secretary Special Appeal Fund, Samaritan Hospital, 301arylebone-road, London. Confidently trusting in a warm response to this appeal,</p>
        <p>We are, etc., MARY ANNA ABERCORN. NINA HAMILTON AND BRANDON. VIOLET GRANBY. BEATRICE CADOGAN. BLANCHE COVENTRY. SOPHIE HARDWICKE. MARY JEUNE. FLORENCE ALEXANDER. CHARLOTTE F. BOULTON. MADGE KENDAL. NELLIE MELBA. CLOTILDE ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Damien's Challenge Sir,—l was (with nrany others) disgusted to read in last week's " Examiner " a challenge from the Rev. H. Dunnico to Councillor Stott to meet him in a public debate. Surely Mr. Dunnico has little of the British love of fairplay to challenge a man whom he well knows has not had his chance of a college education and practice in debate; and I only hope that Mr. Stott will refuse such a one-sided bargain. By the way, how is it that Mr. Dunnico has not tried to refute the state- ments of such gentlemen as the Rev. Mr. Carleton and others on the education question ; they have given him plenty of opportunities, but he has never dared cross swords with them. They are gentlemen who have worked all their lives for the education of the people of Warrington. What has ever Mr. Dunnico done? Does he know that four-fifths of his congregation have been educated in church schools, from the charitable gifts of church people? Has he, or any of his co-religionists ever subscribed a penny to help these schools in their charitable work? What condition would the people of Warrington have been in from an educational point, if it had not been for church schools? Last Sunday night the Vicar of St. Paul's made an appeal to the congre- gation for £5O. The managers have already this year paid £6BO, and the £5O is required to clear off the debt for improvements to Heathside schools. Will Mr. Dunnico ask his congregation to give a collection to this good work from which they have benefited in the past. It is only fair they should help I would advise Mr. Dunnico to give up his one and two- penny martyrdom ideas, and put out his energy to help to teach the children in our town. He may be able to cram a little Christian knowledge in his flock for half-an-hour in the Sunday school, but what about the hundreds, who, but for the day school would never hear the name of God except it was in an oath. The Church and Roman Catholic schools, which thank God are the most numerous, gather all the poor around them, and work for the Master by teaching the young to look up to Him and love Him. It is strange that, in all their speeches and letters,the passive resisters never draw attention to the conscience clause ;no child need get religious instruction, if the parents notify the schoolmaster or mistress that they object. Has the religious instruction received in church schools by any of Mr. Dunnico's congregation done them any harm? There are thousands in Warrington to-day,who have reason to bless and do bless the church schools.—Yours's</p>
        <p>FAIRPLAY.</p>
        <p>FRED STOTT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l was surprised, when opening my " Examiner " last Saturday, to find that the Rev. H. Dunnico had forwarded to you a letter referring to a discussion which was taking place in one of your contemporaries. Some weeks ago, in reply to a correspondent, a letter appeared in the local press from Mr. DunnicO which contained statements in reference to the National. Labour Representation Committee and its representa- tives, which were inaccurate, and if allowed to pass without being challenged or corrected. were likely to damage the Labour Representation movement. I took steps to write to the Secretary of the Labour Representation Committee and the President of the Amalgamated Society of Railway servants, forwarding a copy of Mr. Dunnico's letter, with a request that they would inform me whether the statements were correct or otherwise. The information, as I expected, was in direct contradiction to the statements made by the rev. gentleman. I therefore took the trouble to reply to his letter (which owing to pressure of work meant the sacrifice of a night's sleep) and in that letter embodied the information received from Mr. J. R. Macdonald and Mr. Loraine, intimating also that it Was not my inten- tion to interfere any further in the correspondence between Mr. Dunnico and Mr. Knowles. The letter was written entirely on my own initiative and responsi- bility, and I may further add that I was not "put for- ward as a competent authority" as the rev, gentleman stated in his letter to you. One would have expected, seeing that your correspon- dent is a minister of the gospel, to have had a reason- able reply, if any, to my corrections. Instead, however, Mr. Dunnico in reply, forwarded to the press the most contemptible letter I have ever been privileged to read, couched in language which is a disgrace to a man in his position, and which an ordinary working man would scorn to make use of. In his letter lam charged with making an "unworthy and sinister attack," a "cowardly and despicable attempt to score at his expense " etc., etc. I repudiate the suggestionthat I made an "attack" on,hint. My only" attack "was on the erroneous state- ments in his letter. As far however as lam concerned that matter is now at an end. Regarding the challenge to a public debate, Ido not feel under the slightest obligation to debate in Parr Hall because I have considered it to be my duty to correct some of the rev. gentleman'sstatements through the medium of the Press. Mr. Dunnico knew when he put forward the challenge that I should not accept it, 'having given him reasons for refusing some time ago. If he is so confident of his ability to prove the asser- tions made. surely he can prove them without my assistance in Parr Hall. I would,therefore,suggest that he engage thehall for that purpose. As for myself I absolutely decline to accept the challenge to a debate with this paid agitator." If I was fortunate enough to be provided with a situation for a few years,which required my services for a few hours per week only,and an annual holiday of two months' duration, instead of as at present having scarcely an hour per week for myself, I might then be self-conceited enough to accept a challenge to a public debate. At present, however, at the risk of 'losing the respect of fair-minded men "as your correspondent says I shall, I decline to accept a challenge to a debate in the Parr Hall with either Mr. Dunnico or any other gentleman. Your, etc.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>September 23rd, 1904. STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>September 23rd, 1904. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MORE SHOPS TO BE ERECTED.—In addition to the handsome. buildings which are being erected for Mr. Warburton by Mr. C. W. Davenport at the corner of London Road and fronting Victoria Square, there has this week been commenced the building of two more shops on the site formerly occupied by Beaconsfield Terrace which was pulled down in order to widen the road near the Mulberry Inn. One of these shops is being built for Mr. Norman of" The Bon Marche "Old London Road, draper and outfitter, the contractor being we understand Mr. F. W.Wright of Latchford. THE BAPTIST CHURCH.—The autumnal anniversary services in connection with the Baptist Church, Hill Cliffe, were held on Sunday, and the day being beauti- fully fine there were large congregations. In the morn- ing there was a young people's service, when Mr. J. Shenton presided. Children's hymns were sung and the Rev. R. D. Darby, of Leigh, the preacher for the day, who is also a returned missionary, gave an address into which was woven many of his missionary experiences on the Congo, and so he enlisted the rapt attention of the audience. Mr. Darby also preached in the afternoon and evening. The choir rendered the anthem "0 love the Lord" in a highly creditable manner under the direction of Mr. J. M. Muir, A.R.C.O. Mr. James Strange presided at the organ,morning and evening; and Mr. Muir in the afternoon. The collections for the dg,y were in aid of the church funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>ERECTION OF NEW SCHOOLS SANCTIONED. The Future of the Grammar School. Only twelve minutes were occupied in the transaction of the Warrington Education Committee's business at the monthly meeting on Monday evening. Mr. F. Monks presided, and there were also present :—The Revs. M. Stevenson and Father Wilson' Aldermen Bennett (vice-chairman), and Pierpoint, Councillors Davies, Dodds, Ellison, Lord, Roberts, and Rogers, Colonel J. D. Buckton, Messrs. W. Bibby,'R. T. Faircoigh, E. Plinston, J. Fulton Smith, C. H. Unsworth, and J. Upson, with Mr. J. Moore Murray (director of ducation), and Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer.) THE TRAINING OF PUPIL TEACHERS. FUTURE OF' THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL. It having been reported to the School Management Committee that the resolution of the committee with reference to the schools selected as being suitable for the training of pupil teachers therein, was not approved by the Education Committee at their last meeting, the committee again took the matter into their considera- tion, and it was resolved that application be made to I H.M. Inspector for permission to include the Silver-1 street Wesleyan school among those selected by him in which pupil teachers may be trained. A letter from the Board of Education with reference to the Warrington Grammar School and to the scheme for the supply of education other than elementary, suggesting fa) an amendment of the scheme for the election of the governors of the school (b) a contribution from the committee to the funds of the school, was submitted, and after some discussion, it was resolved that arrange- ments be made for a conference between the governors of the Grammar school and the School Management Committee on the subject. The committee recommen- ded that for the future, the charge to be made for the instruction of pupil teachers from the administrative areas of Lancashire and Cheshire at the committee's classes be £3 per annum in the case of each pupil teacher, and that the grant payable by the Board of Education in respect of the same be retained by the com- mittee. The Director reported that the County Authori- ties were desirous, if possible, of sending first year pupil teachers who are between the ages of 15 and 16 years to the pupil teachers' classes for half-time instruction, hut, in view of the pressure upon the accommodation of the Secondary school, the committee could not see their way to entertain this suggestion. A. number of increases of salary and new appointments in connection with the Secondary school and Technical Institute which were detailed in last week's " Examiner," were recommended. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON moved the adoption of the minutes. With regard to the training of pupil teachers the Education Committee felt that a Noncon- formist school should be added to the number of those in which pupil teachers could be trained. The sub- committee was only too glad to take that course, and the oversight had been remedied. A most important' letter had been received from the Board of Education' with reference to the Warrington "Grammar School and the action of the committee towards it. The School Management Committee felt the wisest and best plan was to ask the governors of the school to meet them face to face and have a conference to see what was best to be done. He was sure they all wished to see anything done that could be done for the aiding of the Grammar School to maintain the honourable position in the educational system of the town which it had always held. (Hear, hear.) The application of the county authorities to send their pupil teachers to the Warrington centre had been refused simply because the Secondary school was so full that the committee did not see their way to take in pupils from other places. Be should like to state with refer- ence to the Secondary school that there seemed to be an impression in the minds of some people that it was entirely filled with those who were going to be pupil teachers; and that no other child could be admitted unless he or she intended to be a pupil teacher. The members of the committee would know at once that that was an erroneous idea, but it was well also that it should go forth to the town that it was erroneous. It was perfectly true that certain children in the Secondary school were preparing to be pupil teachers, but that did not exclude other children from having the benefits of the school. There was a form to be signed by those who entered the school which might have given rise to the misapprehension. There seemed to be an idea in the minds of some people that the form obliged the child to become a pupil teacher. That was not so as it merely related to regular attendance. The Government grant partly depended on the regular attendance of the children, and it was obvious that if a child attended irregularly the committee had.to lose the grant, and the rates suffered in consequence. The form the child signed was merely a promise to attend regularly. Councillor ELLisoN seconded the confirmation of the minutes, and said he was very glad Mr. Stevenson had alluded to the Secondary school because a misappre- hension existed. He was glad the matter ,had been clear3d up so satisfactorily. Councillor DODDS asked how long the teachers whose salaries it was proposed to increase had been in the employ of the committee? Alderman Pfuteorterr : The salaries have been in- creased according to the scale. Councillor DODDS: It may be worth while if the director would state on the agenda the period a teacher whose salary is to be increased has been employed, and what advances have been made previously. The CHAIRMAN: They have had no advance at all. Councillor DODDS: Wehave been going such a short time. Still if teachers have been here only six months I should like a good reason to be given for advancing the salaries so quickly. The Rev. M. STEVENSON, in reply, said that the three ladies in question had been here a year. One of them had only received £7O hitherto which was it small salary for the position she held. The other two ladies had been teaching a year in the Secondary school and had done satisfactory work, and, although there was no scale of salaries in the Secondary school because the number of teachers was so small,the increment was only equal to what they would have if they had been in an elementary school. Councillor DODDS said he was quite- satisfied. He simply called attention to the matter because if the information was not given at the meeting of the Education Committee it would be asked for at the meeting of the Town Council. The minutes were agreed to. THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOLS. The Sites and Buildings Committee,at a meeting held dn the 9th inst., had before them the resolution of the Education Committee. passed at the last meeting, with reference to the provision of temporary school accom- modation. The resolution was "noted.' The Board of Education sent a letter dated September 7th, formally approving of the site for the proposed new school in Latchford. It was resolved, "That the Law Clerk be directed to give the necessary notice, under Section Bof the Education Act. 1902, of the committee's intention to erect a public elementary school in the Latchford dis- trict, and also one to supply part of Bewsey, Orford, and Fairfield wards." The committee considered the steps to be taken with a view to the appointment of an architect for the erection of the proposed new schools, and it was resolved to appoint a sub-committee, consist- ting of Councillors ;Evans and Tinnion, Mr. R. T. Fair- dough and the Chairman (the Mayor), to make the pre- liminaryarrangements, and to visit some recently built i schools n other towns, in order to make themselves acquainted ,with the latest developments of school architecture. It was also resolved to call the new school in Latchford "The Bolton School, Latchford." In the absence of the Mayor, Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH moved the confirmation of the minutes. He said he would like to draw attention to the resolution stating "that in the case of those schools which are used by the managers for purposes other than those of public elementary schools, one-seventh of the cost of the annual painting and decorating of the schools shall be borne by the managers,"and pointed out that that would be additional expenditure to the managers. Previously it had been paid out of the grant, but now the town got the grant and therefore the managers had to pay. Councillor DODDS: Do you move the minutes? Mr. FAIRCLOUGH : Yes. I move their adoption. (Laughter.) Continuing,he said the question of school accommodation had been a rather burning subject with one or two of the members, but he thought they would now be satisfied that the committee were doing all they could. With reference to the appointment of a com- m)ttee of four—no more than four being allowed according to the Act—to inspect some recently erected chools, he thought it would be money well spent. ellear, hear.) The members would also see that it had been resolved to call the new school for Latchford the "Bolton " School. The school was required to have a distinctive name and as Alderman Bolton was Mayor of the town and Chairman of the Sites and Buildings Com- mittee they thought it only right some recognition of the fact ought to be made. ([rear, hear.) Councillor ROBERTS seconded. Alderman PIERPOINT said there was just one matter he would like to mention, and that was with reference to the appointment of an architect. He hoped they would throw the matter open for competition among the local architects. (Hear, hear.) He threw that out as a suggestion to the committee. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH : It is already being done. (Hear, hear.) Alderman PIERPOINT : Then that is all right. Mr. PLINSTON said that as one of those to whom school accommodation had been a ,burning question he would like to congratulate the committee upon showing something at last. They had had a long time to wait but they were now making a bold step and he was pleased that they were going on with the erection of the schools. The minutes were then approved. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee, it was resolved that Mr. John William Kay, of 112, Man- chester-road, be recommended to the Town Council for appointment as representative manager of the St. Peter's School, in place of Mr. E. J. Guest, resigned. The committee approved the recommendation of a special sub-committee that the transfer of the St. George's Mission should be accepted; the committee to pay the trustees rent at the rate of £52 per</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 24, 1904. THE PEARSON AND KNOWLES HARVEST FESTIVALS. COMPANY.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 24, 1904. THE PEARSON AND KNOWLES HARVEST FESTIVALS. COMPANY.</p>
        <p>annum, and to provide for the care, cleaning, and heating of the school, and for the ordinary repairs required to the premises, the committee to be also responsible fnr the payment of the rates ; the payments to be computed from the Ist. August, 1904—the arrange- ments for the lighting of the school to be made later The Director was instructed to make arrangements for the transfer of the St. George's Mission school to some other building in that district. Tho minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>LOCAL JOURNALIST'S MARRIAGE. Edgar —Howard. On Tuesday at Dewsbury Parish Church the marriage, took place of IVlr.George S.Edgar,a journalist of London eldest son of Mr. Peter Edgar, of Winwick-road, War- rington,and Miss Jeannie Howard, youngest daughter of Mrs. Howard, of Wellington road, Dewsbury, The Rev. F. S. Hughes, curate, officiated. The bride who was given away by her brother Mr. Charlie Howard looked charming in an ivory cloth gown, with guipure lace,andicream beaver hat.Slie carried a bouquet of cream roses,while the bridesmaids, Miss Maggie Edgar (sister of the bridegroom) and Miss Bessie Heaton (niece of the bride) were very pretty in cream wool crepoline, with high pompadour silk belts, cream felt hats, with autumn leaves, and they carried bouquets of the ' same with pink roses. They wore engraved gold rings, the gifts of the bridegroom. A large number of people witnessed the ceremony after which the newly-married couple departed for Windermere for the honeymoon, the bride's travelling dress being a purple habit with hat to match. The following is a list of the presents : Mr. H. Howard, oak cabinet Mr. C. Howard, case of cutlery Mr. P. Howard, fish eaters Mr. and Mrs. T. Heaton, silver pepperettes and mustard pot. Miss B. Heaton, antique rose bowl Miss Kathleen Heaton, salad service Mrs. J. Walker, pair of blankets Mrs. T. Tattersfield, Carlton fruit dish Mr. and Mrs. Higham, toilet set Mr. G. Dean, silver coffee service Mr. Potter (of London), four water colours Staff of Mr. F. Potter, revolving book case Mrs. Dean, teapot and plant pots Mrs. Law, Japanese teapot Mrs. Ward, jam dish Mrs. Tonning jewel case Miss M. Sykes, crumb tray and brush Mrs. H. R. Hepworth, tray Mrs. Rayner, antique oak chairs Young ladies in Mrs. Howard's workroom, silver tea- pot and cream jug Mrs. and Miss Horsfield, purse Mrs. W. Wilton, Worcester ware vase Mrs. 0. Talbot, silver cream jug Mr. H. Fitton, Crown Derby vase Miss Day, silver hairpin box Mrs. W. H. Shaw, table centre Miss Lena Swallow, flower stand Miss Walker, picture Miss Poole, pair of oil paintings Miss J. Leek, lace table centre .Mrs. G. Wright, hearthrug Miss Newbury, ornaments Mrs. Hall, silver basket Miss Spedding, afternoon tea cloth Miss F. Horsfield, eiderdown quilt Mr. and Mrs. Lockwood, bedspread and pillow cases Mrs. G. Fitton, bedspread Mr. and Mrs. H. Grounds, silver afternoon tea knives Mrs. and Miss Armitage, silver-backed brush &amp; comb Mrs. H. Thackerah, silver photo frame Mr. Louise de Gernon, case of hair brushes Mr. and Mrs. Heaton and family, silver flower pots Miss F. Styleton, silver photo frame Mr. A. Spedding, pair of vases Mrs. Spedding, silver butter dish Mr. and Mrs. Hodgson, treacle jar Miss Amy Ogden, pewter card tray The Misses Edgar, silver jam spoon Mr. and Mrs. Moore, silver flower pot Mr. and Mrs. P. Mitchell, silver hat brush Miss Johnson, silver photo frame Miss Newbury, silver pickle fork, jam spoon and butter knife Mr. Rayner, bread fork Mr. J. Burnley, pewter card tray Mrs. Day and family, fish carvers Miss Jagger, copper pan and tumblers Mr. and Mrs. Boothroyd, jam dish Mrs. Atkinson, pair of blankets Mr. Norman, embroidered belt Mrs. and Miss Edison, china Mr. and Mrs. Pennington, table cloths and serviettes Mr. and Mrs. Gandy, silver claret jug Miss Lythgoe, reading lamp Mr. P. L. Edgar, silver photo frame Mr. J. Grounds, crumb stoop Miss Margery Heaton, table cloth.</p>
