IRITUALIST SOCIETY. On Sunday and Monday this society. celebrated its harvest festival in the Druids' Hall. The roomhad been tastefully decorated with flowers and evergreens, and there was a good display of frnit, vegetables, corn, &amp;c., and two large loaves of bread. The speaker on Sunday was Mrs. Powell-Williams, and at the evening service the hall was crowded. Mrs. J. Hannan gave a very tasteful rendering of two solos, "The last Milestone " and "Sun of my soul, accompanied by Master Pearse, a juvenile musician of considerable ability. Mr. James Booth was the organist. On Monday a public tea was held, followed by a soical. Mrs. Hannan again sang with great acceptance, and songs were also capitally rendered by Miss Furness, Mr. R. Wood and Mr. Stout, Master Pearse being a very efficient accompanist. Miss Furness also gave an amusing recitation, and Mr. Jackson accompanied by, Mr. C. Knox, contributed a capital 'cello solo and a cello and piano duet. The phonograph was also brought into requisition for a few records. The proceedings closed with a sale of the fruit, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S.</p>
        <p>The first of a series of harvest thanksgiving services in St. Peter's church was celebrated on Thursday even- ing. There was a large congregation. The decoration of the church had been effectively'accomplished by a band of willing workers, and there was a consensus of opinion that the work had never been better done. The communion table and the pulpit had been artistically. treated, and the moss which had been called into r eqm sition formed a good background for the-. red dahlias, asters and other flowers. The decorators were as follow :—Communion table and chancel, Mrs. Percival, Mrs. James, Mrs. J. Taylor, Mrs. Greaves and Mrs. Carter; pulpit and screen, Mrs. Bamforth, Mrs. Lingley, Mrs. Cowden, Mrs. Webster, Mrs. Stanley and Mr. Clayton; font, Miss Boardman and Miss gains; windows, Mrs. Webster, Mrs. Clayton, Misses Barlow, Percival, Hughes and Stanley, and Captain Langton.— The preacher for the occasion was the Rev. T. A. Schon- -1 berg, M.A., vicar of Stowell Memorial Church, Salford. His name was familiar to many parishioners, from the fact that his father was the first vicar of St. Peter's on its formation into a separate ecclesiastical parish, and was in charge of the mission church which preceded the present edifice in Birchall-street. The service was also taken part in by the Rev. B. Percival (vicar) and the Rev. D. R. James (curate). Special harvest-thanks- giving hymns and psalms were sung and the choir very efficiently sang the anthem "While the earth remaineth " (Simper). Mr. Edgar presided at the organ with his accustomed ability.—A collection was taken in aid of special church expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>(ALL RIGHTS RESERVED'.</head>
        <p>(ALL RIGHTS RESERVED'.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of "Search Lights on. Lantl, Labour and Social Problems."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>SOCIAL PROBLEMS AT BARROW-IN-</head>
        <p>SOCIAL PROBLEMS AT BARROW-IN-</p>
        <p>FURNESS.</p>
        <p>"Barrow is the youngest child of England's enter- prize. How extraordinary it is that in an old country' like this, there is still so much youth, and there is still an energy which develops, and expands itself with such rapidity. The basis on which the prosperity of the town' is to be built, is not only a broad solid basis, but one that is likely to be permanently expan- sive."—W. E. GLADSTONE. These prophetic:swords were uttered by Mr. Gladstone when he opened the first dock at Barrow, in 1867. At that time the infant town was turning its marshes and swamps, and sandhills into building plots, and laying the foundations of a flourishing seaport, and a busy</p>
        <p>hive of industry. • Seventy years ago, Barrow had only sixteen houses, and eighty inhabitants. To-day it has sixty-five thou- sand inhabitants, and its rateable value is about £300,000. The story of the origin and growth of this English Chicago is one of the romances of industry. I.—BARROW OWES muca TO ITS IRON ORE.</p>
        <p>I had a conversation some time afro with a mining expert, who knows the district well, and he tells me that vast sums of money have been won and lost in those iron mines. The ore in the neighbourhood of Barrow is not found in veins, and c mrses, that can easily be traced and followed. It is found in isolated deposits that are called "sops," or "pockets," and when these are worked out, new beds of ore must be sought. When our fathers carried on their .mining by rule of</p>
        <p>thumb,and guess-work, I have no doubt the made many mistakes, and some losses. But my friend assures me that if trained experts who know something of geology, and practical mining, go into this business wisely, they can easily keep up a large and regular supply of ore with ordinary care and prudence. In 1S 11, these mines were only raising. about 3.000 tons of ore in a year, and there were no local furnaces to' smelt it. The miners carted it down to a place in Walnev channel that was afterwards known as Piel Pier and shipped it when the tide served, to South Wales. It was a slow, and costly, and laborious method of win- ning and exporting their iron ore, but it was the best they could do under the circumstances. IL—BARROW'S PRESSING NEED WAS A RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>But how was she to get it? She could not make her own rails, or sleepers, or railway engines, or ore waggons, or passenger carriages, and it was hard to tell how she was to get them from any other part of the kingdom. The nearest railway station over land was Carnforth, which is about thirty miles away as the crow flies and twice as far by any ordinary road. In those days the stage coaches and conveyances "crossed the sands," and a risky and dangerous journey it was I can assure you. Mounted guides who knew the way rode in front of the pedestrians' and vehicles, and piloted them over. They invariably followed the re- treating tide as it went out,and crossed the most dangerous part at dead low water, and then hurried on to the other side before the rising tide overtook them and surrounded them. I But suppose a wheel came off, or a conveyance broke down, or a 4 gibbing horse stood still and refused to proceed when you were half way across. Suppose a blinding rain, or a dense fog came on, and you lost your way, and you began moving round in a circle instead of pushineforward at the critical moment. Well it is no use supposing these things, for they have happened many a time to passengers crossing the sands between Morecambe and Kents Bank, and Cartmel and Ulverston. Conveyances have been swept away, and men and horses have been drowned, and the perils of those trips across the sands kept Barrow and the neigh- bouring villages in perpetual isolation on the land side. And it was little better if you tried to get there by sea. The nearest seaport was the new village of Fleet- wood, which had just got a new railway of its own, and was struggling into existence as a seaport. Walney Channel was not navigable at all beyond Piel Pier, and that could only be used while the tide was high. So the makers of Barrow had to order their engines, and railway wagons, and ore trucks in Birmingham, and send them on by train to Fleetwood. Then their troubles began in earnest, for they had no cranes strong enough to lift them into a ship. They had to resort to all sorts of ingenious expedients to get them on board, and how they managed it at all is a mystery to me, and when they had ferried them over the sea to Piel Pier it is a bigger mystery still how they got them safely ashore. But they managed it somehow, and in 1845 they made the first portion of the railway from Piel Pier to Barrow and Dalton-in-Furness,ln 1854 they carried it on to Ulverston, and in 1857 they finished it to Carnforth. What this railway did for the infant town was clearly stated by the late Duke of Devonshire at the opening of the Ramsden Dock. He said: "The source and root of the rapid progress of Barrow has undoubtedly been the rich deposit of iron ore which Providence has largely bestowed on this part of thecountry. But the develop- ment of the trade dates from the introduction of the railway into this district. In the prospectus of the Furness Railway it was stated that the promoters expected to carry one hundred thousand tons of iron ore every year. Within five years they were carrying 250,000 tons and in less than ten years it was 450,000</p>
        <p>tons." lIL—BARROW HAS BEEN FORTUNATE IN ITS</p>
        <p>LEADING MEN.</p>
        <p>I know no town in England that has been so blessed with wise and prudent, and far-seeing leaders. These men were industrial prophets. They saw visions. They dreamed dreams. They realised the possibilities of the place when it was a lonely deserted wilderness, given up to the sea-gulls and the wild birds, and the rabbits. They fenced off sixty acres of fore-shore, and pumped the water out of the enclosed space, and dug out the Ramsden Dock. Then they robbed the sea of one hundred and forty-six acres after the same fashion, and formed the Cavendish Dock. Then they filched sixty- two acres of submerged land from the sea, and built the Buccleugh and the Devonshire Docks. The value'ofthese docks now is enormous, but the land on which they are situated cost nothing beyond the ex- pense of shutting the sea out. This is land grabbing if you like, but it is land grabbing of which I heartily approve. They dredged the Walney Channel as far as the ship- building yard, and made it deep enough to float the largest ships of war, and the finest ocean steamers. Now I have shown you how these wise master-builders acquired thousands of acres of valuable land, by simply evicting the sea and keeping it out. I have shown you how they dug two great highways for themselves to the rest of the world by making a railway and by deepening their channel, and providing a dock for shipping. After these preliminary works had been completed all thb rest was easy. Iron and steel works, and ship- building, and marine engineering yards, and flax and jute works, and arsenals for the manufacture of guns, and armaments came to the town in rapid succession. In 1859 Messrs. Shneider and Hannay built the first iron furnace in Barrow, and that was an epoch making event for the town. It was a bold attempt to use the iron ore on the spot instead of exporting it to other towns to be smelted, and then bringing it back in the manufactured state. There was no difficulty in obtain- ing limestone and iron ore for this furnace in the town so there was little to pay for carriage on these items, but the coke had to be fetched from Durham. The success of this experiment was marvellous. Seven years later the output of iron ore was 691,421 tons, and 239,523 tons of it were smelted at Barrow. Barrow received its charter of incorporation in 1867 and it opened its first dock at the same time. Mr. Gladstone went there to celebrate the double event, and after driving round the town and examining the plans and schemes of improvement that were then in hand, he saidi" With regard to Barrow, I must say that for a combination of natural advantages afloat, and of natural advantages ashore, it probably would not be practicable to find a match for it in any part of the country." If he could come back to Barrow now, and see it as T saw it a few weeks ago, he would say that the makers lof Barrow had wrought some mighty social and indus- trial miracles, when they turned a waste howling wilderness into a prosperous and wealthy town. It is a , bit of good work well and honestly done.</p>
        <p>Motorist in Trouble at Lymm. At Lymm Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, Andrew Butterworth, jun., of 443, Chester road, Old Trafford, , Manchester, was summoned for furiously, driving a Local Military News. ! motor car at Lymm on August 20th. Defendant Captain H. K. Woods, of the South Lancashire pleaded not guilty. Sergeant Washington stated that Regiment, has made a record score on the new gol about three p.m. on the dak in question he was on duty links of the United Service Club at Haslar Portsmouth near the church brow, Lymm, when he saw the He went round the 18 holes in 74. This equals the defendant driving a motor car at a furious rate. He record for the old course. Lieutenant H. T. Cotton, estimated the speed at about twenty-five miles an hour who has served in India with the 2nd Battalion South Defendant said he did not think he was going at such Lancashire Regiment, and second Lieutenant E. F. D. a rate. When asked by the officer if he knew what Nicholson who is how serving there with the Ist speed he was travelling at he said "It is not what I Battalion, have been selected for promotion to higher say: it is what you say. If you say so I shall be rank. The new captain joined the regiment in May, done." Defendant said he was only going about nine 1898, and got his step in January,l9oo. Mr. Nicholson miles an hour.—A fine of two guineas and 17s. nd has served since January, 1902, and joined the costs was imposed, and his motor licence was Fighting Fortieth" in South Africa. ndorsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ST. BARNA.BAS' CFIIIRCH.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNA.BAS' CFIIIRCH.</p>
        <p>There was a large congregation at this church on Thursday evening when the harvest thanksgiving ser- vices commenced:* The decorators may be said to have excelled themselves, and they are to be complimented upon the very tasteful and artistic way in which their work was done. A profusion of choice fruit, flowers, corn and vegetables had been arranged to the best advantage, and the general effect was extremely • pretty. While special attention had been devoted to the altar, font and pulpit, the lavish treat- ment of every part of the church reflected credit upon the workers. The 'following is a list of the decorators :—Altar, IVlrs. Hollingworth ; font,gesdames Iddon, Connell, and Jones; pulpit, Mrs. and Miss Bracegirdle ; west window, Mesdames Bellian, Billington, Carmen, and Millington; altar rails, the Misses Whitehouse; choir stalls, the Misses Fry ; windows and pillars, Nievl t.n).4 Furnival, Tomlcins, Dowling, Evans, and Woodcock, Misses Helsby, Jefferson, Houghton, Powner, and A. E. Smith. The service, which was of an appropriately festal character, was conducted by the V icar(Rev.J. H. G.Hollingwwth). The members of the choir acquitted themselves very creditably, the anthem "Let the people praise Thee' (Caleb Simper) being sung. The solos were well rendered by Mr. W. H. Woodcock (bass) and Mr. T. Evans, jun. (tenor). Mr. T. Evans presided at the organ. The preacher was the Rey. J. R. Wardle,. of King'sHeathßirmingharn,who based his discourse on the words "While the earth rernaineth, seed time and harvest shall not cease" Genesis via., 22. He spoke of the harvest as a manifestation of God's love and bounty which were in such striking contrast to man's coldness and indifference. While urging his hearers to thankfulness for the harvest, he impressed upon them the necessity of sowing seed In a spiritual sense here, if they wished to reap the harvest in the world to come. They could all do their part in the work of the Church and the training of the rung, and if they kept on sowing patiently in faith their harvest would indeed be real.--The offertory was on behalf of the bhoir fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Accident to a Workman.</head>
        <p>Accident to a Workman.</p>
        <p>A serious -accident befel James Miller, 32 yeai-s of age, who lives at 92, Orford-lane, on Monday after- noon. It appears that he was engag,ed on the extension of the Co-operative Stores building in Winwick-street, when a girder fell upon him. He wtts taken to the Infirmary where it was found that he was suffering from a fractured skull.</p>
        <p>ELASTIC STOCKINGS, KNEE CAPS, &amp;C.—For Varicos Veins and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Leg, Sprains, Scc. They are porous, light in texture, and inexpensive and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking.—Prices 4s. 6d., 7s. 6d. and 10s. 6d. each. Postage free.—WHITR &amp; WRIGHT, SUr.gleal Instrument ad Truss Makers, 98, Renshaw-street, Liverpool.</p>
