      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Cost of an Election.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brooks presided at the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians, held on Wednesday morning. There were also present : Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Dr. Murray, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. S. Davies, A. J. Jolley, G. Thompson, J. Payton, R. Bolton, T. Ritchie' T. H. Ashcroft, J. Billinge, R. Forshaw, William Houghton, J. Backhouse, M. W. eton, W. Flannagan, J. Turton, with J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (workhouse master), and A. Wright (superintendent Padgate schools.) .....</p>
        <p>THE COST OF THE RECENT ELECTION AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASHCROFT called attention to an account for the recent election in Viaduct Ward, Earlestown. The CHAIRMAN said Mr. Ashcroft was quite out of order in bringing the matter forward. The CLERK pointed out that the Union did not pay any part of the bill. It was chargeable to the Newton parish. The CHAIRMAN said ho would allow Mr. Ashcroft to proceed if it were the wish of the Board. The Board consenting Mr. ASHCROFT said he thought it was extravagant to spend £l6 17s. 2d. on an election. It was too extrava- gant altogether to pay officials two guineas a day when they only worked from 12 o'clock noon to 8 o'clock at night. The CLERK : It is according to scale. Mr. AsHcaorr : It is very extravagant. Mr. THOMPSON : I suppose it is according to the scale of charges fixed for elections ? The CLERK : It is. Elections are very exceptional things. We have not had one in the whole of the three years preceding this except the triennial election of guardians, and no doubt the fees for presiding officers and clerks are pretty liberal. It is a fallacy to suppose that they are only sitting from 12 o'clock to 8. They are working the whole of the day The subject then dropped. THE COUNTY RATE. LARGE INCREASE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK submitted the caunty rate precept served upon him in accordance with which he prepared and caused to be served upon the respective overseers, pre- cepts for payment on September 10th of the County demands—The Particulars are as follows :—General Purposes (in the £) 1-7/Bd., general elementary educa- tion purposes 5d., special elementary education purpose -id., general higher education purposes at 5-Bd., general police purposes 13/96d., WarrinFton police division pur- poses 1-29/48d., Wigan police division purposes 2-5/48d., West Derby hundred bridges 7/48d., West Derhy hundred main roads 11/12d.—The totals are as follow : Burtonwood £794 ss. 7d.—Cuerdley_ 2248 17s. 7d.— _ _</p>
        <p>daydock £1,084 3s. 7d.—Houghton £77 ss, 2d.—Newton £2,829 2s. Gd.—Penketh .£350 15s. 10d.—Poulton £292 13s.—Rixton £320 16s. sd.—Sankey £342 18s. 2d.— Southworth £l5B 7s. 7d.—Winwick £646. 4s. 2d.—</p>
        <p>Wo-olston £169 1-9 s. 10d. (The two Lancashire precepts for the whole year of 1854, inclusive of Warrington, totalled ,C 1,816 The recent precepts, exclusive of Warrington, for half-years only have been—February 1902, £2,62s—September. 1902, £2,955—February, 1903, £3,025—September 1903 .£5,255- February,l9o4,£s,24l— 1904,. £7,31595. sd. The CLERK pointed out that the poor rate svasllB-1/6d This was exclusive of the county rate. His object in fixing the poor rate at 8-1/6d., was to make the balance against the respective parishes as even as possible. The statement was accepted. DR. PATTERSON APPOIN'TED ON THE ASSESSMENT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>- Mr. MATHER remarked that a few weeks ak,ro, the Board lost a very valuable and old colleague in Mr. Clarke. Mr. Clarke was a member of the Assessment</p>
        <p>_ Committee, and it was necessary that a gentleman representing the Newton part of the Union should be on the committee. He therefore proposed that Dr. Patterou be electei a member of the Assessment Com- mittee. Mr. WILLIAM tiotiGHTON seconded and the proposi- tion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>MR. SETON APPOINTED ON COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>_ The CLERK read the return of the recent election in Viaduct Ward, Earlestovvn. Mr. Seton, the successful candidate, he said was now in attendance and had signed the usual declaration. On the motion of Mr. A. J. JOLLEY, Mr. Seton was appointed a member of the House Committee, the Padgate Schools Committee and the Newton Relief Committee.</p>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved the following resolution pursu- ant to notice given at the last Board meeting :—" That inasmuch as Dr. Richard Sephton has for some years past satisfactorily filled the office of medical officer for the No. 3 District of the Union and his present resi- dence and surgery, although situated out of the district, is accessable and convenient to the Union patients, and there being no duly qualified man resident within the district, he be re-appointed medical officer of that dis- trict for a further period of 3 years terminating on the 29th day of September, 1907." Mr. MATHER seconded and the resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>THE HOUSE COMMITTEE. The House Committee recommended the acceptance o, the tender of Mr. Critchley to supply 500 yards of tweed at 2s. per yard, and an increase of night porter's wages from 265. to 28s. per week. The committee also recom- mended the Board to have the chimneys of the old hospital overhauled, strengthened and pointed. In reply to an enquiry on the House Committee minutes, 1T the Insurances of property against fire, the Clerk said he had applied for a copy of each policy with full details for deposit at the Workhouse,and a copy for thehomes. He was in correspondencevith the Vulcan Boiler Insur- ance Company re insurance of the boilers.— Meeting on September 16th, it was resolved that William Henry Fairclough be appointed hospitarporter at a salary of £25 per annum, subject to superannuation reductions together with uniform, rations and apartments. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved the confirmation of the minutes, and they were agreed to without comment. THE VALUATION BILL. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY reported that on September 14th he attended a meeting of the committee appointed in June last to organise action both in and out of Parlia- ment in defence of Union Assessment Committees and to secure that impending changes in the law of valua- tion and assessment should be real reforms. There was a long discussson as to the policy likely to be most effective, the outcome being that the secretary and he (Mr. Jolley) converted the Chairman and it was unanimously agreed that in view of the courteous reception accorded to the committee by Mr. Long in June last, they should act as friendly critics of the Government and submit proposals to him for the amendment of the Government measure. The general lines of such proposgls having been agreed upon, it was decided to appoint a small sub-com nittee to formulate them and with a view to keeping down expenses as far as possible it was resolved that the sub-committee's draft should be sent by post to each member of the committee for his comments, and after further con- sideration, amended if thought necessary. It being ob- viously desirabl.3that the representations to be made to the president of the Local Government Board should have the support of all boards of guardians in England and Wales and it having been, in fact, agreed that a further conference, if possible even larger than the one in June, should be held, it was resolved to hold such a conference in the same week as the annual meeting of the association of poor law unions in November, and that, to economise time and travelling expenses, the committee to settle the final form in which the pro- posals should be submitted to the conference should meet the day before. The CHAIRMAN thought the thanks of the Board were due to Mr. Jolley for the great interest he had taken in the matter. Mr. Jolley was chairman of the Assessment Committee, and therefore took an interest in the subject of valuation, but he thought they ought to acknowledge their indebtedness to Mr. Jolley for the interest he had taken in the matter. (Hear,hear.) THE SANKEY REGISTRAR AND HIS DUTIES. LETTER TO THE REGISTRAR GENERAL.</p>
        <p>The following letter was read from the General Registration Offico, Somerset House, Lon Jon, W.C., 15t1 September, and addressed to Mr. T. H. Sutton : Sir, I am directed by the Registrar General to state that he has received a request from the Registrar of births and deaths from Sankey sub-district to be . . .</p>
        <p>allowed to discontinue attendance .at his Penketh</p>
        <p>station, and to reduce the number of his attendances at Burtonwood on the ground that so little registration is actually accomplished on his visits to these stations. The Registrar at present attends Burtonwood twice a week and Penketh once a week,. and he states that he has made as many as seven successive attendances without any result, while it is a common occurrence to visit the stations three or four times in succession without any registration being done. I am, therefore, to request that you will lay these facts before the Guardians for their observations on the question of the abandonment of Penketh station (which appears to be within a mile and a half of the head office) and the substitution at Burtonwood of a weekly, attendance for the attendances twice a week. As regards Burtonwood, the twice a week attendances •"ere fixed two years ago at the instance of the Guardians, the Parish Council having desired that the parish should be a separate sub-district and the Registrar General therefore entertains doubt whether any change should be made at that station, which serves a population of over two thousand. Mr. FORSFIAW thought it would be better to let the arrangements in the Sankey sub-district remain as they were for the present. It was perhaps rather creditable to Burtonwood that they did not keep the registrar busy, but still they did all they could to keep him going. (Laughter.) For the present he was satisfied that the existing arrangements should continue. Dr. MURRAY said he would not like to suggest an alteration himself. It was decided that the Clerk should write the Burton wood and Penketh Parish Councils on the matter.</p>
        <p>THE BIRTH CERTIFICATES OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN. On the subject of births in the Workhouse, a letter from the General Register Office was read as follows: am directed by the Registrar General to acknow- ledge the receipt of your letter of the Bth inst., and in reply to state for the informatir of the Guardians that the question as to the description of the place of birth in column 1 of entries relating to children born in the Workhouse is at present under the consideration of the Local Government Board, and that he will in due course</p>
        <p>communicate to the Guardians the decision of the Board on the subject.</p>
        <p>NEWTON RELIEF COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>" ILA THE NEW OFFICES TO BE RENOVATED. At a meeting of the above committee, held under the chairmanship of Mr. Billinge, it was resolved that 11E5 relief offices and house be painted and decorated ins!de and out; that the floor of the committee room (which had given way at one corner) 'be examined and the defects repaired; that the foregoing resolutions be sub- mitted to the Board at their next meeting for confirma- tion. The CLERK said Mr. Owen reported that the defect in the floor was due to dry rot, and seven square yards Of the floor would have to be entirely removed. The rot was caused by a slate having been plastered over the mouth of the ventilator during building operations, and not removed afterwards. Mr. BILLINGE said the offices had been in use for three years. He proposed that the work be proceeded with. Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON, in seconding, facetiously observed that the Board room would not require ani decoration as in his opinion, the members of the Newton Committee were in themselves a sufficient ornamenta- tion for that room. (Laughter.) • I The proposition was carried. CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>Val The following contracts were accepted for the forth- coming half year, except in the case of milk, which is for a year: Bread, Mr. Stringer; flour and oatmeal, Mr. Barlow; meat, Mr. H. Singleton; hoots and shoes, Mr. F. Taylor; leather, Mr. E. Pickavance ; clogs, Mr; Jones; coffins, Mr. Mason; milk for the house, Farmers Stores; for the schools, Mr. .1. W. Houghton. The following were referred to the House Committee:. brushes, drapery, grocery, coal and drugs. The Board then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LOCAL REVISION COURTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL REVISION COURTS.</p>
        <p>(Coatinued from, Page (3)</p>
        <p>Daresbury. THE QUESTION OF FREE LISTS. Mr. C. E. Lloyd attended at the Sessions House Daresbury, on Monday morning to revise the lists for the following parishes :—Acton Grange, DaresburYt Hatton, Kec kw ic k, Moore, Newton-by-Daresburh Preston-o'-th'-Hill, Walton Inferior, Walton Superior Antrobus, Seven Oaks, Whitley Inferior and WhitleY Superior. The assistant overseers for the various parishes were present, and also the political agents, Messrs. Dean and Smith, of Knutsford. The businees was of a routine character. His Honour remarked with reference to the supplying of free lists to the political agents that he could not make an order that that should be done, as he had 110 power, it being a matter between the auditor and the Local Government Board. At the same time he mliq admit that the registration was greatly convenience and assisted by the agents, and without making ant order he wished to express the opinion that it would be a very proper course for the overseers to adopt to supply the copies to them free. On meeting the audi- tors, if the matter was mentioned, they could say thell had supplied the lists gratis, the barrister considering it was for the convenience of the registration. It WO extremely improbable I.:„Li. clay surcharge would bfl made after that.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the business, Mr. Smith said that on behalf of the Knutsford DiV sion Liberal Association he had pleasure in proposing 9 hearty vote of thanks to his Honour for his courtea9 and forbearance during their sittings of the last fort- night in various parts of the division. He dared Sal they had tried his patience somewhat in trying to oe their best for their respective parties, but he had lu„.°! them very well and deserved their warmest thanks. o' also wished to express thanks for the expression of opinion with reference to the free lists, and also add that the lists had been well prepared by the oversee of the various parishes—nearly 100 altogether. Mr. Dean said that on behalf of the ConservatiVe Registration Association he cordially endorsed gr' Smith's remarks, especially with reference to the as he thought, the assistance rendered throughout bythe political agents ought in some way to be acknowledged by the overseers. His Honour returned thanks, and said he had pleasure in again testifying his appreciation of the great assist- ance rendered by the agents. He agreed with gr, Smith about the work of the overseers, the lists ban&amp; extremely well prepared. Mr. Whitlow, one of the assistant overseers present, returned thanks on their behalf for the compliment Paid to their work. They wished however, to state that they did not feel satisfied with the suggestion of allovi- ing the free lists. The political agents were officer° like the overseers were. The sum paid for lists WI some slight remuneration to them, and they did net think it fair that the agents should have them payment, seeing that they had no definite order to that. His Honour: I merely expressed my opinion. I hay! no power to make an order.. If the auditor surchargu'r it—though I don't think he would—the agents wouhlt.e• course, repay it them. Mr. Whitlow: There can be no surcharge. That not the question. Mr. Dean: Of course we will pay postage. don't want them to lose anything. His Honour: Well. I have only given my opinionfit'ir Mr. Whitlow: We don't think the course is to us. The revision for the Grappenhall Polling District!. comprising Grappenhall, Latchford Without and that part of Warrington within the geographical county ot Chester, was conducted by Mr. Clement E. Lloyd in al! Large Room, Grappenhall, on Saturday morning. Theo were present: Mr. W. H. Woodcock, assistant overseel for Warrington; Mr. S. J. Woodcock, assistant over,: seer for Grappenhall, and Mr. Joseph Smith. assistant' overseer for Latchford Without, with the politi/ agents, Mr. J. H. Dean (Conservatives), .and gr. Smith (Liberals). The business was of a foroP- character, no claims of public interest being made. Newton Division. MR. GOUGH STRUCK OFF THE SANKEYLI Mr. James T. Foard, revising barrister, attended P., the Conservative Club, Stocks-lane, Penketh,on Tuegiel morning for the purpose of revising the lists of votlei; for Bold, Cuerdley, Great Sankey and Penketh, form part of the Newton Pairliamentary Division. n xr„ Conservatives were represented by Mr. Holland O, and Mr. Mawdsley, and the Labour party bY nr. Valentine. The assistant overseers present were Mr. Houghton; Penketh, Mr. E. Foster- CuerdleY, James Wright ; and Great Sankey, Mr. Joseph SPithe Mr. Samuel Gough, formerly a member el her Warrington Town Council, and who is a house-furnls„4 carrying on business in Winwick-street, Warringtoeerip his name struck off the list for Great Sankey toWe",.. on the ground that he had been convicted at the filvi 0, pool Assizes of a corrupt and illegal practice au municipal election at Warrington. . to Mr. Foard said he believed the present revisroll have been the shortest yet held.—Mr. Wright; have everything in order. THE WARRINGTON COUNTY VOTERS. At the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday Inhe noon, Mr. Foard proceeded with the revision of „„... voters lists for the townships of Poulton-with-Fet. head, Rixton-with-Glazebrook, Woolston-with-gart!cal croft and Warrington County. The same politr agents as appeared at Penketh were present, as wellop, Mr. J. Howes. of the Warrington Liberal ASSOC' who assisted Mr. Valentine, the agent for Mr. Sc the Labour Candidate. The following assisn, overseers were also present :—.Nlessrs. IV* •tb- Woodcock (Warrington), G. Stubbs (Rixton-WI Glazebrook), F. Wright (Poulton-with-Fearnhead), Daintith (Woolston-with-Aartinscroft). hforcl Mr. R. T. Fairclough„J. P., of Barryfield, Late ertl claimed an ownership vote in respect of proPthst possessed by him in Thynne-street. It transpired the land was leasehold and the claim was disallowr,eliffer Mr. Henry Roberts, J.P. of Kingsley, Hill claimed a vote in respect of freehold land own,e,ivieo him in Monks-street, Warrington--The Rev. J. the supported the claim and said Mr. Roberts had beepeols owner of the land since March last year. The vote allowed.</p>
      </div>
