        <p>A GOOD FAMILY MEDICINE CHEST, WITH a prudent use, has saved many a life, and yet we think the idea might improved upon and reduced to a more simple form. Take some Compound such as COCKLE'S ANTIBILIOUS PILLS. and we find that the desired end may be obtained without scales and weights, or little mysterious compartments, and enchanted bottles with crystal stoppers. Others might be used but COCKLE'S PILLS as tested by many thousands of persons, and found to answer their purposes so well, may be set down as the best.—Observer. eeeKLE,s FREE FROM MERCURY. ANTIBILIOUS OF PURE VEGETABLE INGREDIENTS. PILLS. TY, INDIGESTION, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS. THE Sr. HELENS MUSEUM. Mr. Lancaster, curator of the St. Helens Museum, on Wednesday reported to the Parks Committee a gift of 690 silver, bronze and copper coins plmolthic stone implements, neolithic implements, L., as well as a large number of agate beads, Scc., from Mr. H. W. Seton- Karr, brother of the borough member. The committee passed a resolution of thanks for the valuable gift. NEW PIANO FOR ST. HELENS CORPORATION. The St. Helens Town Hall Committee on Wednesday discussed the purchase of a new grand piano for the assembly-room at the Town Hall. The Surveyor reported that Alderman Beecham and the members of the Selection Committee suggested that when the piano was bought the Corporation might engage some cele- brated player to give a recital, and that it should be treated in the same way as the band performances which the committee arranged for the parks, or, at most, that a small charge should be made. The committee decided to defer the matter for further consideration. A POSTPONED WEDDING. A wedding party at St. Mark's Church. St. Helens,had rather an unusual experience on Thursday. The bridge- groom hailed from Southport, and the bride from Windle. Upon their arrival at the church it was 'dis- covered that by an oversight the certificate of the pub- lication of banns had not been procured from South- port. The marriage, therefore, could not take place,and the wedding party were in a dilemma. Eventually they were driven to Suttod and a special licence applied for in order that the ceremony could take place. 410- CLARKE'S B 41 PILLS are warranted to cure, in sex, all acquired or constitutiunal discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the tack. Free from Mercury. Established upwards of 30 years. In boxes 4., Eid each, of all chemists aud Patent Medisine Vendors through- out the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers. The Lincoln and Midland Counties Drug Company. Lincoln.— Advt. PLORILINE I—FOR THE TEETH AND BREA