        <p>There was no movement, not even the religious mov e- TORY ACTIVITY. ment, which was productive of so much quarrelling and dissension as the labour movement. No sooner did a, labour leader attain a prominent position than he was sub- ii AOTHER OPEN-AIR MEETING AT iec ted to jealousy and villification.Therefore it was utter- -y impossible to have a labour leader.lt was best for the BRIDGE FOOT. workingmen to examine the programmes of the Liberals and Conservatives, the only parties who could possibly form a Government. and see for themselves which party A Programme of Reform. they were likely to get the most from. Au open-air meeting under the auspices of the Con- Votes of thanks were accorded to the speakers. servative Labour Party was held at Bridge Foot on Thursday evening. Mr. W. L. Williams presided and was supported by Councillor W. J. Wilson, Mr. Frank Fearneley, Mr. R. J. Macartney and others. There was a moderate attendance. The CHAIRMAN said he believed the visits of the deputation had been productive of good. They had set, forth in great volume the great benefits which had been derived from successive Conservative Govern- ments. It was very interesting to read the great politi- cal effusion of Lord Rosebery at Lincoln. Two or three years ago Lord Rosebery, in a memorable speech at Liverpool, said the Liberal party from that date would start with a clean slate. That slate to all intents and purposes still remained clean. Nothing of any con- sequence, nothing which would benefit the working classes, had been written upon it. On the other hand they had a statesman of the first rank, one who gave up office and who came forth into the wilderness, as he himself termed it, to prepare the way for what must inevitably come—the great question of fiscal reform. He had with great volubility set forth that his scheme would not cost the working classes one penny more than they were at present paying. Mr. Howes, in a great diatribe, pointed out that Mr. Chamberlain was associating with aristocrats, and that, in other words, workingmen were blessing the foot that kicked them. They might, if they had felt inclined, have asked the gentleman who was seeking the suffrages of this borough whether he did not associate himself with dukifs, not of this country but of Russia. It would have been bring- ing the question more directly to the front, and showing the working classes who were their real friends and who were seeking to benefit them. , Mr. FEARNELEY proceeded to review the legislation of previous Conservative Governments. It was a Con- servative Government which abolished the Combina- tion laws and made freedom of speech aud liberty at public meetings possible. The Factory Acts—with one trifling exception,—the Artisans Dwellings and Housing of the Poor Acts had all been passed by Conservative Governments. The Free Edu- cation system was introduced by a Conservative Govern- ment and passed. The extension of the valuable pro- visions of the Friendly Societies Acts had been the I work of Conservative Governments. They must not omit the Workmen's Compensation Act of the present Government. They were told that the Government had been looking after their friends, meaning he supposed the brewers, the parsons, and the landlords. At the same time they had been looking after workingmen for they passed the Workmen's Compensation Act which the greatest leaders of trade unions confessed to be a second Magna Chirta for the people of this country—that was to say, it gave them a principle which was a basis of security for all dangerous employments such as they had not dreamt of before. Dealing with the work of the !future Mr. Fearneley said they could if bhey liked•accomplish many things within the next five years which their fathers had longed for, many things which would make the road clear for those who followed them, and at the same time would be fur their own immediate benefit. During the p.st two years those of them who were trade unionists had been brought face to face with a very serious difficulty, namely, a judgment given with reference to the liability of trade union funds for the ' indiscretions of anyone connected with those unions, no matter how junior those officers might be. One of the most important, if not the most important thing they had to devote their attention to in the immediate pre- sent was not merely the reversal of that judgment,but the obtaining of legislation that should obviate that judgment and make perfectly secure the funds of their trade unions on a just and equitable basis. The Con- servative Labour Party had put that matter in the forefront and the organisation had .been formed largely with the initial idea of having trade union funds put upon a fair, just and equitable basis of security. They purposed to so organize themselves and agitate upon the question as to be ready when the Government took their places again in Parliament to go with a strone force behind them to claim from the Government that they should, as one of the first things in connection with their next session's work, either pass a. Bill to ensure the safety of trade union funds, or make such arrangements as would permit the pass- age of Mr. Shackleton's Bill which was put aside during the last Session. (A Voice: Why have'nt they done it?) Continuing Mr. Fearneley pointed out that the present Registration Laws were exceedingly clumsy, and there were all sorts of little provisions which prevented workingmen from getting on the register. What they wanted to see intro- duced all over the country was the same principle that obtained in a town where there were more constituencies than one, and voters would be able to move from one division to another and their votes would follow them. Then again it ought to be just as easy for a young fellow to obtain his vote after he had been living in lodgings for twelve months as it was for a house- holder, and he too ought to have the privilege of suc- cession from town to town. Another grievance he would like to see removed by a Registration Amendment or Reform Act was the present disqualification from voting of persons in receipt of out-relief. There was another great matter that called for immediate atten- tion. Of course their Liberal friends, and probably their Socialist friends, would laugh when he mentioned it because it had already been brought on the tapis two or three times. Because it had not been done iminedi- ately their friends laughed it out of emit, as if there was no necessity to talk about it. The more shy people were about the matter the more necessity there was for workingmen to combine and be insistent on it. He was referring to the position of the old worker who was thrown out of work either through the heedlessness of those employers who wanted a younger man or perhaps through infirmity or ill health. They must have no more putting aside for some other season of the old age pension matter. They must all work for it, and they had better put away talking about their party shibboleths and talk about that and other matters he had mentioned. They wanted a reasonable pension— not a finniking sum that left a man still a pauper. He held that every honest workingman who had put in a full life in the labour of the country had an undoubted right and claim upon the nation that in his old age it should pension him as one of its spent workers. Mr. Fearneley proceeded to advocate a change in the poor law system, which, he said, had got into its present con- dition through the indifference of workingmen. The existing law was all that they required if they insisted upon its being properlyadministered. They must realize that it was within their power to do away altogether with the Workhouse system, except for the undeserving poor, and compel Boards of Guardians to give out-door relief. There were „many deserving persons to whom out-door relief wbuld be an inestimable benefit, but who would not go near the relieving officer as they would have to bear the stigma of the word "pauper." He hated that word and failed to see why it should not be swept from our dictionaries altogether. (Hear, hear.) If a man, who had paid his poor rate and done his duty to his conntry, fell for the moment, it was the duty of his country to take care of him until he again found his feet. They could compel the Guardians to adopt the outdoor relief system, and they ought to compel them to do it. Dealing with the question of the unemployed, Mr. Fearneley said that in spite of the statement made by a fossilised old official of the Poor Law—Mr. Jenner lust—who told the Manchester Guar- dians that they had not the power to find employment for people out of work, the Guardians were empowered by law to provide work as had been proved in Preston where that course was adopted. In conclusion Mr. Fearneley asked his hearers to compare the work done by the Liberal and Conservative parties during the past thirty years. If they did so they would see that they had greater reason to be satisfied with the Conservative party as that was the party from which they had in the past derived the greatest benefit and from which in the future they would be likely to derive it. Mr. MACA.RTNEY also addressed the meeting and drew the attention of his hearers to the excessive expenditure of municipal bodies which during the past few years had increased from one hundred to three hundred millions. It was a serious matter for working men as increased rates meant higher rents. They should do their utmost to prevent municipal bodies from embark- ing upon extravagant and unremunerative,schemes. Messrs. RICHARD ORRELL and ROBERT ORRELL de- livered addresses but were subjected to a great deal of interruption the name of Mr. Lloyd, George being cheered. A number of questions were answered by Mr. Fearneley. He was asked if he was aware that in Win- chester, where there was a licensed house to every 200 inhabitants, an arrangement between the magistrates and licensed victualers to close 50 houses had been made before the passing of the Licensing Act, and that the brewers had now withdrawn their offer. Mr. FEARNELEY said he was not aware of the arrange- meat but he would accept the statement as true. The granting of compensation would remove any sense of injustice which might have been felt at the wholesale closing of houses. That would not tend to make the Act any less a temperance measure, as any Act likely even to the smallest extent to reduce drinkinglfacilities must be regarded as a temperance measure if they accepted the doctrine that the closing of houses reduced intemperance. For his own part, he did not accept that view. "Are you aware that Mr. Pierpoint, M.P., voted against Mr. Shackleton's Bill ?" was asked. Mr. FEARNELEY said he was not aware of the fact. He would, however, remind the meeting that the third clause of the Bill was very vague, and even trade unionists vvere not unanimously in favour of it. No doubt that influenced Mr. Pierpoint. Next session when the Bill was re-introduced they would see it was a very different measure from the last one. Mr. T. HORROCK.S asked whether it would not be better for workingmen to form a party of their own, and not have to beg of the Liberal or Conservative party for reforms. Mr. FEARNBLEY said that undoubtedly it would be better if it were practicable, but he was sorry to say that it was not. Unfortunately there was no leader among the workingmen who commanded the confidence ,of the whole of the working-classes of the country</p>
